= Electromagnetism
{wiki}

Electromagnetism describes how <electric charge> and <electric current> interact through the <electric field> $\mathbf E$ and <magnetic field> $\mathbf B$. The <Maxwell equations> describe the fields and their sources; the <Lorentz force> describes the response of a particle of <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$:
$$
\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E_{\mathrm{particle}}}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
The <electric field> can change the particle's <energy>. The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> is perpendicular to <velocity>, so <magnetic forces do no work>; they can bend a trajectory. These are local interactions: a particle responds to the <electromagnetic field> at its position. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_01.html[Feynman lectures, II.1: fields and forces]{ref}.

The remarkable unification is that the same <Maxwell equations> connect <Coulomb's law>, <Faraday's law of induction>, and freely propagating <electromagnetic waves>. A changing <electric field> contributes to magnetic circulation through <displacement current>; together with <Faraday's law>, this permits waves in vacuum without conducting matter. The <Poynting theorem> then explains how the fields store and transport <energy>. This connects the force on a charge to the energy carried by light.

In his 1865 paper, <James Clerk Maxwell> derived transverse disturbances and compared the propagation speed inferred from electrical measurements with independently measured light speeds. Part VI, especially §§95–97, argues that their agreement supports identifying light as an <electromagnetic wave>. This was a comparison with finite experimental accuracy, rather than a numerical identity between the historical measurements. https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rstl.1865.0008[Maxwell, A Dynamical Theory of the Electromagnetic Field, Philosophical Transactions 155 (1865), 459–512]{ref}; https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Dynamical_Theory_of_the_Electromagnetic_Field/Part_VI[transcription of Maxwell's original Part VI]{ref}.

= Thermoelectric effect
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A <thermoelectric effect> couples <heat transfer> and the transport of <electric charge>: a <temperature gradient> can generate an <electromotive force>, while an <electric current> can carry <heat> and cause heating or cooling at a junction of different materials. This coupling makes electrical measurement of <temperature> possible through a <thermocouple>, and permits conversion of a sustained flow of <heat> into electrical <work>. A finite open-circuit voltage does not itself deliver <power>: electrical <power> requires an <electric current> through a load. Thermoelectric conversion therefore involves both <electromagnetism> and <thermodynamics>. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/history.html[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: thermoelectric effects and applications]{ref}.

= Seebeck effect
{c}
{parent=Thermoelectric effect}

The <Seebeck effect> is the generation of a thermoelectric <electromotive force> by a <temperature> difference. In a <thermocouple>, two different homogeneous <electrical conductors> A and B connect a measuring junction at $T_h$ to two reference connections at a common $T_c$. With negligible <electric current>, the measured terminal voltage is determined by the difference of their <Seebeck coefficients>. A hot junction alone does not specify the reading: the reference <temperature>, the second material, and the terminal order matter.

\Image[seebeck-thermocouple-experiment.png]
{title=Two-material <thermocouple>: junction <temperatures> and the voltage sign}
{description=An idealized open-circuit <Seebeck effect> experiment. The A and B wires meet at the measuring junction; each reference connection meets an identical meter lead at the same $T_c$. The positive meter input connects to A. The shown $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, $70\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and $+2\,\mathrm{mV}$ belong to the explicitly toy constant-<Seebeck-coefficient> example below, not measured data.}

For a motivating experiment, join one end of A to one end of B, insulating the wires elsewhere. Connect their free ends to identical meter leads at reference <temperature> $T_c$, for example in an ice-water <heat bath>. A meter with input <electrical resistance> much larger than the <thermocouple>'s <electrical resistance> approximates an open circuit, meaning $I\approx0$. Keep the measuring junction in a separately controlled <heat bath> at $T_h$ and wait for stable junction <temperatures>. Both reference connections must share $T_c$; identical leads with identical endpoint <temperatures> have cancelling thermoelectric contributions, allowing the meter to be elsewhere. https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/director/planning/report97-1.pdf[Marx, Link and Scott, Economic Assessment of the NIST Thermocouple Calibration Program (1997), §2.1]{ref}.

Define the reading as $V_{AB}=V_A-V_B$, with the positive input on the A reference terminal and the negative input on B. Use the <Seebeck coefficient> convention in which the voltage change along a homogeneous wire at zero <electric current> is $dV=-S(T)\,dT$. Following the path from A at $T_c$ to the common junction at $T_h$, then back along B to $T_c$, gives
$$
V_{AB}(T_h,T_c)=\int_{T_c}^{T_h}\bigl[S_A(T)-S_B(T)\bigr]\,dT.
$$
This formula assumes homogeneous materials whose <Seebeck coefficients> depend only on the local <temperature>, and no additional uncompensated lead contributions. Here voltage is the electrical transport voltage associated with <electrochemical potential>, rather than a direct measurement of an isolated equilibrium contact <electric potential>.

Start with $T_h=T_c$, then increase $T_h$ while holding $T_c$ fixed and record $V_{AB}$ at each stable setting. In the ideal model the equal-<temperature> reading is zero. If $S_A-S_B>0$ throughout the interval, A reads positive when $T_h>T_c$. Exchanging meter inputs reverses the reading; exchanging the two junction <temperatures> reverses the oriented <integral>. Repeating the run with identical homogeneous materials gives zero because $S_A=S_B$. These controls test the stated model; a nonzero experimental baseline should be measured rather than silently removed by assuming it is zero.

For an explicitly toy linear example, stipulate constant <Seebeck coefficients> $S_A=25\,\mu\mathrm{V/K}$ and $S_B=-15\,\mu\mathrm{V/K}$. Choose reference <temperature> $T_c=20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and measuring-junction <temperature> $T_h=70\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. A <temperature> difference has the same numerical size in <kelvins> and on the <degree Celsius> scale, so
$$
S_A-S_B=40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K},\qquad \Delta T=50\,\mathrm K,
\qquad V_{AB}=(40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K})(50\,\mathrm K)=+2.00\,\mathrm{mV}.
$$
Swapping the inputs gives $-2.00\,\mathrm{mV}$. Bringing both junction regions to the same <temperature> gives zero. With <temperature>-dependent <Seebeck coefficients>, shifting both junction <temperatures> equally while keeping their difference fixed can change the reading. No actual material pair is claimed to have the stipulated coefficients over this interval.

\Image[seebeck-toy-voltage-slope.png]
{title=Toy <Seebeck effect> calibration: voltage slope and reversed inputs}
{description=Calculated lines, not measured data. The solid line is $V_{AB}=(40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K})\Delta T$; the dashed line shows the same device with reversed meter inputs. The <slope> is the difference of the two <Seebeck coefficients>, not either coefficient separately. The reference <temperature> is fixed at $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$; negative $\Delta T$ means the measuring junction is colder than the reference connections.}

Real <thermocouples> need a calibrated voltage-versus-<temperature> relation: the constant-<Seebeck-coefficient> approximation is useful only where the coefficient difference changes little. Wire inhomogeneity can make the result depend on where the <temperature gradient> lies, and reference connections at unequal or unknown <temperatures> introduce errors. An electronic reference correction uses a measured reference <temperature> in place of an ice bath. https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/director/planning/report97-1.pdf[Marx, Link and Scott (1997), §2.1: reference correction and wire homogeneity]{ref}. Burns and colleagues provide the standardized reference functions for common letter-designated <thermocouple> types; the toy line above is not a replacement for them. https://www.nist.gov/publications/temperature-electromotive-force-reference-functions-and-tables-letter-designated[Burns et al., Temperature-electromotive force reference functions and tables (1993), NIST Monograph 175]{ref}.

The historical experiment used a different detector. In his 1821–1823 investigations, Seebeck found that a closed <electrical circuit> of two dissimilar materials with junctions at different <temperatures> deflected a compass needle. The modern explanation is that thermoelectric <electromotive force> drives an <electric current>, whose <magnetic field> deflects the needle. The open-circuit experiment above detects voltage while drawing negligible <electric current>. The striking connection is that a thermal imbalance can become an electrical signal and then a magnetic response. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/history.html[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: Seebeck's compass experiment]{ref}.

= Seebeck coefficient
{c}
{parent=Seebeck effect}
{title2=$S$}

= Thermopower
{synonym}

The <Seebeck coefficient> $S(T)$ quantifies the thermoelectric voltage response per small <temperature> difference at zero <electric current>. Use $S=-dV/dT$ along a homogeneous material, with voltage understood through <electrochemical potential>. Its units are <volts> per <kelvin>, commonly $\mu\mathrm{V/K}$. The word <thermopower> is also used, despite this being a voltage response rather than <power>; some authors use that word for $|S|$, so check their convention. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: Seebeck coefficient and thermopower convention]{ref}.

For the A-minus-B terminal order in the <Seebeck effect> experiment,
$$
\frac{\partial V_{AB}}{\partial T_h}=S_A(T_h)-S_B(T_h),\qquad
\frac{\partial V_{AB}}{\partial T_c}=-\bigl[S_A(T_c)-S_B(T_c)\bigr].
$$
Thus a small <temperature> difference around $T_0$ gives $V_{AB}\approx[S_A(T_0)-S_B(T_0)]\Delta T$. The finite-interval ratio $V_{AB}/\Delta T$ is an average of the coefficient difference, not necessarily its value at either endpoint. Ordinary two-material measurements determine a relative <Seebeck coefficient>: to recover $S_A$ from the measured difference, add a separately known $S_B$. Neither the choice of a zero for voltage nor the use of a familiar reference material makes $S_B$ identically zero.

A concrete research example is Martin and colleagues' 2021 certification experiment. They measured small <temperature> differences and voltages on six <silicon>–germanium specimens at 13 base <temperatures> between $295$ and $900\,\mathrm K$. At each setting they acquired 40 voltage-and-<temperature>-difference pairs and used <linear regression> with a free intercept, so a constant voltage offset did not become part of the <slope>. Platinum voltage leads required a reference-material correction; their terminal order gave $S_{\rm measured}=S_{\rm Pt}-S_{\rm specimen}$, hence $S_{\rm specimen}=S_{\rm Pt}-S_{\rm measured}$. This is an actual measurement protocol, unlike the toy plot above. https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=932592[Martin et al., Development of a high-temperature (295–900 K) Seebeck coefficient Standard Reference Material (2021), certification measurements and Eq. (1)]{ref}.

Microscopically, a <temperature gradient> changes <charge carrier> transport until an opposing electrical response balances it at zero net <electric current>. In the usual simple <semiconductor> picture, <electrons> and <electron holes> give opposite <Seebeck-coefficient> signs, and their contributions are weighted by their <electrical conductivities>. The <Seebeck coefficient> is a transport property: <density of states> alone does not determine it, since carrier motion and scattering also matter. Its usefulness extends beyond <temperature> sensing to diagnosing which carriers transport <electric charge>. https://thermoelectrics.matsci.northwestern.edu/thermoelectrics/[Northwestern thermoelectrics research group: Seebeck coefficient, scattering contributions, and bipolar transport]{ref}.

= Thermocouple
{parent=Seebeck effect}
{wiki}

A <thermocouple> uses two different <electrical conductors> and the <Seebeck effect> to infer a measuring-junction <temperature> from a voltage and a known reference <temperature>. The reference material and the reference <temperature> are distinct choices: B specifies the comparison <Seebeck coefficient>, while $T_c$ specifies the lower limit of the <integral>. The two-material experiment in <Seebeck effect> gives the geometry, sign convention, and calibration limitations.

The useful measurement is a pair: the raw terminal <electric potential difference> and the <thermocouple reference junction> <temperature>. For the A-positive terminal order and homogeneous-wire assumptions of <Seebeck effect>, write the open-circuit <electromotive force> as
$$
E(T_h,T_c)=\int_{T_c}^{T_h}[S_A(T)-S_B(T)]\,dT.
$$
Here $T_h$ is the <thermocouple measuring junction> <temperature> and $T_c$ is the common <temperature> of the reference connections. The subscript $h$ names the measuring end; it need not be hotter. A <voltmeter> senses the complete connected thermoelectric path through the wires and its leads, in the <electrochemical potential> convention already described under <Seebeck effect>. It does not measure an absolute <temperature>, the individual <Seebeck coefficient> of A, or an isolated contact <electric potential>.

\Image[thermocouple-reference-junction-wiring.png]
{title=<Thermocouple> wiring with two <copper>-lead junctions at a common reference <temperature>}
{description=Original schematic. The <isothermal terminal block> contains electrically separate A–Cu and B–Cu transitions and a local <temperature> sensor. Both homogeneous <copper> leads end at the same meter-input <temperature> $T_m$, so their thermoelectric contributions cancel. The displayed $+2.4\,\mathrm{mV}$ is calculated from a stipulated constant difference of <Seebeck coefficients>, not measured data or a calibration of actual materials.}

= Thermopile
{parent=Thermocouple}

A <thermopile> connects multiple <thermocouples> in series to turn a <temperature> difference into a larger electrical signal. Absorbed <thermal radiation> can heat its sensing junctions while reference junctions remain cooler; the signal depends on the thermal and optical design.

= Thermocouple measuring junction
{parent=Thermocouple}
{title2=$T_h$}

The <thermocouple measuring junction> joins A and B at the location whose <temperature> is to be inferred. Its own <temperature> is what the electrical measurement estimates. To infer a surrounding object's <temperature>, the junction must approach <thermal equilibrium> with that object: insufficient immersion, <heat conduction> along the wires, or a changing environment can keep the junction at another <temperature>. A stable electrical display alone does not establish that this thermal condition holds. The two-<heat-bath> procedure and terminal-reversal controls under <Seebeck effect> provide the starting experiment; the practical issue here is the reference and conversion chain.

= Thermocouple reference junction
{parent=Thermocouple}
{title2=$T_c$}

The <thermocouple reference junction> is the connection region where the two thermoelements meet the measurement leads. In ordinary <copper>-lead wiring there are two material transitions, A–Cu and B–Cu, rather than a second welded A–B junction. They must have a common known <temperature> $T_c$ for the simple expression $E(T_h,T_c)$ to apply. An ice-water <heat bath> can establish a nominal $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ reference; <cold-junction compensation> instead measures $T_c$ locally. The word “cold” describes the reference role and imposes no requirement that $T_c<T_h$.

= Isothermal terminal block
{parent=Thermocouple reference junction}

An <isothermal terminal block> keeps the two reference transitions and the reference <thermometer> at approximately the same <temperature>, while maintaining electrical insulation between the terminals. Thermal contact is required; an electrical short between A and B would bypass the signal. Merely using <copper> does not eliminate the <Seebeck effect> in the meter leads. If both homogeneous <copper> leads start at $T_c$ and end at a common meter-input <temperature> $T_m$, their contributions have equal magnitude and opposite sign around the measurement path:
$$
\int_{T_c}^{T_m}S_{\rm Cu}(T)\,dT-\int_{T_c}^{T_m}S_{\rm Cu}(T)\,dT=0.
$$
This cancellation uses matched endpoint <temperatures> and homogeneous lead material, rather than $S_{\rm Cu}=0$. A <temperature gradient> along each lead is allowed under these assumptions. If the two transitions are at different <temperatures>, one measured $T_c$ cannot generally correct both; unmatched connectors or wire inhomogeneity can introduce additional <electromotive forces>. The FP-TC-120 documentation explicitly locates the reference sensing in the terminal base or connector block and specifies isothermal versions of these assemblies.<FP-TC-120 and cFP-TC-120 Operating Instructions by National Instruments>[p. 7, Cold-Junction Compensation: terminal sensors and isothermal terminal assemblies]{ref}

= Cold-junction compensation
{parent=Thermocouple reference junction}

= CJC
{synonym}
{c}

<Cold-junction compensation> converts a reading with reference <temperature> $T_c$ into the equivalent reading for a chosen standard reference. Define the <thermocouple reference function> $F(T)=E(T,0)$, where $0$ means $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, not absolute zero. Additivity of the <definite integral> gives
$$
E(T_h,T_c)=F(T_h)-F(T_c),\qquad
F(T_h)=V_{\rm raw}+F(T_c),\qquad
T_h=F^{-1}\!\bigl(V_{\rm raw}+F(T_c)\bigr).
$$
The final <inverse function> requires a suitable one-to-one calibration interval. A terminal <thermometer> supplies $T_c$ independently; the raw <thermocouple> <electric potential difference> supplies only the difference of the two reference-function values. In general, first converting $V_{\rm raw}$ to a <temperature> and then adding $T_c$ is wrong, because the <thermocouple reference function> need not be linear. The SCC-TC manual explicitly adds the reference-equivalent voltage before its final voltage-to-<temperature> conversion.<SCC-TC Series Thermocouple Input Modules User Guide by National Instruments>[pp. 5–6, Scaling Voltage Measurements, steps 1–5]{ref}

For a stipulated model only, take $S_A-S_B=40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K}$ throughout the interval. No actual material pair is assigned this constant. Because <degree Celsius> differences have the same numerical size as <kelvin> differences,
$$
T_h=80\,{}^\circ\mathrm C,\quad T_c=20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
V_{\rm raw}=(40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K})(60\,\mathrm K)=2.4\,\mathrm{mV}.
$$
In this model $F(20)=0.8\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $F(80)=3.2\,\mathrm{mV}$. Adding the $0.8\,\mathrm{mV}$ correction produces $3.2\,\mathrm{mV}$, whose inverse calibration gives $80\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Applying the zero-reference calibration directly to $2.4\,\mathrm{mV}$ would instead display $60\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$.

Now physically change only the reference <temperature> to $30\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, leaving the measuring junction at $80\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. The raw reading becomes $2.0\,\mathrm{mV}$; the correction becomes $F(30)=1.2\,\mathrm{mV}$, so the corrected total stays $3.2\,\mathrm{mV}$. Physically changing the reference changes the measured <electric potential difference>. Adding a software correction changes its interpretation and leaves the raw reading intact. If the actual reference is $30\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ but the software still assumes $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, this model displays $70\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$.

\Image[thermocouple-reference-compensation-calibration.png]
{title=Adding the reference-equivalent voltage before inverting the <thermocouple reference function>}
{description=Calculated constant-<Seebeck-coefficient>-difference example only. The zero-reference line $F(T)=E(T,0)$ and the $20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ raw-reading line differ by $F(20)=0.8\,\mathrm{mV}$. At $T_h=80\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, adding that correction takes $2.4$ to $3.2\,\mathrm{mV}$. Changing the reference to $30\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ changes the raw reading and the correction in opposite directions. Real calibration curves need not be straight.}

An actual implementation in <FP-TC-120 and cFP-TC-120 Operating Instructions by National Instruments> (p. 7, Cold-Junction Compensation) uses terminal-mounted <temperature> sensors, reports their reading as “CJ Temperature”, and enables compensation in <temperature> mode but not voltage mode. This makes the distinction between a raw voltage readout and a compensated <temperature> readout observable. The specified reference-sensor range also produces an out-of-range indication: a calculated number outside a supported range is not a validated measurement.<FP-TC-120 and cFP-TC-120 Operating Instructions by National Instruments>[p. 7, Cold-Junction Compensation: mode selection, CJ Temperature and out-of-range reporting]{ref}

= Thermocouple voltage calibration
{parent=Thermocouple}

<Thermocouple voltage calibration> relates an actual assembled sensor's <electromotive force> to independently established junction <temperatures>. It must be distinguished from selecting a nominal letter-type conversion curve. In <Thermocouples Calibrations Services by National Institute of Standards and Technology> (opening method description and bath-calibration table), NIST describes comparison with a maintained reference <thermocouple>, comparison with a standard platinum <resistance thermometer> in controlled <heat baths>, and fixed-point calibration at the freezing points of zinc, aluminum, silver and gold on the <International Temperature Scale of 1990>. These are independent thermal references, unlike a test that checks only a voltage-input circuit.<Thermocouples Calibrations Services by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[method description; table for base-metal thermocouples compared with an SPRT in a temperature-controlled bath]{ref}

For a concrete electrical check, the SCC-TC guide describes shorting the input after 30 minutes of powered operation and adjusting its offset to a zero-voltage readout. Its separate calibrator procedure requires equal connection <temperatures> at the calibrator and module, measures the supplied voltage, converts it to <temperature>, and compares with the calibrator setting. Such an injected-signal test checks the readout chain; it does not expose a real measuring junction to an independently known bath <temperature>.<SCC-TC Series Thermocouple Input Modules User Guide by National Instruments>[p. 9, Calibrating System Offsets; Calibrating the SCC-TC0X Using a Thermocouple Calibrator]{ref}

Useful additional checks follow from the model: log the raw voltage, the reference <temperature>, and the final inferred <temperature> separately; hold $T_h$ stable while changing $T_c$ and test whether <cold-junction compensation> preserves the inferred $T_h$; compare against an independent <thermometer> at several stable <heat-bath> settings. Check immersion and repeatability instead of assuming a steady display establishes <thermal equilibrium>. These are proposed diagnostic checks, not new experimental results. The equal-<temperature> and polarity controls are already described under <Seebeck effect>.

Limits arise separately from the <thermocouple reference function>, sensor-wire changes, thermal contact, terminal <temperature> differences, and the voltage-input circuit. In particular, the SCC-TC guide warns of connector-to-module <temperature gradients> and voltage drops caused by the fault-detection <electric current> flowing through lead <electrical resistance>.<SCC-TC Series Thermocouple Input Modules User Guide by National Instruments>[p. 6, Uncompensated Connectors and Accuracy; p. 7, Errors Due to Open-Thermocouple Detection Circuitry]{ref} A fitted curve or compensation algorithm cannot make all these errors vanish. No single accuracy is assigned to every <thermocouple>.

= Thermocouple reference function
{parent=Thermocouple voltage calibration}
{title2=$F(T)=E(T,0)$}

A <thermocouple reference function> gives the nominal thermoelectric <electromotive force> versus <temperature> for a specified material pair and reference <temperature>, conventionally $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Real <Seebeck coefficients> vary with <temperature>, so
$$
F'(T)=S_A(T)-S_B(T)
$$
is generally nonconstant. A lookup calibration can use <interpolation> between tabulated values; a fitted calibration can use <polynomials> over stated intervals. Use the correct material type, units, reference and validity interval in both the forward and inverse conversions. The FP-TC-120 manual specifically requires the channel's <thermocouple> type to be configured and describes its use of NIST-175 reference characteristics.<FP-TC-120 and cFP-TC-120 Operating Instructions by National Instruments>[p. 6, Connecting Thermocouple Input Signals: channel type and NIST-175 linearization]{ref} A nominal reference curve is not evidence that an aged or inhomogeneous wire still follows it.

A local error estimate explains why the reference <thermometer> matters. If $\delta V$ is an additive voltage error and $\delta T_c$ is the error in the supplied reference <temperature>, <differentiation> of the compensation equation gives, for $F'(T_h)\ne0$,
$$
\delta T_h\approx\frac{\delta V+F'(T_c)\,\delta T_c}{F'(T_h)}.
$$
A small <slope> $F'(T_h)$ magnifies voltage errors. In the stipulated $40\,\mu\mathrm{V/K}$ model, a $40\,\mu\mathrm V$ offset gives a $1\,\mathrm K$ inferred-<temperature> error, and a $1\,\mathrm K$ reference error gives a $1\,\mathrm K$ inferred-<temperature> error. These are sensitivities of the chosen model, not universal performance figures for real sensors.

= Lorenz-gauge reduction of the electromagnetic action
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}

The <electromagnetic field tensor> action differs from the component-gradient potential action by $(2\mu_0)^{-1}\int \partial^bA^a\partial_aA_b\,d^4x$. Integration by parts expresses this term as a boundary integral plus $(2\mu_0)^{-1}\int(\partial_aA^a)^2\,d^4x$. With the <Lorenz gauge> and fixed or vanishing boundary data, the two actions therefore differ only by a boundary term. Varying the component-gradient action without restriction gives the potential wave equations; the Lorenz condition must be supplied to recover all <Maxwell equations>.

= Bioelectromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

= Volume conductor
{parent=Bioelectromagnetism}

A <volume conductor> is a conducting three-dimensional medium through which <electric currents> flow. In the quasistatic bioelectric model, the total <current density> is $\mathbf J=\mathbf J^p-\sigma\nabla u$, comprising an impressed source current and its Ohmic return current. <Electric charge conservation> gives $\nabla\cdot(\sigma\nabla u)=\nabla\cdot\mathbf J^p$. An insulating boundary has zero normal total current.

= Geselowitz formula
{c}
{parent=Volume conductor}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\mathbf B^p-\frac{\mu_0\sigma}{4\pi}\int_S u\,\mathbf n'\times\frac{\mathbf r-\mathbf r'}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r'|^3}\,dS'$}

For a homogeneous <volume conductor> with an insulating boundary $S$, the <Geselowitz formula> expresses the exterior <magnetic field> as the primary <Biot-Savart law> field minus a surface contribution from the induced <electric potential>. For a <current dipole>, $\mathbf B^p=\mu_0\mathbf Q\times(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)/(4\pi|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|^3)$. On a spherical boundary, $\mathbf n'$ is parallel to $\mathbf r'$, so the surface term has zero radial component at the observation point.

= Spherical volume conductor model
{parent=Volume conductor}
{title2=$|\mathbf r|<a$}

A <spherical volume conductor model> approximates a conducting body by a sphere, usually with uniform or radially stratified conductivity. It provides explicit forward models for <electroencephalography> and <magnetoencephalography>. Its symmetry makes a <radial current dipole> magnetically silent outside the sphere; departures from spherical symmetry need not retain that property.

= Sarvas formula
{c}
{parent=Spherical volume conductor model}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\nabla\frac{(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\mathbf r}{F}$}

For the exterior of a <spherical volume conductor model>, put $P=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|$, $r=|\mathbf r|$ and $F=rP^2+P\mathbf r\cdot(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)$. The displayed <Sarvas formula> follows by taking the radial component of the <Geselowitz formula> and integrating the <magnetic scalar potential> from infinity. It is independent of a spherically symmetric conductivity profile. The sign shown uses $\mathbf B=+\mu_0\nabla U/(4\pi)$; conventions defining a magnetic potential with a minus sign reverse $U$. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3823129/[Sarvas's original paper] derives the spherical-conductor field and its consequences for the inverse problem.

= Spherical current-dipole logarithmic kernel
{parent=Sarvas formula}
{title2=$\phi=\frac1b\log\frac{r+P+b}{r+P-b},\quad b=|\mathbf r_0|$}

For $r>b$ and $P=|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|$, the displayed kernel satisfies $\phi=\int_r^\infty ds/[s|s\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf r_0|]$. The limit at $b=0$ is $1/r$. Differentiating along $\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0$, which preserves $b$, gives $(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf r_0}\phi=(\mathbf Q\times\mathbf r_0)\cdot\mathbf r/F$. Thus it generates the normalized <magnetic scalar potential> in the <Sarvas formula>.

= Maxwell–Chern–Simons one-form equation
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$d{*F}+kF=0$}

For a <differential one-form> $A$ on an oriented three-dimensional spacetime, varying $\frac12\int(F\wedge*F+kA\wedge F)$, with $F=dA$, gives $d*F+kF=0$. With signature $(-,+,+)$, ${*}^2=-1$ on one- and two-forms; writing $B=*F$ gives $dB-k*B=0$ and the identity $d*B=0$. An Abelian <gauge transformation> changes the action by $\frac k2\int_{\partial M}\Lambda F$. Thus the bulk field equation is invariant, while invariance of the integrated action depends on boundary conditions.

= Electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$\mathbf E,\mathbf B$}
{wiki}

= Maxwell field
{synonym}
{c}

The combined <electric field> and <magnetic field> that determine the <Lorentz force> on a charged particle.

= Electromagnetic field momentum
{parent=Electromagnetic field}
{title2=$\mathbf P_{\mathrm{field}}$}

An <electromagnetic field> carries <momentum>. Its contribution joins the <mechanical momentum> of matter and <momentum> crossing the chosen system boundary in a conservation balance.

\Include[electrical-circuit]{parent=Electromagnetism}

= Gaussian units
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

= Capacitor
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A <capacitor> stores <electrostatic energy> by separating <electric charge> between two <electrical conductors>. In the ideal two-terminal model their <electric charges> are $+Q$ and $-Q$, with a vacuum or insulating gap preventing direct transfer between them. Write $V=\phi_+-\phi_-$ for the terminal <electric potential difference>, where $\phi_+$ and $\phi_-$ are the <electric potentials> of the two <electrical conductors>. A linear <capacitor> obeys
$$
Q=CV,
$$
where its <capacitance> $C$ depends on the geometry and <permittivity>, and is independent of the applied <electric potential difference> while these stay fixed. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: capacitors and capacitance]{ref}.

For example, the <parallel-plate capacitor> below has <capacitance> $C=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$. At terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$, the <electric charge> magnitude on either plate is
$$
Q=CV=(8.854\times10^{-11})(10)\,\mathrm C
=8.854\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm C=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}.
$$
The total <electric charge> of the device is $Q+(-Q)=0$: <charge neutrality> does not imply zero <capacitor energy>. This <capacitor> stores $4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$ in its <electric field>. The schematic in <parallel-plate capacitor> shows the separation of <electric charge>; the plots in <capacitor energy> show how storage grows with terminal <electric potential difference>.

The useful distinction is between storing net <electric charge> and storing separated <electric charge>. A <capacitor> can release <electrostatic energy> into an <electrical circuit>; in an ideal <RLC circuit>, <energy> moves between the <capacitor>'s <electric field> and the <magnetic field> associated with <inductance>. An ideal disconnected <capacitor> retains its separated <electric charges> and <capacitor energy> because there is no path for discharge.

= Parallel-plate capacitor
{parent=Capacitor}
{title2=$C=\epsilon A/d$}

A <parallel-plate capacitor> consists of two parallel <electrical conductors> with overlapping <area> $A$ and separation $d$. If the gap is small compared with both plate widths and is filled by a uniform material of positive <permittivity> $\epsilon$, its <capacitance> is approximately
$$
C\simeq\frac{\epsilon A}{d}.
$$
This neglects the <fringing field of a capacitor> near the plate edges. The interior <electric field> points from the plate of positive <electric charge> to the plate of negative <electric charge>, and is approximately uniform. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: the parallel-plate approximation]{ref}.

For a worked vacuum example, stipulate $A=0.01\,\mathrm{m^2}$, $d=0.001\,\mathrm m$, and <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0\approx8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$. This rounds the value in <Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>. Within the approximation,
$$
C\simeq\frac{(8.854\times10^{-12})(0.01)}{0.001}\,\mathrm F
=8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}.
$$
At terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$, the <electric field> magnitude is $E\simeq V/d=10^4\,\mathrm{V/m}$, the plate <electric charge> magnitude is $Q=CV=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$, and the <capacitor energy> is $U=CV^2/2=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$. These numbers use the stipulated rounded <vacuum permittivity>; finite plates also have corrections from the <fringing field of a capacitor>.

\Image[parallel-plate-capacitor-field.png]
{title=Uniform <electric field> in a <parallel-plate capacitor>}

The formula makes design changes easy to predict: doubling the overlapping <area> doubles the <capacitance>, and halving the gap doubles the <capacitance>, with <permittivity> fixed and the <fringing field of a capacitor> neglected. Doubling <permittivity> also doubles <capacitance> if the material uniformly fills the gap and remains linear. This lets a compact device store more separated <electric charge> at the same terminal <electric potential difference>.

The short derivation uses <surface charge density> $\sigma=Q/A$. In the interior, <Gauss's law> gives <electric field> magnitude $E\simeq\sigma/\epsilon=Q/(\epsilon A)$. The <electric potential difference> is $V\simeq Ed=Qd/(\epsilon A)$, so dividing $Q$ by $V$ gives $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$. A narrow curved <capacitor> gap has the same leading local approximation when its width is much less than the curvature radius.

= Fringing field of a capacitor
{parent=Parallel-plate capacitor}

= Capacitor fringing field
{synonym}

The <fringing field of a capacitor> is the nonuniform <electric field> near the edges of its <electrical conductors>, extending outside the gap. For a <parallel-plate capacitor>, neglecting it is useful when the gap is much smaller than both plate widths; the approximation $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$ is less reliable when these lengths become comparable.

Finite plates break the translational <symmetry> of the infinite-plate model. Each <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium> is an <equipotential>, so the <electric field> must meet a smooth conducting surface normally. Near an edge, the <equipotentials> curve into the surrounding space and the <electric field> changes both direction and magnitude. The <surface charge density> adjusts to enforce the conducting <boundary conditions>; it is not uniform all the way to a sharp edge. The <electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor> explain why zero <electric field> inside the metal does not imply zero <electric field> just outside it.

The practical issue is where the nearly uniform region ends. A probe near the centre can support $E\simeq V/d$ while a probe close to an edge detects a lateral component and a different magnitude. A total <capacitance> reading also includes <electric flux> outside the simple footprint. The <fringing field of a capacitor> can therefore matter in <permittivity> measurements even when a central <electric field> measurement looks nearly ideal. Keysight's three-<electrode> fixture diverts the fringe contribution away from the measured response.<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Solution: fringe capacitance and the three-electrode 16451B fixture]{ref}

\Image[capacitor-fringing-field-and-guard-electrode.png]
{title=Finite-plate <electric field> and a separate <guard electrode of a capacitor>}
{description=Left: original <bounded two-dimensional capacitor field model> with $d/W=0.25$, showing computed <equipotentials> and <electric field> direction. The visible window is a crop of the stated bounded domain. Right: a conceptual cross-section through a surrounding guard ring, with an insulated central sensing <electrode>; the arrows are schematic, not a computed guarded solution. Neither panel is measured data.}

= Capacitor gap aspect ratio
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}
{title2=$\eta=d/W$}

The <capacitor gap aspect ratio> compares the gap $d$ with a lateral plate width $W$. For rectangular plates of widths $W_x,W_z$, require both $d/W_x\ll1$ and $d/W_z\ll1$ for the leading <parallel-plate capacitor> approximation. Large <area> alone is insufficient: a long narrow strip can have large <area> and still have $d$ comparable with its smaller width. In addition, the observation point must be many gap widths from an edge. Reducing $d/W$ improves the interior approximation without making the edge itself disappear.

In a two-dimensional straight gap, write the <electric potential> as the infinite-plate solution plus a perturbation:
$$
\phi(x,y)=\frac{V}{d}y+\delta\phi(x,y),\qquad -\frac d2<y<\frac d2.
$$
The perturbation satisfies the <Laplace equation> and vanishes on the two ideal plates. <Separation of variables> in a semi-infinite strip, with $s$ the distance inward from its entrance, gives transverse modes
$$
\delta\phi_n(s,y)=a_n e^{-n\pi s/d}\sin\!\left(\frac{n\pi(y+d/2)}{d}\right),\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.
$$
Thus edge disturbances have a natural penetration scale set by $d$. Modes forbidden by a particular <symmetry> have zero coefficients; this expression is an explanation of localization in an ideal strip, not a universal numerical correction to finite-plate <capacitance>. The outside <boundary conditions> determine the coefficients.

\Image[capacitor-fringing-small-gap-aspect-ratio.png]
{title=<Capacitor gap aspect ratio> and the width of the nearly uniform <electric field> region}
{description=Three original solutions of the same <bounded two-dimensional capacitor field model> at $d/W=0.10,0.25,0.50$. The lower curves show $-E_y d/V$ on the midplane $y=0$; the dashed reference is the infinite-plate value inside the gap. The dashed value is not the infinite-plate prediction outside the plate footprint. All curves are calculated, with the same mesh and outer <Dirichlet boundary conditions>; they are neither measurements nor an exact open-domain three-dimensional <capacitance> calculation.}

There is no single aspect-ratio cutoff guaranteeing an assigned accuracy: the requested observable, plate shape and thickness, surroundings, <dielectric> distribution, and guard geometry all matter. The small-gap approximation is local and is not uniform across an edge. It also does not justify extrapolating $C\simeq\epsilon A/d$ to arbitrarily large $d$, where finite <electrical conductors> and their external <electric field> dominate. At fixed $V$, formally shrinking an ideal gap makes the interior <electric field> grow like $1/d$; real <electric currents> across an imperfectly insulating gap, surface roughness and contact eventually invalidate that ideal limit.

= Bounded two-dimensional capacitor field model
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}
{title2=$\partial_x^2\phi+\partial_y^2\phi=0$}

The diagrams solve an explicitly bounded <Dirichlet problem> in a uniform charge-free medium of constant <permittivity>. Take a width scale $W=1$ and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=1$. Two zero-thickness ideal plate segments occupy $|x|\leq W/2$ at $y=\pm d/2$, with <electric potentials> $\pm V/2$. Fix $\phi=0$ on the entire rectangle $x=\pm2W$, $y=\pm1.5W$. This boundary is an additional enclosing <equipotential>, rather than a condition at infinity. The model is invariant along the omitted third direction. The connection between the <electric potential>, its <gradient>, and the charge-free <Laplace equation> follows from <electrostatics>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[II.6–1, equations (6.3)–(6.6)]{ref}

On a square mesh of spacing $h=W/160$, the <finite difference method> imposes
$$
\phi_{i,j}=\frac14\left(\phi_{i+1,j}+\phi_{i-1,j}+\phi_{i,j+1}+\phi_{i,j-1}\right)
$$
at free nodes, while plate and outer nodes retain their prescribed <electric potentials>. There are $641\times481$ nodes. Each plotted gap aligns exactly with the mesh; the smallest gap has 16 mesh intervals. The generators solve the resulting <linear system> by the <conjugate gradient method>, stopping when the maximum dimensionless five-point residual is below $2\times10^{-10}$, and use <central finite differences> to approximate $\mathbf E=-\nabla\phi$ away from plate nodes.

The <discrete maximum principle> keeps the computed <electric potential> between $-V/2$ and $+V/2$. Reflection checks require evenness in $x$ and oddness in $y$; halving $h$ tests discretization effects, while enlarging the outer rectangle tests sensitivity to its artificial <boundary conditions>. These are different checks: a small residual only establishes that the discrete equations were solved. Differentiating near zero-thickness endpoints is especially sensitive to the mesh, so the plots do not report a converged edge peak. No total <electric charge>, exact open-domain three-dimensional <capacitance>, or measured material property is extracted from this model.

For a bounded numerical check, compare $h=W/80$ with the plotted $h=W/160$ at midplane positions $x/W=0,0.25,0.40,0.50,0.60,0.80$. At $d/W=0.10$, the largest change in $-E_y d/V$ is approximately $0.0265$, at $x/W=0.50$; the other five sampled changes are below $0.00472$. At $d/W=0.25$, moving the outer rectangle from $\pm2W\times\pm1.5W$ to $\pm3W\times\pm2W$ while retaining $h=W/80$ changes these samples by at most approximately $0.00622$. These calculated sensitivities support the qualitative comparison and expose its limits; they are not experimental uncertainties or evidence that the artificial boundary has converged to infinity.

= Mapping a capacitor fringing field
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}

The <Field Mapper Kit (PK-9023), Product Manual 012-04346D by PASCO scientific> (p. 3, Parallel plate capacitor) proposes a direct investigation of the plate-length-to-gap ratio using <conductive-paper electric potential mapping>. Its finite straight <electrodes> represent a cross-section of the plates. The apparatus is a gridded resistive sheet on a corkboard, silver-ink <electrodes>, pushpin electrical contacts, a $5$–$20\,\mathrm V$ direct supply capable of $25\,\mathrm{mA}$, and a <voltmeter> with input <electrical resistance> at least $10\,\mathrm{M\Omega}$. The manual specifies waiting 20 minutes for the ink to reach its maximum <electrical conductivity>, then checking each <electrode>: its internal voltage drop should be no more than $1\%$ of the applied <electric potential difference>.<Field Mapper Kit (PK-9023), Product Manual 012-04346D by PASCO scientific>[pp. 1–2: required equipment, drying, contacts, electrode-conductivity check]{ref}

For the readout, keep one <voltmeter> lead on the reference <electrode> and scan the other across the sheet. Mark points having the same reading to trace an <equipotential>. A second method holds the two probe tips at a fixed separation and pivots them to find the largest <electric potential difference>; a suggested separation is one centimetre. Avoid printed grid marks when making contact.<Charge, Equipotential, and Field Mapper (ES-9060), Instruction Manual by PASCO scientific>[pp. 8–10, Parts III–IV: equipotential tracing and paired-probe field-gradient mapping]{ref}

The inference follows from the measured <electric potential>, rather than assuming straight <electric field> lines. With probe separation $\Delta s$ and direction $\widehat{\mathbf t}$, a local <finite difference> gives
$$
\mathbf E\cdot\widehat{\mathbf t}\simeq-\frac{\phi(P+\Delta s\widehat{\mathbf t})-\phi(P)}{\Delta s}.
$$
An arrow toward rising <electric potential> follows $+\nabla\phi$, so it must be reversed to show $\mathbf E$. To investigate the <fringing field of a capacitor>, compare contours well inside the gap with contours near and beyond the ends, then repeat at a changed gap while keeping the sheet boundary and plate length fixed. Curving contours and an outside <gradient> establish nonuniformity in this planar setup. Compare $-E_y d/V$ at recorded positions to distinguish simple voltage rescaling from geometric changes; no measured values are supplied here.

Additional useful controls are repeat reference readings to detect drift, reversal of supply polarity, and repeating selected readings with reduced probe spacing. Keep contact quality and sheet edges fixed during the aspect-ratio comparison. Finite probe separation averages the <gradient>; reducing it improves spatial resolution but magnifies the effect of voltage uncertainty. <Voltmeter loading>, imperfect ink <electrodes>, and inhomogeneous sheet <electrical conductivity> can mimic distortions. These proposed checks supplement the manufacturer's electrode check, rather than claiming a completed experiment.

The sheet carries a steady <electric current>. For uniform <electrical conductivity>, <Ohm's law> and <electric charge conservation> give the two-dimensional <Laplace equation> away from <electrodes>, as explained in <conductive-paper electric potential mapping>. Uncontacted sheet edges have zero normal <electric current>, whereas the diagram model fixes zero outer <electric potential>. These distinct <boundary conditions> must be matched before a quantitative comparison. The apparatus reveals a planar <electric potential> map; it does not directly measure the free-space three-dimensional <capacitance> of metal plates.

= Guard electrode of a capacitor
{parent=Fringing field of a capacitor}

A <guard electrode of a capacitor> surrounds an electrically isolated sensing <electrode> and is held at the same <electric potential>. The small insulating slot separates their readout paths. The guard extends the nearly planar <equipotential>, moving the main outer edge away from the sensing region; it does not abolish the <fringing field of a capacitor> everywhere. In a capacitance fixture, fringe <electric current> is routed through the guard instead of the sensing readout. Keysight describes this three-<electrode> method in <Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials> (Solution, 16451B fixture).<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Solution: guard absorbs current through fringe capacitance]{ref}

Measuring the sensing and guard <electric currents> together would include the diverted response again. Grounding an arbitrary surrounding <electrode> also does not reproduce a guard unless its <electric potential> matches the sensing <electrode>. Finite guard width, the insulating slot, plate misalignment and specimen air gaps limit the approximation. Use <capacitance measurement calibration> and the geometry appropriate to the sensing region when inferring <permittivity>. The source also warns that guarding changes the <electromagnetic field> unfavourably at high <frequency>, so the low-<frequency> electrostatic picture has a restricted range.<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Solution: air gaps, high-frequency guard limitations, fixture compensation]{ref}

= Spherical capacitor
{parent=Capacitor}
{title2=$C=4\pi\epsilon_0ab/(b-a)$}

Two concentric conducting spherical shells of radii $a<b$ with charges $Q,-Q$ have an <electric field> only in the intervening vacuum: $E_r=Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ by <Gauss's law>. Integrating gives a potential difference $Q(1/a-1/b)/(4\pi\epsilon_0)$, and hence the displayed <capacitance>. The <electrostatic energy> is $Q^2/(2C)$, either by charging work or by integrating $\epsilon_0|E|^2/2$ over the gap.

= Capacitor energy
{title2=$U=Q^2/(2C)$}
{parent=Capacitor}

The <capacitor energy> is the <electrostatic energy> stored when <electric charge> is separated. For a linear <capacitor> of fixed positive <capacitance> $C$, plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$, and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=Q/C$, it is
$$
U=\frac{Q^2}{2C}=\frac12CV^2=\frac12QV.
$$
The zero is the uncharged state. Thus the <capacitor energy> is positive for nonzero $Q$, even though the total <electric charge> of the two plates is zero. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-3-energy-stored-in-a-capacitor[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.3: energy stored in a capacitor]{ref}.

For the <parallel-plate capacitor> example, <capacitance> $C=8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F$ and terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$ give
$$
U=\frac12(8.854\times10^{-11})(10)^2\,\mathrm J
=4.427\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm J=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}.
$$
The plate <electric charge> magnitude is $Q=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$. Doubling terminal <electric potential difference> with <capacitance> unchanged doubles the plate <electric charge> magnitude and quadruples the <capacitor energy>.

\Image[capacitor-charge-and-energy.png]
{title=Linear <electric charge> and quadratic <capacitor energy> versus <electric potential difference>}

The <electric charge> is a <linear function> of terminal <electric potential difference>, while <capacitor energy> is a <quadratic function>. The second curve in each plot uses twice the <capacitance>: at a given terminal <electric potential difference>, both plate <electric charge> magnitude and <capacitor energy> double. These relations let us calculate how much <energy> an <electrical circuit> can receive when a <capacitor> discharges.

A brief charging argument explains the factor $1/2$. If the plate <electric charge> magnitude has reached $q$, the terminal <electric potential difference> is $q/C$. Transferring a further amount $dq$ of <electric charge> quasistatically requires <work> $dW=(q/C)\,dq$. With the geometry and <permittivity> fixed, the <integral> is
$$
U=\int_0^Q\frac qC\,dq=\frac{Q^2}{2C}.
$$
Using $Q=CV$ gives the other two forms. This <integral> accounts for the gradually rising terminal <electric potential difference>; it is the <work> stored in the <capacitor>'s <electric field>, rather than a claim that all <energy> drawn from a fixed-voltage <battery> during charging ends up stored.

The field picture gives the same result. In the vacuum gap of a <parallel-plate capacitor>, the <electric field> has <energy density> $u=\epsilon_0 E^2/2$. Multiplying by the gap <volume> $Ad$ gives the <electrostatic energy>
$$
U\simeq\frac12\epsilon_0E^2Ad
=\frac12\frac{\epsilon_0 A}{d}V^2
=\frac12CV^2.
$$
For the worked example, $u=4.427\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{J/m^3}$ and $Ad=10^{-5}\,\mathrm{m^3}$ reproduce $U=4.427\,\mathrm{nJ}$. The <electrostatic energy> resides in the <electric field> throughout the gap, despite overall <charge neutrality>.

When <capacitance> changes, specify the constraint. At fixed plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$, increasing <capacitance> decreases <capacitor energy> $Q^2/(2C)$; doubling <capacitance> halves it. At fixed terminal <electric potential difference> $V$, increasing <capacitance> increases <capacitor energy> $CV^2/2$; doubling <capacitance> doubles it. In the latter case a connected <battery> supplies additional <electric charge> and <energy>, and there may also be mechanical <work>. Use the <fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance> to account for all transfers before inferring a <force> from changing <capacitor energy>.

= Fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance
{parent=Capacitor energy}
{title2=$dU_{\rm field}=dW_{\rm mech}+V\,dQ$}

For a quasistatic change of <capacitance> at fixed terminal <electric potential difference>, the <capacitor energy> is $U=CV^2/2$ and plate <electric charge> is $Q=CV$. Electrical <work> supplied to the <electric field> by the <battery> is $V\,dQ$, so mechanical <work> done on the <capacitor> is $dW_{\rm mech}=dU-V\,dQ=-V^2dC/2$. Increasing the separation of a <parallel-plate capacitor> makes $dC<0$: mechanical <work> is positive even though <electrostatic energy> decreases, because electrical <energy> returns to the source. Conversely, when $dC>0$, the <battery> supplies $V^2dC$: half increases <capacitor energy> and half is available as mechanical <work> done by the <electric field>. Differentiating <capacitor energy> alone at fixed terminal <electric potential difference> omits this exchange with the <battery>.

= Electromagnetic interaction
{parent=Electromagnetism}

The electromagnetic interaction is the long-range interaction of electrically charged matter with the electromagnetic field. Its massless gauge boson is the <photon>.

= Electromagnetic radiation
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

<Electromagnetic radiation> is a propagating disturbance of the <electric field> and <magnetic field> that carries <energy> and <momentum> away from its source. It can be a sustained <electromagnetic wave> or a finite <wave packet>: periodic oscillation is not required. In vacuum the disturbance travels at the <speed of light>. A receiver responds to fields arriving now from an earlier source event, as expressed by <retarded time>. This is how electrical activity at one place can deliver <energy> and information elsewhere without a connecting wire.

Stored <electromagnetic energy> alone does not establish outgoing <electromagnetic radiation>. A stationary <electric charge> in <electrostatics>, or a steady localized <electric current> in <magnetostatics>, can store <electromagnetic energy> with zero <Poynting vector>. Static combinations can have circulating <energy flux> without net outward <power>. Near a changing source, reactive <energy> can return later. The <near and far field> distinction separates these contributions from outgoing <radiation zone> fields.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 27: Field Energy and Field Momentum>[§27-5, Fig. 27-6: static fields with circulating energy flux]{ref}

A single traveling vacuum <plane electromagnetic wave> obeying the <Maxwell equations> has $\mathbf B=\widehat{\mathbf k}\times\mathbf E/c$. Its <Poynting vector> gives the <energy density and flux of a plane electromagnetic wave>:
$$
\mathbf S=\frac{\mathbf E\times\mathbf B}{\mu_0}
=\epsilon_0c|\mathbf E|^2\widehat{\mathbf k},\qquad
I=\langle\mathbf S\rangle\cdot\widehat{\mathbf k}
=\frac{c\epsilon_0E_0^2}{2}.
$$
Here $I$ is <wave intensity>; $\epsilon_0$ and $\mu_0$ are <vacuum permittivity> and <vacuum permeability>. For a <monochromatic wave> with <linear polarization> and peak amplitude $E_0$, squared <cosine> averages to $1/2$. Pulses require the instantaneous <Poynting vector>; arbitrary incident-and-reflected superpositions need not obey this relation.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 27: Field Energy and Field Momentum>[§27-5, Eqs. (27.16)–(27.18): traveling-wave energy flux]{ref}

The <electromagnetic momentum density> is $\mathbf g=\mathbf S/c^2$. Completely absorbing a unidirectional vacuum pulse of <energy> $U$ transfers <momentum> $U/c$; see <radiation pressure on a perfectly absorbing surface>. A calculated $1.0\,\mathrm{mJ}$ pulse transfers $3.34\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$. Symmetric emission can lose <energy> without net recoil because opposite <momenta> cancel.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 27: Field Energy and Field Momentum>[§27-6, Eq. (27.21) and Fig. 27-7: field momentum and absorbed energy]{ref}

The direction of emission matters as much as its total <power>. For an oscillating <electric dipole moment> along $z$, the <angular distribution of electric dipole radiation> is strongest across the axis and vanishes along it. Its origin is geometric: the <electric dipole radiation> field is the transverse projection of the second time derivative of the <electric dipole moment>, so its magnitude contains $\sin\theta$. Squaring this <electric field> to obtain <wave intensity> gives $\sin^2\theta$. The <electric-dipole approximation for radiation> requires a source much smaller than its <wavelength> and observation in the <radiation zone>. <The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 28: Electromagnetic Radiation>[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, Electromagnetic Radiation, §§28-2–28-3: transverse acceleration and dipole geometry]{ref}

\Image[electric-dipole-radiation-angular-pattern.png]
{title=Normalized angular pattern of <electric dipole radiation>}
{description=Calculated <radiation-zone> model, not measured data. The polar curve's radial coordinate is normalized <power> per <solid angle>, $\sin^2\theta$, rather than physical distance or a field line. The angle $\theta$ is measured from the dipole axis. Rotating this meridional pattern about that axis gives the three-dimensional angular distribution.}

For example, the <power> per <solid angle> at $\theta=30^\circ$ is one quarter of its equatorial value, while the <electric field> amplitude is one half. To normalize the angular distribution to total <power>, use <spherical coordinates> with $d\Omega=\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi$:
$$
\int\sin^2\theta\,d\Omega
=2\pi\int_0^\pi\sin^3\theta\,d\theta=\frac{8\pi}{3},\qquad
\frac{1}{P}\frac{dP}{d\Omega}=\frac{3}{8\pi}\sin^2\theta.
$$
The <integral> follows by substituting $x=\cos\theta$, giving $\int_{-1}^1(1-x^2)\,dx=4/3$. This is a probability-like normalization over direction, with units of inverse <steradian>; it does not make the dipole an isotropic radiator. The same angular <integral> underlies the <Larmor formula> for nonrelativistic accelerated <electric charge>.

Detection requires the incident <electromagnetic field> to do something observable. An <antenna> develops an electrical response; a <thermopile> converts absorbed <energy> into a thermal and then electrical signal; a <photodetector> can respond through <photon> absorption. <Hertz spark transmission and detection experiment> makes this conversion visible as a tiny spark. Detector orientation, sensitivity, and surrounding reflections all matter: failure to see a signal does not prove that no <electromagnetic radiation> reached the receiver.

= Radio wave
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

A <radio wave> is <electromagnetic radiation> in the radio portion of the <frequency spectrum>. In vacuum its <frequency> and <wavelength> obey the <vacuum frequency-wavelength relation>.

= Microwave
{parent=Radio wave}
{wiki}

= Vacuum frequency-wavelength relation
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{title2=$\nu\lambda=c$}

A <monochromatic wave> in vacuum has <frequency> $\nu$ and <wavelength> $\lambda$ related by $\nu\lambda=c$, where $c$ is the <speed of light>. During one <oscillation period> $T=1/\nu$, a constant-<wave-phase> surface travels one <wavelength>, so $\lambda=cT$. Equivalently, the <plane electromagnetic wave> relation $\omega=ck$ with <angular frequency> $\omega=2\pi\nu$ and <wavenumber> $k=2\pi/\lambda$ gives the same result.

As a calculated example, a vacuum <radio wave> with $\nu=300\,\mathrm{MHz}=3.00\times10^8\,\mathrm{Hz}$ has $\lambda=0.999308\,\mathrm m$ using $c=299792458\,\mathrm{m/s}$. Doubling <frequency> halves <wavelength>, independently of <wave amplitude> and <wave intensity>. The <frequency spectrum> of a pulse contains multiple <frequencies>; the relation applies to each vacuum component, rather than assigning one exact <wavelength> to the whole pulse. In a homogeneous medium replace $c$ by the appropriate <phase velocity>; the vacuum equation does not assert that all media have the same <wavelength> at a given <frequency>.

= Outgoing radiation pulse
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{title2=$\overline S_r(r,t)=P_{\rm emit}(t-r/c)/(4\pi r^2)$}

An <outgoing radiation pulse> carries a finite amount of <electromagnetic energy> through successively larger enclosing <spheres>. In vacuum, far from a localized source and without absorption or additional reflections, let $P_{\rm emit}(t)$ be the emitted <power>. The <retarded time> at <radius> $r$ is $s=t-r/c$. Define $\overline S_r$ as the angular average of the radial <Poynting vector> over the <sphere>, not a cycle average:
$$
\overline S_r(r,t)=\frac{P_{\rm emit}(t-r/c)}{4\pi r^2},\qquad
\overline u(r,t)=\frac{\overline S_r(r,t)}{c},\qquad
\frac{dU}{dr}=4\pi r^2\overline u=\frac{P_{\rm emit}(t-r/c)}c.
$$
Here $\overline u$ is the angularly averaged <energy density> in the leading <radiation-zone> approximation. Multiplying by shell <area> removes geometric dilution: the <energy> per unit radial thickness translates outward without losing its shape. These angular averages do not assume that an actual <electric dipole> emits equally in all directions. Nor do they specify an exact spherically symmetric transverse solution of the <Maxwell equations>.

For an explicitly stipulated model, emit
$$
P_{\rm emit}(s)=P_0g(s/\tau),\qquad
g(q)=\begin{cases}\sin^2(\pi q),&0\le q\le1,\\0,&\text{otherwise},\end{cases}
\qquad U=\int P_{\rm emit}(s)\,ds=\frac{P_0\tau}{2}.
$$
After emission ends, $t>\tau$, the pulse lies between $r=c(t-\tau)$ and $r=ct$, provided this shell is in the <radiation zone>. Its radial thickness remains $c\tau$. Substituting $s=t-r/c$ in the shell <integral> $\int4\pi r^2\overline u\,dr$ gives $U=P_0\tau/2$ at every such time. The same substitution in the time <integral> of $4\pi r^2\overline S_r$ at any enclosing <sphere> gives that same total crossing <energy>. This is <conservation of energy> in two different views.

\Image[outgoing-radiation-pulse-spherical-spreading.png]
{title=Outgoing pulse: increasing shell <area> dilutes local <energy flux>}
{description=Original calculated model with $L=c\tau$ and $P_{\rm emit}=P_0\sin^2(\pi s/\tau)$ during emission. The circle diagrams show shell support, not equally strong emission in all directions. The lower-left curves show angularly integrated <energy> per radius and have equal areas. The lower-right curves compare <electric field> and <energy-flux> ratios at corresponding <retarded times> along a fixed direction. All values are model predictions, not historical measurements.}

Along any fixed direction with nonzero outgoing <power>, compare the same part of the pulse at two <radii>, hence at different arrival times. The leading <electric field> amplitude falls as $1/r$, while its squared <wave intensity> falls as $1/r^2$. At $2r$ the field is one half and the <energy flux> one quarter; the signal arrives $r/c$ later. A small fixed <area> receiver therefore intercepts less <energy> at greater distance even though every enclosing <sphere> receives the same total pulse <energy>. The <inverse-square law> expresses spreading over <area>, rather than destruction of <energy>. Close to the source, the neglected <near and far field> terms prevent extrapolating these formulas down to $r=0$.

= Hertz spark transmission and detection experiment
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}

In his 1888 reflection experiments, <Heinrich Hertz> excited a straight conductor with a central <spark gap> driven by an <induction coil>. The receiver was a wire circle of $35\,\mathrm{cm}$ <radius> with an adjustable small <spark gap>; tiny sparks were observed in darkness. There was no connecting transmission wire. <Electric Waves: Being Researches on the Propagation of Electric Action with Finite Velocity through Space by Heinrich Hertz>[Hertz, Electric Waves (1893 translation), VII, pp. 108–109; VIII, pp. 125–127]{ref}

He removed hanging metal fixtures, installed a $4\,\mathrm m$ by $2\,\mathrm m$ zinc reflecting sheet, and moved and rotated the receiver. Spark strength alternated with position and gap orientation, supporting <specular reflection> and <wave interference>. He checked observations from farther away because the observer's body perturbed the response. Changing oscillator dimensions changed the spatial pattern. <Electric Waves: Being Researches on the Propagation of Electric Action with Finite Velocity through Space by Heinrich Hertz>[Hertz, Electric Waves, VIII, pp. 125–127 and 135–136: preparations and controls]{ref}

These tests supported propagating <electromagnetic waves>, rather than mere detection of nearby induction. They did not establish unlimited reception distance. His wire-versus-air <wave interference> experiment addressed finite <velocity>; he later judged its quantitative velocity comparison doubtful because of room effects. <Electric Waves: Being Researches on the Propagation of Electric Action with Finite Velocity through Space by Heinrich Hertz>[Hertz, Electric Waves, VII, pp. 107–108; Introduction, pp. 9–12: retrospective limitations]{ref}

The physical inference combines transmission with a spatially structured response to a reflected disturbance. In an ideal monochromatic incident-and-reflected model, <standing wave> nodes are separated by $\lambda/2$; combining a reliable <wavelength> with independently known <frequency> gives <phase velocity> $v=\nu\lambda$. A spark is a threshold event, however: its presence or length is not a calibrated <wave intensity> or a direct arrival-time measurement. The actual loop also responds to its orientation and finite size, so the simple standing-wave model is an explanation of the test's logic, rather than a reconstruction of Hertz's measured points. <Poynting theorem> explains how source <energy> can reach and drive the receiver's discharge.

= X-ray
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}

X-rays are high-frequency <electromagnetic radiation>, conventionally with wavelengths of roughly $0.01$ to $10\,\mathrm{nm}$. The precise wavelength convention varies with context. Hot accreting gas and energetic electronic processes can emit X-rays, whose short wavelengths make them useful probes of compact objects and atomic structure.

= Larmor formula
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{c}
{title2=$P=\frac{q^2\mu_0a^2}{6\pi c}$}
{wiki}

The nonrelativistic radiated power of an accelerated point charge is $P=q^2\mu_0a^2/(6\pi c)$ in <SI> units. Integrating its dipole radiation pattern over solid angle gives this result; relativistic motion requires the Lienard generalization.

= Liénard radiated-power formula
{c}
{parent=Larmor formula}
{title2=$P=\mu_0q^2\gamma^6(|a|^2-|v\times a|^2)/(6\pi)$, $c=1$}

= Lienard formula
{c}
{synonym}

The relativistic generalization of the <Larmor formula> uses the invariant proper-acceleration magnitude: $P=-\mu_0q^2 A^aA_a/(6\pi)$ in signature $(+---)$. In coordinate time this is the displayed expression. For perpendicular magnetic circular motion, $\gamma m|a|=|q|vB$, giving $P=\mu_0q^4(\gamma^2-1)B^2/(6\pi m^2)$. The statement concerns total instantaneous emitted power; directional radiation flux is not itself invariant.

= Mie scattering
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{c}
{wiki}

The electromagnetic scattering solution for a sphere describes the transition between <Rayleigh scattering> and <geometrical optics>. Grain-size-dependent efficiencies can produce structure in the <radiation-pressure coefficient>.

= Bremsstrahlung
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

<Bremsstrahlung> is <electromagnetic radiation> emitted when a charged particle accelerates during interaction with another charged particle or field. The microscopic process $e^-+Z\to e^-+Z+\gamma$ creates a <photon>, while its inverse absorbs one. Thus it contributes to a photon-number collision source in the <photon number current>, unlike ordinary <Compton scattering>. <Thermal bremsstrahlung> averages this emission over a thermal distribution of charged particles.

= Thermal radiation
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

Thermal radiation is <electromagnetic radiation> emitted by matter because of its temperature. Matter in <thermal equilibrium> emits a spectrum governed by its absorptivity, approaching the <Planck law> for a <blackbody>.

= Scattering efficiency
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{title2=$Q_{\rm sca}=\sigma/(\pi a^2)$}

For a <sphere> of <radius> $a$, the <scattering efficiency> is its effective scattering <area> divided by the projected <geometric cross-section> $\pi a^2$. It is a <dimensionless quantity>. For <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere> with relative <refractive index> $m$ and incident <wavenumber> $k$,
$$
Q_{\rm sca}=\frac{\sigma}{\pi a^2}=\frac83(ka)^4\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2.
$$
At fixed <wavelength> and fixed <refractive index>, the <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> scales as $a^6$, whereas the <scattering efficiency> scales as $a^4$. Doubling <radius> multiplies the former by $64$ and the latter by $16$. An efficiency here is an effective scattering <area> relative to a reference <area>, rather than a fraction of all light in an arbitrary beam.

For a worked toy example, take $a=10\,\mathrm{nm}$, $m=1.5$, and $\lambda=550\,\mathrm{nm}$. Then $ka=2\pi(10/550)\simeq0.1142$ and $(m^2-1)/(m^2+2)=5/17$. Substitution gives <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma\simeq1.234\times10^{-20}\,\mathrm{m}^2$ and <scattering efficiency> $Q_{\rm sca}\simeq3.929\times10^{-5}$. At incident <light intensity> $1\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, the scattered <power> is approximately $1.234\times10^{-20}\,\mathrm W$. These are leading small-particle estimates, not a measured air-<molecule> cross-section.

= Rayleigh scattering
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic radiation}
{wiki}

<Rayleigh scattering> is <elastic scattering> of <electromagnetic radiation> by particles much smaller than the incident <wavelength>. In the stationary particle approximation, the scattered <electromagnetic radiation> keeps the incident <frequency>: its direction changes, rather than its color. The incident <electric field> induces an oscillating <electric dipole moment>, which produces <electric dipole radiation>. This is a useful example of how a microscopic response can explain a familiar large-scale sight, <why the sky is blue>. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node108.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Rayleigh Scattering: bound-charge response and wavelength dependence]{ref}.

The smallness condition for <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere> of <radius> $a$ is $ka\ll1$, where the <wavenumber> is $k=2\pi/\lambda$ and $\lambda$ is the surrounding medium's <wavelength>. For ordinary materials with modest <refractive index>, the incident <electric field> then varies little across the particle, making the <electric-dipole approximation for radiation> useful. For very large <refractive index>, also check the internal size $|m|ka\ll1$ before applying the simple <electric dipole> model. Once these conditions fail, use <Mie scattering> for a <sphere> instead of extrapolating the <Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence>.

Historically, the 1871 paper https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Scientific_Papers/Volume_1/On_the_Light_from_the_Sky,_Its_Polarization_and_Colour[Rayleigh, On the Light from the Sky, Its Polarization and Colour: original-paper transcription]{ref} connected the color and <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> of scattered skylight with particles smaller than the <wavelength>. Its <dimensional analysis> anticipates scattered <light intensity> proportional to particle <volume> squared and to $\lambda^{-4}$. The original mechanical picture is historical; the modern explanation uses induced <electric dipole radiation>.

= Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$\sigma=\frac{8\pi}{3}k^4a^6\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2$}

= Rayleigh scattering cross-section
{c}
{synonym}

Consider a fixed, homogeneous, nonabsorbing, nonmagnetic <sphere> of <radius> $a$ in <vacuum>. Let $m$ be its relative <refractive index>, a fixed <real number>; in <vacuum> this equals the <sphere>'s <refractive index>. Treat $m$ as independent of <wavelength> over the range being compared, neglecting <optical dispersion>. Air can be approximated by <vacuum> here because its <refractive index> is close to one. These assumptions describe a toy particle, rather than a literal model of an individual air <molecule>.

The <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma$ is an effective <area>: the total scattered <power> is $P_{\rm sca}=I_{\rm inc}\sigma$, where $I_{\rm inc}$ is the incident <light intensity>. For $ka\ll1$ its leading approximation is
$$
\sigma=\frac{8\pi}{3}k^4a^6\left(\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}\right)^2,\qquad k=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda}.
$$
The units are <area>, since $k^4a^6$ has units $\mathrm{m}^{-4}\mathrm{m}^6=\mathrm{m}^2$. This <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> measures scattering, not conversion of the incident <electromagnetic radiation> into <heat>. The definition follows https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node107.html[Fitzpatrick, Thomson Scattering: total and differential scattering cross-sections]{ref}.

To see the mechanism, the nearly uniform applied <electric field> produces an <electric dipole moment> with peak magnitude
$$
p_0=4\pi\epsilon_0a^3\frac{m^2-1}{m^2+2}E_0,
$$
where $E_0$ is the incident <electric field> peak <wave amplitude> and $\epsilon_0$ is <vacuum permittivity>. This is the <sphere>'s response in <electrostatics>, with its <permittivity> divided by <vacuum permittivity> equal to $m^2$ under the nonmagnetic assumption. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node42.html[Fitzpatrick, Boundary Value Problems with Dielectrics, equation 554: induced sphere dipole]{ref}. The averaged <electric dipole radiation> <power> is $P_{\rm sca}=\omega^4p_0^2/(12\pi\epsilon_0c^3)$, with <angular frequency> $\omega=ck$ and <speed of light> $c$. Dividing by $I_{\rm inc}=\epsilon_0cE_0^2/2$ gives the displayed <Rayleigh scattering cross-section>. Thus doubling <radius> multiplies the <electric dipole moment> by eight and the scattered <power> by $64$, provided the larger <sphere> still satisfies $ka\ll1$. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/em/lectures/node95.html[Fitzpatrick, Electric dipole radiation: angular power and its integral]{ref}.

= Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$\sigma\propto\lambda^{-4}$}

For a fixed <sphere> and fixed <refractive index>, both the <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> and <scattering efficiency> are proportional to $\lambda^{-4}$. Halving <wavelength> multiplies either by $16$, as long as the shorter <wavelength> still satisfies $ka\ll1$. If <optical dispersion> changes $m$, the material factor changes too; the pure fourth-power comparison then needs correction.

For equal incident <light intensities> at blue <wavelength> $450\,\mathrm{nm}$ and red <wavelength> $650\,\mathrm{nm}$, the same toy <sphere> gives
$$
\frac{\sigma(450\,\mathrm{nm})}{\sigma(650\,\mathrm{nm})}=\left(\frac{650}{450}\right)^4=\frac{28561}{6561}\simeq4.35315.
$$
Blue <light> therefore produces about $4.35$ times the scattered <power>. This compares the particle's response at two <wavelengths>; it does not assert that an actual sky has this ratio of observed blue and red <light intensities>.

\Image[rayleigh-wavelength-scaling.png]
{title=<Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence> for a fixed small <sphere>, normalized at $650\,\mathrm{nm}$}
{description=The curve is $(650\,\mathrm{nm}/\lambda)^4$ over $350$ to $750\,\mathrm{nm}$. Blue and red comparison points are marked at $450$ and $650\,\mathrm{nm}$. Fixed <radius> and nondispersive <refractive index> are assumed, with $ka\ll1$ throughout. This is a particle response curve, not a simulated sky <optical spectrum>.}

= Rayleigh scattering angular pattern
{c}
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}
{title2=$F(\theta)=(1+\cos^2\theta)/2$}

For incident <unpolarized light> and the isotropic <electric dipole> response of <Rayleigh scattering by a small sphere>, let $\theta$ be the angle between the incident beam's propagation direction and the outgoing direction. The <differential scattering cross-section> and its relative angular pattern are
$$
\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\frac{3\sigma}{16\pi}(1+\cos^2\theta),\qquad F(\theta)=\frac{1+\cos^2\theta}{2},
$$
where $d\Omega$ is an element of <solid angle>. Averaging over the two transverse directions of incident <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> gives this pattern. Forward scattering at $0^\circ$ and backward scattering at $180^\circ$ are equally strong: $F=1$. Sideways scattering at $90^\circ$ is weakest, but nonzero: $F=1/2$. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/jk1/lectures/node107.html[Fitzpatrick, Thomson Scattering, equations 1283–1287: averaging the dipole angular pattern]{ref}.

\Image[rayleigh-angular-scattering.png]
{title=<Rayleigh scattering angular pattern> for incident <unpolarized light>, measured from the incident beam}
{description=The plotted radial coordinate is $F(\theta)$, a relative <differential scattering cross-section>, rather than the distance travelled by <light>. The incident propagation axis points right: $0^\circ$ is forward, $180^\circ$ backward, and $90^\circ$ sideways. Rotating this plane about the incident axis gives the three-dimensional pattern.}

The normalization is consistent because
$$
\int(1+\cos^2\theta)\,d\Omega=2\pi\int_0^\pi(1+\cos^2\theta)\sin\theta\,d\theta=\frac{16\pi}{3}.
$$
Thus integrating $d\sigma/d\Omega$ gives the total <Rayleigh scattering cross-section> $\sigma$. The plotted $F$ has maximum one; it is not itself normalized to unit <solid angle> integral.

For a single <electric dipole> driven by incident <linear polarization>, the <angular distribution of electric dipole radiation> instead has a $\sin^2\alpha$ factor, where $\alpha$ is measured from the <electric dipole moment> direction, parallel to the incident <electric field>. That direction is perpendicular to the incident beam. Confusing $\alpha$ with the beam angle $\theta$ would incorrectly predict zero forward scattering. Averaging the dipole patterns over incident <unpolarized light> yields the displayed $F(\theta)$.

= Why the sky is blue
{parent=Rayleigh scattering}

In a clear <terrestrial atmosphere>, <Rayleigh scattering> by air <molecules> redirects some sunlight toward an observer looking away from the <Sun>. Shorter <wavelengths> are preferentially scattered, enriching the scattered <light> in blue compared with the incoming sunlight. Along the longer atmospheric path to the low <Sun>, more short-<wavelength> <light> is removed from the direct beam, helping produce red sunsets. https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/[NASA Space Place, Why Is the Sky Blue?: scattered sunlight and red sunsets]{ref}.

Violet <light> has an even shorter <wavelength> than blue <light>, so the <Rayleigh scattering wavelength dependence> alone cannot predict the perceived sky color. The incoming solar <optical spectrum> and the eye's differing response to <wavelengths> both matter; human vision is less sensitive to violet than to blue. https://www.weather.gov/fgz/SkyBlue[NOAA National Weather Service, Why Is The Sky Blue?: solar spectrum and visual response]{ref}. <Multiple scattering> and other atmospheric particles also affect the observed sky. Cloud droplets are generally too large for this simple <Rayleigh scattering> model: <Mie scattering> describes spherical droplets, with much weaker visible <wavelength> selectivity, while repeated scattering helps make clouds appear white. https://www.giss.nasa.gov/edu/icp/research/ppa/1997/reyes/[NASA GISS, Aerosols in the Atmosphere: particle size, Mie scattering and white clouds]{ref}.

= Right-hand rule
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The right-hand rule fixes the orientation of a <cross product> and relates the circulation direction of an <electric current> to the direction of its <magnetic field>.

= Permittivity
{title2=$\epsilon$}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

<Permittivity> describes how an <electric field> and a material's <electric polarization> combine into the <electric displacement field>. In a static, local response with <linearity> and <isotropy>, and no <electric polarization> at zero <electric field>, the <constitutive equation> is
$$
\mathbf D=\epsilon\mathbf E,\qquad \epsilon=\epsilon_0\epsilon_r.
$$
Here $\epsilon_0$ is <vacuum permittivity>, while $\epsilon_r$ is the <relative permittivity>, a <dimensionless quantity>. The <SI> unit of <permittivity> is <farad> per metre, $\mathrm{F/m}$: multiplying it by an <electric field> in $\mathrm{V/m}$ gives an <electric displacement field> in $\mathrm{C/m^2}$. A larger positive <permittivity> lets the same <parallel-plate capacitor> hold more plate <electric charge> at the same <electric potential difference>.

The material contribution is <electric polarization> $\mathbf P$, the <electric dipole moment> per unit <volume>. The <electric displacement field> and <electric susceptibility> $\chi_e$ satisfy
$$
\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E+\mathbf P,\qquad
\mathbf P=\epsilon_0\chi_e\mathbf E,\qquad
\epsilon_r=1+\chi_e.
$$
Substitution gives the first <constitutive equation>. The <electric field> here is the averaged, total field inside the material, not just the field of the external plate <electric charges>. The <continuum approximation> averages over many <chemical atoms> or <molecules>; locality means that the <electric polarization> at a position depends on the <electric field> there. Static equilibrium, fixed material conditions and sufficiently small <electric fields> are needed for this scalar proportionality. <Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course by Richard Fitzpatrick/Polarization>{ref}.

For an ideal <parallel-plate capacitor> completely filled by a uniform <dielectric>, hold the free plate <electric charge> magnitude $Q$ fixed and neglect the <fringing field of a capacitor>. Writing $A$ for the plate <area>, <Gauss's law> gives $D=Q/A$. Thus
$$
E=\frac{Q}{\epsilon_0\epsilon_r A},\qquad
P=\frac{Q}{A}\left(1-\frac1{\epsilon_r}\right).
$$
The <electric dipoles> point along the <electric field>. Their surface <electric charges>, described by <polarization density>, oppose the field from the free plate <electric charges>. For a hypothetical <dielectric> with $\epsilon_r=4$, the internal <electric field> is one quarter of its <vacuum> value, while $P=3D/4$. At fixed <electric potential difference> instead, the internal <electric field> stays approximately $V/d$ and the plate <electric charge> grows fourfold. These are different experimental constraints. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_10.html#Ch10-S3[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.10–3, equations (10.5)–(10.11): polarization surface charge, field reduction and capacitance]{ref}.

\Image[dielectric-polarization-fixed-charge-fields.png]
{title=<Electric polarization> and <electric field> reduction at fixed plate <electric charge>}
{description=An original schematic and normalized model comparison for a uniform <dielectric> with <relative permittivity> four. The arrows for <electric dipole moments> run from negative to positive <electric charge>; the two field contributions oppose each other. The bar values compare <electric displacement field> $D$, $\epsilon_0E$, and <electric polarization> $P$, which all have units $\mathrm{C/m^2}$. The schematic is not to microscopic scale.}

<Permittivity> is a useful material summary, not a universal constant of the material under every condition. <Temperature>, <frequency> and <electric field> strength can change the response. In a <linear anisotropic dielectric>, <permittivity> is a <tensor>, $D_i=\sum_j\epsilon_{ij}E_j$, so the <electric displacement field> need not be parallel to the <electric field>. At nonzero <frequency>, delayed <electric polarization> requires <complex permittivity> and may produce <dielectric loss>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_32.html#Ch32-S2[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.32–2, equations (32.5)–(32.8) and following paragraph: frequency dependence and phase lag]{ref}.

= Dielectric
{parent=Permittivity}
{wiki}

A <dielectric> is a material in which an <electric field> produces <electric polarization> while transport of <electric charge> is sufficiently small for the intended use. An insulating solid in a <capacitor> is an example; real <dielectrics> can still have <dielectric loss> and nonzero <electrical conductivity>.

The important distinction is between rearranging bound <electric charge> and transporting mobile <charge carriers> through the specimen. Bound <electric charges> can move a little within a <chemical atom> or <molecule>, or an existing <electric dipole> can turn. Their averaged <electric dipole moments> produce <electric polarization> even if there is negligible steady <electric current> between the <electrodes>. A changing <electric polarization> does give a <bound current>; the absence of steady conduction does not mean that the material is electrically unresponsive.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 10: Dielectrics>[II.10–2 and II.10–3: induced atomic dipoles and relaxation of bound surface charge]{ref}

For uniform <electric polarization> $\mathbf P=P\widehat{\mathbf x}$ in a rectangular slab, <polarization density> gives zero bound volume <charge density> in the interior and opposite bound <surface charge densities> $-P,+P$ on the left and right faces. The <electric charge> has been redistributed, not created. More generally, the <divergence theorem> makes the cancellation explicit:
$$
\int_{\mathcal V}(-\nabla\cdot\mathbf P)\,d\mathcal V
+\int_{\partial\mathcal V}\mathbf P\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=0.
$$
Here $\mathbf n$ is the outward <normal vector> on the material boundary. This accounts for both bound volume <charge density> and bound <surface charge density>; omitting either can falsely suggest that an initially neutral <dielectric> acquired net <electric charge>.

An <electrical insulator> between <capacitor> plates supports a useful combination: appreciable <electric polarization>, little leakage <electric current>, and tolerance of the intended <electric field>. This lets a small <parallel-plate capacitor> store separated <electric charge> and recoverable <capacitor energy>. The comparison in <dielectric capacitor response> explains why specifying fixed <electric charge> or fixed <electric potential difference> changes the answer. High <relative permittivity> alone does not establish good insulation or a large usable <energy density>; <dielectric loss> and <dielectric breakdown> matter as well.

= Electric susceptibility
{parent=Dielectric}
{title2=$\chi_e$}
{wiki}

The <dimensionless quantity> relating induced <electric polarization> to the internal <electric field> through $\mathbf P=\epsilon_0\chi_e\mathbf E$ in the static scalar model. It describes the material contribution; <relative permittivity> includes the <vacuum> term as well, $\epsilon_r=1+\chi_e$. Zero <electric susceptibility> gives $\epsilon_r=1$, not zero <permittivity>.

The scalar $\chi_e$ assumes a local response with <linearity> and <isotropy>, settled <electric polarization>, fixed material conditions, and no residual <electric polarization> at zero <electric field>. It relates $\mathbf P$ to the averaged total internal <electric field>, which already includes the field of the bound <electric charges>. It does not relate $\mathbf P$ to the field the external <electrodes> would produce in <vacuum>. The distinction is essential for a self-consistent calculation.<Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course by Richard Fitzpatrick/Polarization>[equations (807)–(812): total field, displacement, linear susceptibility and relative dielectric constant]{ref}

Substitute the scalar relation into the definition of <electric displacement field>:
$$
\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E+\mathbf P
=\epsilon_0(1+\chi_e)\mathbf E.
$$
Comparison with $\mathbf D=\epsilon\mathbf E$ proves $\epsilon=\epsilon_0(1+\chi_e)$ and $\epsilon_r=1+\chi_e$. Both $\mathbf D$ and $\mathbf P$ have units $\mathrm{C/m^2}$, so $\chi_e$ is a <dimensionless quantity>. In a stipulated static model with $\epsilon_r=3$, $\chi_e=2$: at a prescribed internal <electric field>, the material contribution $P$ is twice $\epsilon_0E$. This is a hypothetical response, not a measured material constant. The <electric susceptibility> is a convenient bridge from <microscopic dielectric polarization> to a macroscopic <capacitance> measurement, but it is not a measure of <electrical conductivity>.

= Microscopic dielectric polarization
{parent=Dielectric}

Two routes to <electric polarization> are displacement and reorientation. An <electric field> can shift the centres of positive and negative <electric charge> in a neutral <chemical atom> or <molecule>, inducing an <electric dipole moment>. A <polar molecule> already has an <electric dipole moment>; a field biases its orientation. At finite <temperature>, random molecular motion opposes complete alignment, so a nonzero average need not mean that every <electric dipole> points the same way.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/8.5 Molecular Model of a Dielectric>[§8.5, Figures 8.19–8.20 and the thermal-fluctuation qualification following Figure 8.20]{ref}

For a single species with number per unit <volume> $n$, the <electric polarization> is
$$
\mathbf P=n\langle\mathbf p\rangle.
$$
The brackets denote the local average <electric dipole moment>, rather than its magnitude. Opposite orientations can cancel while individual <electric dipole moments> remain nonzero.<Classical Electromagnetism: An intermediate level course by Richard Fitzpatrick/Polarization>[equation (803): number density times locally averaged molecular dipole moment]{ref} This averaging explains how an initially unpolarized material can respond strongly without acquiring net <electric charge>.

\Image[dielectric-microscopic-displacement-and-orientation.png]
{title=Two microscopic routes to <electric polarization>}
{description=Original schematic: upper panels show coincident charge centres becoming separated; lower panels show existing <electric dipoles> changing from cancelling orientations to a biased distribution with incomplete alignment. Arrows run from negative to positive <electric charge>. Positions, displacements and orientations are illustrative, not measurements or a simulation of a particular substance.}

= Dielectric capacitor response
{parent=Dielectric}
{title2=$C=\epsilon_r C_0$}

Compare two initially identical <parallel-plate capacitors> with plate <area> $A$, fixed gap $d$, <vacuum> <capacitance> $C_0=\epsilon_0A/d$, and initial <electric potential difference> $V_0$. Completely fill both gaps with the same uniform linear <dielectric> of static <relative permittivity> $\epsilon_r>1$, neglecting the <fringing field of a capacitor>. Keep one connected to an ideal voltage source and isolate the other. The geometric <capacitance> becomes $C=\epsilon_r C_0$ in both cases; the electrical constraint determines what else changes.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/8.4 Capacitor with a Dielectric>[§8.4, Figure 8.17 and equation (8.11): isolated capacitor, unchanged free plate charge and increased capacitance]{ref}

Write $Q_0=C_0V_0$, $E_0=V_0/d$ and $U_0=C_0V_0^2/2$. Applying $Q=CV$, $E=V/d$ and the <capacitor energy> formula gives the complete comparison:
$$
\begin{array}{c|cc}
&\text{fixed voltage}&\text{isolated, fixed charge}\\ \hline
C/C_0&\epsilon_r&\epsilon_r\\
Q/Q_0&\epsilon_r&1\\
V/V_0&1&1/\epsilon_r\\
E/E_0&1&1/\epsilon_r\\
U/U_0&\epsilon_r&1/\epsilon_r
\end{array}
$$
Thus the statement that a <dielectric> reduces the <electric field> needs the fixed-free-charge condition. With fixed <electric potential difference>, the voltage source supplies additional free plate <electric charge>, maintaining $E=V_0/d$ despite the new bound <electric charges>.

\Image[dielectric-capacitor-voltage-and-charge-constraints.png]
{title=<Dielectric capacitor response> at fixed voltage and fixed charge}
{description=Original normalized comparison for the stipulated model $\epsilon_r=3$: every ratio is relative to the same initially charged <vacuum> <capacitor>. The dashed line marks the initial value. The bars show final values, not a time history or measured data. Plate geometry is unchanged and the <dielectric> completely fills the gap.}

For the stipulated $\epsilon_r=3$ example, the voltage source supplies $\Delta Q=2Q_0$. At constant $V_0$, its delivered electrical <work> is $V_0\Delta Q=4U_0$, whereas the increase in <capacitor energy> is $2U_0$. In an ideal reversible quasistatic insertion, the remaining $2U_0$ is mechanical <work> delivered by the assembly, as accounted for in <fixed-voltage capacitor energy balance>. For the isolated assembly, <capacitor energy> falls from $U_0$ to $U_0/3$, allowing $2U_0/3$ of mechanical <work> in the same ideal limit. Actual losses reduce recoverable <work>; energy increases in the connected case do not arise from the <dielectric> supplying free <energy>.

= Electrical insulator
{parent=Dielectric}
{wiki=Insulator_(electricity)}

An <electrical insulator> has sufficiently small transport of <electric charge> for its operating conditions. Insulation is a practical requirement involving <electric potential difference>, <temperature>, geometry and duration, rather than a universal claim of zero <electrical conductivity>. <Electric polarization> can be substantial in an <electrical insulator>; <dielectric breakdown> can end the insulating regime.

For an idealized uniform slab with small constant <electrical conductivity> $\sigma$, the <Ohmic conductor> model predicts leakage <electrical resistance> $R=d/(\sigma A)$ in parallel with <capacitance> $C=\epsilon A/d$. An isolated <capacitor> then has
$$
\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\frac{Q}{RC},\qquad
Q(t)=Q(0)e^{-t/(RC)},\qquad RC=\frac{\epsilon}{\sigma}.
$$
This is a derived model of slow discharge, not an assertion that every leakage mechanism obeys <Ohm's law>. Its time scale matches <charge relaxation>. The fixed-charge comparison in <dielectric capacitor response> needs an observation time short compared with $RC$, but long enough for <electric polarization> to settle. During a maintained voltage, leakage consumes <power> $V^2/R$ as <Joule heating>; delayed <electric polarization> can add <dielectric loss> under changing <electric fields>.<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Overview: real and imaginary permittivity, storage and dissipated loss]{ref}

Large <relative permittivity> alone does not rule out either mechanism.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/8.5 Molecular Model of a Dielectric>[§8.5, paragraph following Table 8.1: high dielectric constant does not ensure a good insulator; mobile ions permit conduction]{ref}

= Dielectric breakdown
{parent=Dielectric}
{wiki}

<Dielectric breakdown> is failure of an insulating regime under a sufficiently strong <electric field>, allowing substantial <electric current> through the material. For example, an <electron> freed from a <chemical atom> or <molecule> can become a mobile <charge carrier>. The small-field linear <electric susceptibility> model does not describe this conducting state.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/8.5 Molecular Model of a Dielectric>[§8.5, dielectric breakdown paragraph immediately before the definition of dielectric strength]{ref}

= Dielectric strength
{parent=Dielectric breakdown}
{title2=$E_{\rm bd}$}
{wiki}

<Dielectric strength> characterizes the <electric field> at <dielectric breakdown> under specified test conditions. In an ideal uniform gap, it sets a voltage scale $V_{\rm bd}\simeq E_{\rm bd}d$.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/8.5 Molecular Model of a Dielectric>[§8.5: dielectric strength definition and voltage limit for a specified plate separation]{ref} The <parallel-plate capacitor> model consequently gives the stored <energy density> scale
$$
u_{\rm bd}\simeq\frac12\epsilon E_{\rm bd}^2.
$$
This is a model scale, not an operating rating: <fringing field of a capacitor> make local <electric fields> differ from $V/d$, while leakage and <dielectric loss> spend some supplied <energy> as heat. Comparing usable <capacitor energy> therefore requires insulation and breakdown performance as well as <permittivity>.

= Complex permittivity
{parent=Permittivity}
{title2=$\epsilon^*(\omega)=\epsilon'(\omega)-i\epsilon''(\omega)$}

For harmonic <electric fields> with time convention $e^{i\omega t}$, write $\mathbf D=\epsilon^*(\omega)\mathbf E$ for their complex amplitudes, where $\omega$ is <angular frequency>. The <real part> $\epsilon'$ describes the in-phase <electric displacement field>; the minus <imaginary part> $\epsilon''$ describes the loss component. The sign reverses with the opposite time convention. <Complex permittivity> can also be a <tensor> in a <linear anisotropic dielectric>.

= Dielectric loss
{parent=Complex permittivity}
{wiki}

Conversion of electrical <energy> into heat in a <dielectric> driven by an <electric field>. In the stated harmonic convention, a passive scalar response has $\epsilon''\geq0$ for positive <angular frequency>. A <dielectric capacitance measurement> measures a dissipative component as well as <capacitance>; leakage from <electrical conductivity> can contribute to the inferred loss.

= Dielectric relaxation
{parent=Complex permittivity}

Delayed adjustment of <electric polarization> after a change in <electric field>. A simple illustrative model lets the slowly adjusting part $\mathbf P_s$ obey $\tau\,d\mathbf P_s/dt+\mathbf P_s=\epsilon_0\Delta\epsilon_r\mathbf E$, with a positive response time $\tau$. A harmonic <electric field> then gives a contribution $\Delta\epsilon_r/(1+i\omega\tau)$ to <complex permittivity> divided by <vacuum permittivity>. This model is an assumption, not a claim that all <dielectrics> have one response time.

= Dielectric capacitance measurement
{parent=Permittivity}

The motivating observation is that inserting an insulating material between fixed <capacitor> plates increases its <capacitance>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_10.html#Ch10-S1[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, II.10–1, opening paragraphs: observed increase in capacitance with an inserted insulator]{ref}. To investigate this response, use two flat parallel metal electrodes with measured overlapping <area> $A$ and separation $d$, a uniform specimen filling the gap, and an instrument that measures <electrical impedance>. Record the <capacitance> with and without the specimen at the same small applied <electric potential difference>, <frequency> and <temperature>, after <capacitance measurement calibration>. An increase in corrected <capacitance> indicates a larger <permittivity>, provided the geometry is unchanged. A guard electrode reduces the contribution of the <fringing field of a capacitor>. <Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>{ref}.

For negligible <dielectric loss> in the <parallel-plate capacitor> approximation,
$$
C=\frac{\epsilon A}{d},\qquad C_0=\frac{\epsilon_0A}{d},\qquad
\epsilon_r=\frac{C}{C_0}=\frac{Cd}{\epsilon_0A}.
$$
An air reference approximates $C_0$; it is not an exact <vacuum> reference. For the following synthetic example, stipulate $A=0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$, $d=1.00\,\mathrm{mm}$ and the rounded <vacuum permittivity> $\epsilon_0\approx8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$. Then $C_0=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$ in this model, and <relative permittivity> four gives $C=354.16\,\mathrm{pF}$. A residual parallel <capacitance> of $20\,\mathrm{pF}$ would make an uncorrected sample/reference ratio $374.16/108.54\approx3.447$, rather than four. This additive error does not cancel in a ratio.

\Image[synthetic-dielectric-capacitance-frequency-response.png]
{title=Synthetic <capacitance> response to <relative permittivity> and <frequency>}
{description=Left: the ideal scalar <capacitance> and a hypothetical additive stray <capacitance> of $20\,\mathrm{pF}$. Right: a hypothetical <dielectric relaxation> model, $\epsilon_r^*=2+2/(1+if/f_0)$ with $f_0=1\,\mathrm{kHz}$, for the same geometry. The solid curve is the parallel <capacitance> $C_p=C_0\epsilon_r'$; the dashed curve is the loss component $C_0\epsilon_r''$, not an independently measured storage <capacitance>. Every plotted value is synthetic.}

At nonzero <frequency> $f$, measure both magnitude and <phase> of the <electric current> relative to the applied <electric potential difference>. Define $Y=I/V=1/Z$, where $Z$ is <electrical impedance>. For the stated <complex permittivity> convention, the ideal uniform specimen gives
$$
Y=i\omega C_0\epsilon_r^*=\omega C_0\epsilon_r''+i\omega C_0\epsilon_r',\qquad
C_p=\frac{\operatorname{Im}Y}{\omega},\qquad \omega=2\pi f.
$$
Thus a parallel <capacitor> and <electrical conductance> describe the readout at that <frequency>; a single scalar <capacitance> cannot describe both components. The diagram's assumed <dielectric relaxation> gives $C_p=265.62\,\mathrm{pF}$ and $C_0\epsilon_r''=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$ at $1\,\mathrm{kHz}$. These are model predictions, not observations.

Repeat the reference measurement after removing the specimen and vary the small applied <electric potential difference> to test drift and <linearity>. Record specimen thickness, electrode <area>, contact conditions, <frequency> and <temperature>. Air gaps act as additional layers in series and can lower inferred <relative permittivity>; roughness, uncertain thickness and the <fringing field of a capacitor> limit the geometric formula. At sufficiently high <frequency>, the fixture itself changes the <electromagnetic field>, so low-<frequency> guarding and a lumped <capacitor> model require reassessment. <Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>{ref}.

= Capacitance measurement calibration
{parent=Dielectric capacitance measurement}

Characterize the instrument and fixture before inferring <permittivity>: use the prescribed open and short measurements to compensate residual parallel response and series <electrical impedance>, and check a known reference <capacitor> or the required load standard. Keep cables and electrode geometry reproducible. An open fixture measurement is distinct from the empty-gap reference <capacitor>; blindly subtracting its entire <capacitance> would remove the desired reference signal. Compensation cannot determine an incorrectly measured specimen thickness or eliminate all specimen-dependent <fringing field of a capacitor> errors. <Open and Short Compensation and Z Port Extension>{ref}.

= Foil capacitor dielectric experiment
{parent=Dielectric capacitance measurement}

Southern Methodist University's teaching lab, Introduction to the Parallel Plate Capacitor Lab, makes the response tangible with a <capacitor> assembled from aluminium foil, paper and taped wire contacts. Its instructions specify approximately $10\,\mathrm{cm}\times10\,\mathrm{cm}$ foil rectangles, aligned with the paper completely separating them. A <multimeter> in <capacitance> mode supplies the readout; a ruler measures plate dimensions and a micrometer measures specimen thickness. The lab replaces the paper with other sheets, including cardstock, plastic wrap and wax paper. It asks students to keep foil <area> and alignment consistent, measure thickness at several positions, estimate thickness uncertainty, and wait for the <capacitance> reading to stabilize. These are published preparation and measurement instructions, not measured results reported here.<SMU Physics 110x labs/Introduction to the Parallel Plate Capacitor Lab>[Experiments 1–2, Equipment You Will Be Using, and final construction and dielectric-study instructions]{ref}

The inference uses each specimen's own thickness $t$, not just a comparison of raw meter readings. With corrected <capacitance> $C_s$ and overlapping <area> $A$, the ideal estimate is $\epsilon_r=C_s t/(\epsilon_0A)$. A thicker sheet can give a smaller <capacitance> despite larger <relative permittivity>. This is why changing material while letting the plate gap change does not isolate <electric susceptibility> by itself.

Useful additional controls are to keep leads in the same positions, use <capacitance measurement calibration>, repeat one sheet after the others to check drift, and repeat the assembly to assess contact reproducibility. An air-gap reference must have known spacers maintaining the same separation; removing a supporting sheet and letting the foil touch is not such a reference. Record the instrument's test <frequency> and measurement mode when available. A <capacitance> display alone does not establish negligible <dielectric loss> or a strictly static <permittivity>. These are proposed controls extending the published teaching instructions.

Air gaps produce a particularly revealing systematic error. Suppose a uniform sheet of thickness $t$ and <relative permittivity> $\epsilon_r$ is separated from the <electrodes> by air layers of total thickness $g$. Approximate air <permittivity> by <vacuum permittivity>, neglect the <fringing field of a capacitor> and use the same <area> in each layer. The common normal <electric displacement field> has magnitude $D=Q/A$, so integrating the <electric field> through the layers gives
$$
V=\frac{Q}{\epsilon_0A}\left(g+\frac{t}{\epsilon_r}\right),\qquad
C_s=\frac{\epsilon_0A}{g+t/\epsilon_r}.
$$
If the analyst inserts the sheet thickness alone into the ideal filled-gap formula, the inferred value is
$$
\widehat\epsilon_r=\frac{C_s t}{\epsilon_0A}
=\frac{\epsilon_r}{1+\epsilon_r g/t}.
$$
For the stipulated model $\epsilon_r=3$ and $g=t/10$, this gives $\widehat\epsilon_r=30/13\simeq2.308$, not three. These values are calculated examples, not measured sheet properties. If total plate separation $d=t+g$ is used instead, the effective estimate is $(t+g)/(g+t/\epsilon_r)$; it still mixes the sheet and the air. Thus measuring a thickness accurately cannot compensate for an unrecognized layer.

The <fringing field of a capacitor> and additive lead <capacitance> further limit a foil measurement. Keep lateral plate dimensions large relative to separation, and use a <guard electrode of a capacitor> in a suitable fixture for more demanding work.<Materials Measurement: Dielectric Materials>[Solution: guarded three-electrode fixture, fringe capacitance, specimen flatness and air gaps]{ref} A family of thickness measurements can help distinguish material response from an additive offset, but air gaps can vary with the assembly. A large, repeatable reading supports a <capacitance> change; extracting a material <electric susceptibility> requires the geometry, gaps, instrument response and <dielectric loss> assumptions to survive these checks. No measured <relative permittivity> is claimed for any of the lab's sheets.

= Vacuum permittivity
{title2=$\epsilon_0$}
{parent=Permittivity}
{wiki}

<Vacuum permittivity> is the electromagnetic constant $\epsilon_0$ in <Coulomb's law> and the <vacuum Maxwell equations>. In classical <vacuum>, $\mathbf P=0$ and the <electric displacement field> is $\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E$. A material's <permittivity> includes its <electric polarization> response; <vacuum permittivity> supplies the common reference, not a separate adjustable material property. Its <SI> unit is <farad> per metre.

For elementary calculations, <vacuum permittivity> is approximately $8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$. This is a deliberately rounded value, not an exact decimal or a statement of current measurement precision.<Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>[2022 CODATA adjustment, vacuum electric permittivity row: value and nonzero standard uncertainty]{ref} It sets the scale in <Coulomb's law>, $F=|q_1q_2|/(4\pi\epsilon_0r^2)$ for two stationary point <electric charges> with separation <length> $r$ in <vacuum>, and therefore enters the reference <capacitance> $C_0=\epsilon_0A/d$ of a <parallel-plate capacitor>.

Since the revised <SI> took effect on 20 May 2019, the numerical value of <vacuum permittivity> in <SI> units is determined experimentally, rather than fixed exactly by the unit definitions. The numerical values of the <elementary charge> $e$, <Planck constant> $h$ and <speed of light> $c$ are fixed exactly. The <fine-structure constant> $\alpha$ is measured, and
$$
\alpha=\frac{e^2}{2\epsilon_0hc},\qquad
\epsilon_0=\frac{e^2}{2\alpha hc},\qquad
\epsilon_0\mu_0=\frac1{c^2},
$$
where $\mu_0$ is <vacuum permeability>. Consequently <vacuum permittivity> and <vacuum permeability> have experimental uncertainty, while their product is exact in <SI> units because $c$ is exact. This is a change in metrological status, not a new material dependence of <vacuum permittivity>.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[pp. 123–125 (defining constants and alpha relation); section 2.3.1, printed p. 128 (ampere, epsilon0 and mu0); 26th CGPM Resolution 1, printed p. 194 (20 May 2019 effective date)]{ref}

\Include[electric-charge]{parent=Electromagnetism}

= Electric field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The electric field is the <force> per unit charge on a stationary test particle.

= Electric force
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\mathbf F_E=q\mathbf E$}

The <electric force> on an <electric charge> $q$ is $\mathbf F_E=q\mathbf E$, using the <electric field> of the other sources and excluding the charge's own self-field. Positive <electric charge> gives <force> along the <electric field>; negative <electric charge> reverses it. A field $\mathbf E=400\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ therefore gives $\mathbf F_E=-8\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm N\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ on $q=-2\,\mathrm{nC}$. This is the electric part of the <Lorentz force>; the magnetic contribution is $q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-4-electric-field[OpenStax, University Physics II, §5.4: electric field and force]{ref}.

= Electric flux
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\Phi_E=\int_S E\cdot n\,dS$}
{wiki}

<Electric flux> measures how much <electric field> crosses a chosen <oriented surface>, with a sign distinguishing the two crossing directions. A strong <electric field> running along a surface contributes nothing to its <electric flux>: only the <normal component> counts. This lets <Gauss's law> turn a potentially complicated <electric field> into one number recording the net enclosed <electric charge>.

For a surface $S$ with chosen <unit normal> $\mathbf n$, the <electric flux> is the <surface integral>
$$
\Phi_E(S)=\int_S\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=\int_S\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf S,\qquad d\mathbf S=\mathbf n\,dA.
$$
Here $dA$ is positive <surface area>, while $d\mathbf S$ is the <vector area element>. The <dot product> makes <electric flux> a signed <scalar>, rather than a <vector> like the <electric field>. Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$: <electric field> measured using the <newton (unit)> per <coulomb>, multiplied by <surface area> in square metres. Reversing the <orientation of a surface> replaces $\mathbf n$ by $-\mathbf n$ and reverses the <electric flux>. Calling it <electric flux> does not imply that <electric charge> is moving through the surface. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_03.html#Ch3-S2[Feynman lectures II.3, §3–2: flux of a vector field]{ref}.

For an open patch in a <plane>, with <surface area> $A$ and a constant <electric field>, the <unit normal> is constant and the <surface integral> simplifies to
$$
\Phi_E=\mathbf E\cdot(A\mathbf n)=EA\cos\theta,\qquad E=|\mathbf E|.
$$
The <angle> $\theta$ is between the <electric field> and the chosen <unit normal>, not between the <electric field> and the <plane>. The <cosine> describes the signed projected <surface area> seen by the <electric field>. Thus <electric flux> is positive for $0\leq\theta<90^\circ$, zero at $90^\circ$, and negative for $90^\circ<\theta\leq180^\circ$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-1-electric-flux[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §6.1: electric flux]{ref}.

For example, take $E=100\,\mathrm{N/C}$ and $A=0.02\,\mathrm{m^2}$, so $EA=2\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$. Three choices of <angle> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Phi_E(0^\circ)&=2\cos0^\circ=2\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C},\\
\Phi_E(60^\circ)&=2\cos60^\circ=1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C},\\
\Phi_E(120^\circ)&=2\cos120^\circ=-1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Keeping the $60^\circ$ patch fixed and reversing its <unit normal> gives $120^\circ$ and changes $+1$ to $-1\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$. The <electric field> and physical patch have not changed. If the <electric field> is instead tangent to the patch, its <dot product> with the <unit normal> vanishes: <electric flux> is zero even though $E=100\,\mathrm{N/C}$.

\Image[open-surface-electric-flux.png]
{title=Three open patches in a <plane> in a constant <electric field>: the <angle> to the chosen <unit normal> gives <electric fluxes> of 2, 1 and minus 1 newton square metres per <coulomb>}

For a <closed surface> enclosing a volume $V$, choose the outward <unit normal>. <Gauss's law> gives
$$
\Phi_E(\partial V)=\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dA=\frac{Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}}{\varepsilon_0}.
$$
Here $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>, and $Q_{\mathrm{enclosed}}$ is the signed total <electric charge> inside. The <electric field> in the <surface integral> includes all sources, including external <electric charges>. Assume no ideal <point charge> lies on the integration surface. Zero enclosed <electric charge> implies zero total <electric flux>; it does **not** imply zero <electric field> everywhere on the <closed surface> or inside it. Positive and negative contributions to the <surface integral> can cancel. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/6-2-explaining-gausss-law[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §6.2: outward flux and enclosed charge]{ref}.

To see the cancellation with sources inside, place <point charges> $+q$ at $(-a,0,0)$ and $-q$ at $(a,0,0)$, where $q>0$, $a=0.30\,\mathrm m$, and both lie inside the <sphere> of radius $R=1\,\mathrm m$ centered at the origin. This separated pair has the <electric dipole> geometry. Its enclosed <electric charge> is $q-q=0$, so its total outward <electric flux> is zero. At the point $P=(0,0,R)$ on the <sphere>, <Coulomb's law> and the <superposition principle> give the individual <electric fields>
$$
\mathbf E_+(P)=\frac{kq(a,0,R)}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}},\qquad
\mathbf E_-(P)=\frac{kq(a,0,-R)}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}},\qquad k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}.
$$
The $z$ components cancel and the positive $x$ components add:
$$
\mathbf E(P)=\frac{2kqa}{(R^2+a^2)^{3/2}}\widehat{\mathbf x}\approx0.527244\frac{kq}{R^2}\widehat{\mathbf x}\ne\mathbf0.
$$
For the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and $q=1\,\mathrm{nC}$, this is about $4.740\,\mathrm{N/C}$ along positive $x$. At $P$, the outward <unit normal> is $\widehat{\mathbf z}$, so this nonzero <electric field> is tangent to the <sphere>. At other points of the <sphere>, its <normal component> can be positive or negative. The source-field formula applies away from the two <point charges>, where the <electric field> has zero <divergence>; it is singular at the sources. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_04.html#Ch4-S2[Feynman lectures II.4, §§4–1 and 4–2: electrostatic sources and superposition]{ref}.

\Image[zero-net-flux-nonzero-field.png]
{title=Cross-section of the three-dimensional <electric field> of two opposite <point charges> inside a unit-radius <sphere>: zero total <electric flux> coexists with a nonzero tangential <electric field> at the top}

The diagram shows <streamlines> of the three-dimensional <electric field> in the section $y=0$. The circle is a cross-section of the <sphere>, not the integration surface: <Gauss's law> uses the entire three-dimensional <surface integral>. A two-dimensional <line integral> of outward field around that circle would be a different quantity, with units $\mathrm{N\,m/C}$ rather than the <electric flux> units $\mathrm{N\,m^2/C}$.

= Electric potential
{parent=Electric field}
{title2=$\Phi$}
{wiki}

= Electrostatic potential
{synonym}

In <electrostatics>, the <electric potential> $\Phi$ tells us the interaction <potential energy> per unit test <electric charge> in the <electric field> of prescribed sources. Its most directly useful quantity is an <electric potential difference>: how much <work> per unit <electric charge> an external agent must supply to move that test <electric charge> slowly between two points. This replaces the bookkeeping of a <vector> <force> along a path with the difference of two <scalar> values. The test <electric charge> must be small enough not to rearrange the sources appreciably.

For motion from $A$ to $B$, write $\Delta\Phi=\Phi(B)-\Phi(A)$. With fixed sources and only the <electric force> doing <work>,
$$
\Delta\Phi=-\int_A^B\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l,\qquad
\Delta U=q\Delta\Phi,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=-q\Delta\Phi,\qquad
\Delta K=-\Delta U.
$$
Here $U$ is interaction <potential energy>, $K$ is <kinetic energy>, and the <line integral> uses the <electric field> produced by the other <electric charges>. For slow transport with unchanged <kinetic energy>, the external <work> is $W_{\mathrm{ext}}=\Delta U$, so $\Delta\Phi=W_{\mathrm{ext}}/q$ for nonzero $q$. This external <work> has the opposite sign to the <work> done by the <electric field>. The <SI> unit is the <volt>,
$$
1\,\mathrm V=1\,\mathrm{J/C}.
$$
A <volt> measures <energy> per unit <electric charge>, not <energy> itself. For example, crossing an <electric potential difference> of $+5\,\mathrm V$ changes the <potential energy> of $q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ by $+10\,\mathrm{nJ}$, but changes that of $q=-2\,\mathrm{nC}$ by $-10\,\mathrm{nJ}$. Both test <electric charges> encounter the same <electric potential difference>; their opposite signs reverse the <energy> change.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.2 Electric Potential and Potential Difference>[§7.2, equations 7.4–7.7: energy per charge, work and potential difference]{ref}

The <electrostatic potential> is a <scalar> <function> of position. The <electric field> is a <conservative vector field>, and all three components follow from the negative <gradient> of the <electric potential>:
$$
\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi,\qquad E_x=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial x},\quad E_y=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial y},\quad E_z=-\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial z}.
$$
The <electric field> points toward decreasing <electric potential>. A positive test <electric charge> feels <electric force> in that direction; a negative test <electric charge> feels <electric force> in the opposite direction, since $\mathbf F=q\mathbf E$. A freely accelerated negative test <electric charge> therefore moves toward higher <electric potential> while lowering its <potential energy> $U=q\Phi$.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.4 Determining Field from Potential>[§7.4, equations 7.13–7.15: components and negative gradient]{ref}

Only differences fix physical <electrostatic potentials>: adding the same constant $C_0$ everywhere, $\Phi'=\Phi+C_0$, changes neither the <gradient> nor any <electric potential difference>. Choosing an <electrical reference potential> is this elementary <gauge transformation>. Setting zero at infinity is convenient for finitely many localized <point charges>; it is not available for an ideal uniform <electric field> extending to infinity. There we can choose a finite reference point. Assigning zero at ground is a convention, whereas physically grounding an <electrical conductor> also allows exchange of <electric charge> to impose a boundary value. Zero <electric potential> at a point never by itself implies zero <electric field>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.3 Calculations of Electric Potential>[§7.3, equation 7.8 and Figure 7.18: infinity and ground references]{ref}

For a fixed <point charge> $Q$ at the origin in <vacuum>, choose zero at infinity. <Coulomb's law> gives
$$
\Phi(r)=\frac{kQ}{r},\qquad
\mathbf E(r)=\frac{kQ}{r^2}\widehat{\mathbf r},\qquad
k=\frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0},\qquad r>0,
$$
where $\varepsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>. Use the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and $Q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$. At $r=0.20\,\mathrm m$,
$$
\Phi=89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad E_r=449.5\,\mathrm{N/C}.
$$
A test <electric charge> $q=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ has $U=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$ and radial <electric force> $F_r=-1.3485\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm N$, directed inward. Moving it inward from $r_A=0.20\,\mathrm m$ to $r_B=0.10\,\mathrm m$ raises the <electric potential> from $89.9$ to $179.8\,\mathrm V$ but lowers its <potential energy>:
$$
\Delta\Phi=+89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Delta U=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=+2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J.
$$
This is the <Coulomb potential energy> calculation using <electric potentials>. The source's singular self-field at $r=0$ is excluded; when finding the <force> on a source <point charge>, use only the <electric field> of the other sources.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.3 Calculations of Electric Potential>[§7.3: point-charge potential and scalar superposition]{ref}

For a uniform <electric field> $\mathbf E=300\,\mathrm{V/m}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$, choose $\Phi(0)=12\,\mathrm V$. Then
$$
\Phi(x)=12\,\mathrm V-(300\,\mathrm{V/m})x,\qquad
\Delta\Phi=-\mathbf E\cdot(\mathbf r_B-\mathbf r_A).
$$
Moving from $x_A=0$ to $x_B=0.04\,\mathrm m$ gives $\Delta\Phi=-12\,\mathrm V$. For $q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ the change in <potential energy> is $-24\,\mathrm{nJ}$ and the <electric field> does $+24\,\mathrm{nJ}$ of <work>. For $q=-2\,\mathrm{nC}$, the same prescribed displacement gives $\Delta U=+24\,\mathrm{nJ}$: the external agent must supply that <work> for slow transport. This uniform <electric field> approximates the interior of a <parallel-plate capacitor> sufficiently far from the edges; the <fringing field of a capacitor> limits the approximation.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.4 Determining Field from Potential>[§7.4: voltage gradient in a uniform field]{ref}

\Image[electric-potential-work-and-equipotentials.png]
{id=electric-potential-work-and-equipotentials}
{title=Analytic <electric potential> models: <point charge> <equipotentials>, a uniform <electric field>, and the sign of the change in <potential energy>}

An <equipotential> is a <level set> $\Phi=\text{constant}$. On a smooth <equipotential> with nonzero <gradient>, every tangent displacement has $d\Phi=\nabla\Phi\cdot d\mathbf l=0$, so the <electric field> is normal to the <equipotential>. Thus moving a test <electric charge> along an <equipotential> requires no net electrical <work>. Around a <point charge>, the <equipotentials> are <spheres>; the circles in the diagram are their sections, not an assumption of two-dimensional <Coulomb's law>. A uniform <electric field> has parallel <plane> <equipotentials>. For contours separated by the same small <electric potential difference>, smaller normal separation means a stronger <electric field>, approximately $|\mathbf E|\approx|\Delta\Phi|/\Delta n$. At a point where $\nabla\Phi=0$, the <electric field> is zero and has no direction to call normal.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.5 Equipotential Surfaces and Conductors>[§7.5, Figures 7.30–7.32: equipotentials, perpendicular field and zero work]{ref}

The <superposition principle> adds <electric potentials> as signed <scalars>. For finitely many stationary <point charges> $q_j$ at $\mathbf r_j$ in <vacuum>,
$$
\Phi(\mathbf r)=k\sum_j\frac{q_j}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j|},\qquad
\mathbf E(\mathbf r)=k\sum_jq_j\frac{\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j}{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_j|^3}.
$$
For example, the <electric dipole> with $+q$ at $(a,0,0)$ and $-q$ at $(-a,0,0)$ has zero <electric potential> everywhere in the midplane $x=0$, yet its <electric field> at the midpoint is $\mathbf E=-(2kq/a^2)\widehat{\mathbf x}$. With $q=2\,\mathrm{nC}$ and $a=0.10\,\mathrm m$, this is $-3596\,\mathrm{N/C}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$. At $(2a,0,0)$, the same model gives $\Phi=2kq/(3a)\approx119.867\,\mathrm V$ and $E_x=8kq/(9a^2)\approx1598.222\,\mathrm{N/C}$. Cancellation of <electric potentials> is quite different from cancellation of <vector> <electric fields>.

\Image[electrostatic-dipole-potential.png]
{title=Computed dipole equipotentials and electric-field directions: zero potential on the vertical midline still permits a nonzero field}

A motivating measurement is <conductive-paper electric potential mapping>. A documented teaching experiment uses slightly conductive paper on an insulating board, two circular <electrodes> in highly conducting ink, a steady supply, and a digital <voltmeter>. Connect the supply to the two <electrodes>, keep the <voltmeter> common lead on the negative <electrode>, and use its other lead as a movable probe. Adjust the supply using the measured <electric potential difference> between <electrodes>; the published procedure uses $10\,\mathrm V$. The observable is the <voltmeter> reading $\Phi(P)-\Phi(\mathrm{COM})$ at the probe position $P$. Locate several points reading $2\,\mathrm V$, record their positions on a separate map, and join them into an <equipotential>; repeat at other readings. Lift the probe between contacts so it does not damage the paper. This procedure makes the <electrical reference potential> concrete: every reading compares two contacts.<Physics 2212 laboratory manual by Eric Murray/Physics 2212, Lab 5: Electric Potential and Field>{ref}

From neighboring readings infer an <electric field> component by a <finite difference>, $E_x\approx-[\Phi(x+h)-\Phi(x)]/h$. Reconstruct two in-plane components with displacements along two independent directions. Alternatively, draw the <electric field> normal to mapped <equipotentials>, toward lower <electric potential>. The second diagram illustrates the same measurement principle with bar <electrodes> spanning a uniform sheet, insulating top and bottom edges, and a synthetic model $\Phi(x)=(50\,\mathrm{V/m})x$. The model readings $4.0\,\mathrm V$ at $x=0.08\,\mathrm m$ and $5.0\,\mathrm V$ at $x=0.10\,\mathrm m$ imply $E_x=-50\,\mathrm{V/m}$. These values are calculated illustrations, not reported experimental data. The documented circular-<electrode> experiment similarly estimates the <electric field> from readings at successive centimetre marks.<Physics 2212 laboratory manual by Eric Murray/Physics 2212, Lab 5: Electric Potential and Field>{ref}

\Image[electric-potential-voltmeter-mapping.png]
{id=electric-potential-voltmeter-mapping}
{title=<Voltmeter> setup for <conductive-paper electric potential mapping> and a synthetic profile whose negative <derivative> gives the <electric field>}

Useful controls include checking the <voltmeter> zero, checking that the <electric potential difference> along each painted <electrode> is negligible, and repeating reference readings to detect supply drift. Reversing the supply should reverse the signs relative to the same reference contact. Contact resistance, leakage <electric currents>, and touching the sheet can distort the map. A <voltmeter> has finite input <electrical resistance> and draws a small <electric current>; <voltmeter loading> changes the quantity being measured if that <electrical resistance> is insufficiently large relative to the sheet's effective source <electrical resistance>.<Electricity and Magnetism laboratory manual/EC-2: Electric Fields>{ref}<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/10.4 Electrical Measuring Instruments>[§10.4, Measuring Voltage with a Voltmeter: parallel connection and large input resistance]{ref}

The paper carries a steady <electric current>, so this is a planar analogue rather than a direct measurement of isolated <point charges> in three-dimensional <vacuum>. For constant <electrical conductivity>, <Ohm's law> and steady <charge conservation> give $\mathbf j=-\sigma\nabla\Phi$ and $\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$, hence the two-dimensional <Laplace equation> away from <electrodes>. Insulating sheet edges impose zero normal <electric current>, while finite <electrodes> fix boundary values. This derivation explains both the analogy and its limits: inhomogeneous paper, finite boundaries, and finite <electrodes> can change the contours. One should not fit the three-dimensional <point charge> formula $kQ/r$ to a planar map without testing that model. Small probe spacing resolves spatial variation better, but amplifies uncertainty in the inferred <finite difference>. The published laboratory instructions explicitly ask students to assess whether their proposed field fit is appropriate.<Electricity and Magnetism laboratory manual/EC-2: Electric Fields>{ref}<Physics 2212 laboratory manual by Eric Murray/Physics 2212, Lab 5: Electric Potential and Field>{ref}

The scalar description has a crucial limit. <Faraday's law of induction> for a stationary closed path $\Gamma$ says
$$
\oint_\Gamma\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l=-\frac{d\Phi_B}{dt},\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\frac{\partial\mathbf B}{\partial t},
$$
where $\Phi_B$ is <magnetic flux>. Nonzero changing <magnetic flux> gives nonzero circulation, incompatible with a single-valued <electric potential> alone. More generally, a scalar <electric potential> and a <magnetic vector potential> together give
$$
\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi-\frac{\partial\mathbf A}{\partial t},\qquad
\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A.
$$
Consequently, in the presence of induction, two <voltmeter> lead routes between the same contacts can produce different readings if the enclosed <magnetic flux> changes. This follows by subtracting the two path <line integrals>; it is a deduction from <Faraday's law of induction>, not a claimed outcome of the paper experiment. The steady setup and negligible induction justify interpreting its readings as <electric potential differences>. Merely introducing a scalar <electric potential> that depends on <time> cannot supply a nonzero <curl> of the <electric field>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 21: Solutions of Maxwell’s Equations with Currents and Charges>[§21–3]{ref}

A short mathematical justification connects the statements above. Along a smooth path, the <chain rule> gives $d\Phi=\nabla\Phi\cdot d\mathbf l$. Integrating and using $\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi$ yields the endpoint formula by the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>, proving <path independence>. Conversely, vanishing circulation around every closed path lets us define $\Phi(P)=-\int_{P_0}^{P}\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l$ independently of the route, with $\Phi(P_0)=0$. Zero <curl> is a local test; for a continuously differentiable <curl-free vector field>, a <simply connected> open region suffices for a global potential. The explicit finite-source formula already gives a global <electric potential> away from its source points.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 4: Electrostatics>{ref}

= Voltmeter
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

A <voltmeter> measures an <electric potential difference> between two contacts; its finite input <electrical resistance> can cause <voltmeter loading>.

= Voltmeter loading
{parent=Voltmeter}

<Voltmeter loading> is the change in an <electric potential difference> caused by the <electric current> drawn by the measuring <voltmeter>.

For a <loaded voltage divider> in steady <direct current>, a <voltmeter> connected across the lower <resistor> places its finite input <electrical resistance> $R_m$ in parallel with the lower arm's <electrical resistance> $R_2$ and any intended <electrical load> of <electrical resistance> $R_L$. With positive, fixed <electrical resistances>, an upper <resistor> of <electrical resistance> $R_1$, and an ideal <voltage source> supplying <electric potential difference> $V_{\mathrm{in}}$, <Kirchhoff's current law> and <Ohm's law> give
$$
\frac{V_{\mathrm{in}}-V_m}{R_1}=\frac{V_m}{R_2}+\frac{V_m}{R_L}+\frac{V_m}{R_m},\qquad
R_p=\left(\frac1{R_2}+\frac1{R_L}+\frac1{R_m}\right)^{-1},\qquad
V_m=V_{\mathrm{in}}\frac{R_p}{R_1+R_p}.
$$
Here $V_m$ is the connected circuit's <electric potential difference> across the <voltmeter>; omit $1/R_L$ if there is no intended <electrical load>. The parallel connection and need for large input <electrical resistance> are standard <voltmeter> operating principles.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/10.4 Electrical Measuring Instruments>[§10.4, Measuring Voltage with a Voltmeter: parallel connection and large input resistance]{ref}

<Voltmeter loading> is a bias relative to the target <electrical circuit> with the <voltmeter> absent and any intended <electrical load> still present. <Voltage divider loading error> instead compares the attached-load output with the wholly unloaded output. An accurate readout of $V_m$, supported by <measurement calibration>, can still differ from either target: calibration accuracy concerns the indication of the connected <electric potential difference>, whereas <voltmeter loading> changes that <electric potential difference> itself.

= Conductive-paper electric potential mapping
{parent=Electric potential}

<Conductive-paper electric potential mapping> uses a <voltmeter> to locate <equipotentials> on a resistive sheet, then infers the in-plane <electric field> from the measured <electric potential differences>.

= Electrical reference potential
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$\Phi_{\mathrm{ref}}=0$}

An <electrical reference potential> fixes the arbitrary additive constant of an <electric potential>. Assigning zero to ground makes other potentials differences from that baseline; it does not assert that the <electric field> vanishes there. If a source calculation gives $\Phi(a)=89.9\,\mathrm V$ and $\Phi(b)=179.8\,\mathrm V$ relative to infinity, choosing $a$ as the zero instead gives $\Phi'(a)=0$ and $\Phi'(b)=89.9\,\mathrm V$, with the same <electric potential difference>. Grounding an <electrical conductor> additionally connects it to a reservoir that can exchange <electric charge> to maintain the prescribed <electric potential>; that physical boundary condition differs from merely relabeling an unchanged solution.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.3 Calculations of Electric Potential>[§7.3: potential references, including ground in Figure 7.18]{ref}; https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_06.html#Ch6-S8[Feynman lectures II.6, §6–8: a grounded conducting plane]{ref}.

= Constrained electrostatic equilibrium
{parent=Electric potential}

A particle constrained to a surface can have a stable minimum of <electrostatic potential> restricted to that surface even when the unrestricted equilibrium is unstable. Away from source charges, <Laplace's equation> makes the full potential <Hessian matrix> trace zero, which rules out a positive-definite three-dimensional Hessian but does not rule out a positive-definite restriction to two dimensions. Equal positive charges at the corners of a square give a planar minimum at its centre and an unstable perpendicular direction.

= Electrostatic reciprocity identity
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$\int_V(\phi_1\rho_2-\phi_2\rho_1)\,dV=\varepsilon_0\int_{\partial V}(\phi_2\nabla\phi_1-\phi_1\nabla\phi_2)\cdot d\mathbf S$}

For two <electrostatic potentials> satisfying the <Poisson equation> $\Delta\phi_i=-\rho_i/\varepsilon_0$, <Green's second identity> yields the displayed reciprocity formula. The <product rule for divergence> applied to $\phi_1\nabla\phi_2-\phi_2\nabla\phi_1$ cancels the cross-gradient terms. The normal points outward from the integration region; for an exterior region this means inward toward the excluded object on its inner boundary. Boundary terms may be removed only under suitable decay or boundary conditions.

= Image charge
{parent=Electric potential}

An image charge is a fictitious point charge placed outside the physical solution region so that its potential, combined with the real sources, enforces the required conductor boundary values. The <uniqueness of the Dirichlet problem> then identifies the constructed potential with the physical one. The image need not describe the actual induced surface-charge distribution.

= Fall time of a charge toward a grounded plane
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$t_{\rm hit}=\pi\sqrt{2\pi\varepsilon_0 m d^3}/|q|$}

The image of a charge $q$ at height $z$ is $-q$ at height $-z$. Their separation is $2z$, giving force $-q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0z^2)$. Starting at rest at height $d$, energy conservation gives $\dot z^2=q^2(1/z-1/d)/(8\pi\varepsilon_0m)$. Integrating the reciprocal speed to the plane yields the stated finite time in the electrostatic Newtonian model. The induced-conductor potential energy is $-q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0z)$; using the full image-pair energy would double the force incorrectly.

= Falling charge above a grounded conducting plane
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$T=\pi\sqrt{2\pi\varepsilon_0md^3}/|q|$}

A real charge of height $z$ has a reflected <image charge> at $-z$. Its acceleration is $\ddot z=-\kappa/z^2$, with $\kappa=q^2/(16\pi\varepsilon_0m)$. Released from rest at height $d$, its ideal-model arrival time is
$$
T=\frac{\pi d^{3/2}}{2\sqrt{2\kappa}}.
$$
The image follows the instantaneous reflected position. The induced-charge energy carries one-half the full real-image pair interaction factor, consistent with this force.

= Image charge for a grounded conducting sphere
{parent=Image charge}
{title2=$q'=-aq/R,\quad\mathbf p'=a^2\mathbf p/R^2$}

For a charge $q$ at $\mathbf p$, $R=|\mathbf p|>a$, outside a grounded sphere of radius $a$, the displayed image gives zero potential on the sphere and decay at infinity. On $|\mathbf x|=a$, one has $|\mathbf x-\mathbf p'|=(a/R)|\mathbf x-\mathbf p|$, proving boundary cancellation. The induced force is inward, of magnitude $q^2aR/[4\pi\varepsilon_0(R^2-a^2)^2]$, from <Coulomb's law> applied to the image field. The self-field is excluded.

= Electric potential difference
{parent=Electric potential}
{title2=$V_{AB}=\phi(A)-\phi(B)$}

An <electric potential difference> compares <electric potentials> at two points. For a move from $a$ to $b$, define $\Delta\Phi=\Phi(b)-\Phi(a)$; the terminal convention $V_{AB}=\Phi(A)-\Phi(B)$ instead means the potential at $A$ relative to $B$, so the order must always be stated. In <electrostatics>, the <line integral> of the <electric field> gives
$$
\Delta\Phi=-\int_a^b\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l,\qquad
\Delta U=q\Delta\Phi,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=q\int_a^b\mathbf E\cdot d\mathbf l=q\bigl(\Phi(a)-\Phi(b)\bigr)=-\Delta U.
$$
Here $\Delta U$ is the change in interaction <potential energy> of the test <electric charge> $q$ with fixed sources, while $W_{\mathrm{field}}$ is <work> done by the <electric field>. For an external agent moving the charge slowly without a change in <kinetic energy>, its <work> is $\Delta U$. A positive <electric charge> gains <kinetic energy> when accelerated toward lower <electric potential>; a negative <electric charge> gains <kinetic energy> when accelerated toward higher <electric potential>. Both motions lower $q\Phi$.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/7.2 Electric Potential and Potential Difference>[§7.2: potential difference, work, and energy]{ref}

For example, fix $Q=+2\,\mathrm{nC}$ at the origin and compare distances $r_a=0.20\,\mathrm m$ and $r_b=0.10\,\mathrm m$. With the stipulated approximation $k=8.99\times10^9\,\mathrm{N\,m^2/C^2}$ and zero at infinity,
$$
\Phi(a)=\frac{kQ}{r_a}=89.9\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Phi(b)=\frac{kQ}{r_b}=179.8\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\Delta\Phi=89.9\,\mathrm V.
$$
Moving $q=-3\,\mathrm{nC}$ inward therefore gives
$$
U_a=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
U_b=-5.394\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
\Delta U=-2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J,\qquad
W_{\mathrm{field}}=+2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J.
$$
If only the <electric force> does <work>, <conservation of energy> makes the gain in <kinetic energy> $2.697\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm J$. Direct integration confirms the signs: $\int_{r_a}^{r_b}kQ\,dr/r^2=kQ(1/r_a-1/r_b)=-89.9\,\mathrm V$, which multiplied by the negative test <electric charge> gives positive <work>. The <electric potential difference> is unchanged by choosing a different <electrical reference potential>.

In an <electrical circuit>, an <electric potential difference> is the voltage across a component's terminals. In an <electrical network>, a vertex <voltage> is specified relative to a reference vertex; its differences across edges drive <current flow>. For a uniform <electric field>, the integral reduces to $\Delta\Phi=-\mathbf E\cdot(\mathbf r_b-\mathbf r_a)$. A field $\mathbf E=300\,\mathrm{V/m}\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ across a displacement $0.04\,\mathrm m\,\widehat{\mathbf x}$ therefore gives $\Delta\Phi=-12\,\mathrm V$, as in the interior approximation for a <parallel-plate capacitor>. The <volt>, measured in <joules> per <coulomb>, expresses <energy> transfer per unit <electric charge>, which makes it useful for comparing components without specifying the amount of charge transported.

= Point charge
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

A point charge idealizes electric charge concentrated at one point. In vacuum, a charge $Q$ at the origin has potential $Q/(4\pi\epsilon_0r)$.

= Equipotential
{parent=Electric potential}
{wiki}

An equipotential is a level set of the electric potential. The electric field is normal to every smooth equipotential.

\Include[magnetic-field]{parent=Electromagnetism}

= Capacitance
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$C$}
{wiki}

The <capacitance> of a linear two-terminal <capacitor> is the constant relating its separated <electric charge> to its terminal <electric potential difference>:
$$
Q=CV,\qquad C=\frac QV\quad(V\ne0).
$$
Here $Q$ is the <electric charge> on one <electrical conductor>, the other carries $-Q$, and $V$ is their <electric potential difference> with the same ordering. Reversing the terminal ordering reverses both $Q$ and $V$ and leaves <capacitance> unchanged. An ideal passive <capacitor> has $C\ge0$; an ordinary pair of separated <electrical conductors> with positive <permittivity> has $C>0$. At $V=0$, use $Q=CV$ rather than the undefined ratio $0/0$.

For example, <electric charge> magnitude $Q=0.8854\,\mathrm{nC}$ at terminal <electric potential difference> $V=10\,\mathrm V$ gives <capacitance> $C=88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$. At $20\,\mathrm V$, the same linear <capacitor> has $Q=1.7708\,\mathrm{nC}$ and the same <capacitance>. The plots in <capacitor energy> show that <capacitance> is the <slope> of the <electric charge> versus terminal <electric potential difference> graph.

For fixed geometry and linear material response, <capacitance> depends on dimensions and <permittivity>, rather than the source's terminal <electric potential difference>. This is why <capacitance> is a useful property of a component: once it is known, $Q=CV$ predicts the separated <electric charge> for each operating terminal <electric potential difference>. The <SI> unit is the <farad>, $1\,\mathrm F=1\,\mathrm{C/V}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: capacitance and its unit]{ref}.

For combinations of <capacitors>, the same terminal definition gives an equivalent <capacitance>. Two identical <capacitors> of <capacitance> $C$ connected in parallel share the same terminal <electric potential difference>, and their <electric charges> add, giving $C_{\rm eq}=2C$. Connected in series with an initially neutral isolated junction, they carry equal plate <electric charge> magnitudes and their terminal <electric potential differences> add, giving $C_{\rm eq}=C/2$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-2-capacitors-in-series-and-in-parallel[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.2: capacitors in series and in parallel]{ref}.

= Farad
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$\mathrm F$}
{wiki}

The <farad>, symbol $\mathrm F$, is the <SI> unit of <capacitance>:
$$
1\,\mathrm F=1\,\frac{\mathrm C}{\mathrm V}.
$$
A <capacitor> of <capacitance> $1\,\mathrm F$ carries separated <electric charge> magnitude $1\,\mathrm C$ at terminal <electric potential difference> $1\,\mathrm V$. A picofarad is $10^{-12}\,\mathrm F$, so the worked <parallel-plate capacitor>'s <capacitance> $88.54\,\mathrm{pF}$ is $8.854\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm F$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance[OpenStax University Physics 2, §8.1: the farad]{ref}.

= Capacitance of an isolated conducting sphere
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$C=4\pi\epsilon R$}

An isolated conducting sphere of radius $R$ in a medium of <permittivity> $\epsilon$ has surface <electrostatic potential> $V=Q/(4\pi\epsilon R)$ relative to infinity. Thus its <capacitance> is $C=Q/V=4\pi\epsilon R$.

= Sphere-plane capacitance
{parent=Capacitance}
{title2=$C(R,h)$}

For a conducting sphere of radius $R$ at center height $h>R$ above a grounded infinite plane,
$$
C(R,h)=4\pi\epsilon R\sinh\alpha\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{\sinh(n\alpha)},
\qquad\cosh\alpha=\frac hR.
$$
The distance $h$ is measured to the center, so the surface gap is $h-R$. Relative to the <capacitance of an isolated conducting sphere>, the enhancement is $c(R/h)=C(R,h)/(4\pi\epsilon R)$.

The <method of images> gives a constructive proof. At fixed sphere potential $V_s$, start with $q_0=4\pi\epsilon RV_s$ at height $z_0=h$ and place its opposite image below the plane. Cancel that image's nonconstant sphere potential by the successive charges
$$
q_{n+1}=\frac R{h+z_n}q_n,
\qquad z_{n+1}=h-\frac{R^2}{h+z_n},
$$
with opposite mirror charges below the plane at every step. The resulting sphere potential is $V_s$ and the plane potential is zero. Since $q_n=4\pi\epsilon RV_s\sinh\alpha/\sinh((n+1)\alpha)$, summing the sphere charges gives the displayed <capacitance>. In particular $c(y)=1+y/2+O(y^2)$ for small $y$.

The exact sphere-plane boundary-value problem is also treated in https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6034[Behunin and colleagues' analysis of sphere-plane electrostatics].

= Capacitance per unit length
{parent=Capacitance}

Capacitance per unit length is the charge per unit length divided by the potential difference. A vacuum coaxial cable with radii $a<b$ has
$$
C'=\frac{2\pi\varepsilon_0}{\log(b/a)}.
$$

= Inductance
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{title2=$L$}
{wiki}

<Inductance> relates <electric currents> to the <magnetic flux linkage> they produce. For a single linear coil with fixed geometry and magnetic response, its <self-inductance> $L$ is the constant in
$$
\Lambda=LI.
$$
Here $\Lambda$ is <magnetic flux linkage> from the coil's own <electric current> $I$. The <SI> unit of <inductance> is the <henry (unit)>. Knowing <inductance> lets us predict the induced <electromotive force> when <electric current> changes, and the <magnetic energy> stored by an <inductor>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: self-inductance and inductors]{ref}.

For example, a coil of <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ carrying <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$ has <magnetic flux linkage> $1.5\,\mathrm{Wb}$. If its $100$ turns link the same <magnetic flux>, each turn has <magnetic flux> $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$. Keeping the geometry and magnetic response fixed while doubling <electric current> doubles <magnetic flux linkage> and quadruples the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

The comparison with <capacitance> is useful: a linear <capacitor> has $Q=CV$ and stores <capacitor energy> $CV^2/2$ in its <electric field>, whereas a linear <inductor> has $\Lambda=LI$ and stores <magnetic energy> $LI^2/2$ in its <magnetic field>. The <RLC circuit> brings both forms of <energy> storage together.

= Magnetic flux linkage
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\Lambda=\sum_{k=1}^N\Phi_k$}

= Flux linkage
{synonym}

<Magnetic flux linkage> adds the signed <magnetic fluxes> through the turns of a coil:
$$
\Lambda=\sum_{k=1}^N\Phi_k,\qquad
\Phi_k=\int_{S_k}\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S.
$$
Choose each <oriented surface> $S_k$ consistently with positive <electric current> around its turn using the <right-hand rule>. If all $N$ turns link approximately the same <magnetic flux> $\Phi$, then $\Lambda\approx N\Phi$; equality holds when every turn links exactly the same <magnetic flux>. Uniform <magnetic field> perpendicular to identical flat turns of <area> $A$ gives $\Phi=BA$. For a stipulated $100$-turn coil with <magnetic flux> $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$ per turn, <magnetic flux linkage> is $1.5\,\mathrm{Wb}$, not $0.015\,\mathrm{Wb}$. The turn count is dimensionless. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: flux through the turns of a coil]{ref}.

= Henry
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

The <henry (unit)> is the <SI> unit of <inductance>:
$$
1\,\mathrm H=1\,\frac{\mathrm{Wb}}{\mathrm A}
=1\,\frac{\mathrm{V\,s}}{\mathrm A}.
$$
A coil with <self-inductance> $1\,\mathrm H$ has <magnetic flux linkage> $1\,\mathrm{Wb}$ at <electric current> $1\,\mathrm A$. A rate of change of <electric current> $1\,\mathrm{A/s}$ produces induced <electromotive force> of magnitude $1\,\mathrm V$. The <weber (unit)> measures <magnetic flux> and <magnetic flux linkage>; the <henry (unit)> measures their ratio to <electric current>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: the unit of self-inductance]{ref}.

= Self-inductance
{parent=Inductance}
{title2=$\Lambda=LI,\quad\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-L\dot I$}

<Self-inductance> is <inductance> associated with a coil's own <electric current>, rather than <magnetic flux> supplied by another coil. In the linear model, fixed geometry and magnetic response give constant $L$ in $\Lambda=LI$. With the turn orientations defined in <magnetic flux linkage>, <Faraday's law of induction> gives the self-induced <electromotive force>
$$
\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-\frac{d\Lambda}{dt}=-L\frac{dI}{dt}.
$$
The final equality requires constant $L$; if the geometry changes, use $-d\Lambda/dt$. The minus sign expresses <Lenz's law>: the induced <electromotive force> opposes the change in <electric current>. It does not always oppose the <electric current> itself. When positive <electric current> decreases, the induced <electromotive force> acts in its direction. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-2-self-inductance-and-inductors[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.2: self-induced emf and its direction]{ref}.

For terminal sign bookkeeping, label the ideal <inductor>'s terminals $a,b$, choose positive <electric current> entering $a$ and leaving $b$, and define $v_L$ as the terminal <electric potential difference> drop from $a$ to $b$ in the lumped <electrical circuit> model. This passive convention gives
$$
v_L=+L\frac{dI}{dt}=-\mathcal E_{\rm ind}.
$$
Thus a coil with <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ and increasing <electric current> $dI/dt=12\,\mathrm{A/s}$ has terminal drop $v_L=6\,\mathrm V$ and induced <electromotive force> $\mathcal E_{\rm ind}=-6\,\mathrm V$. These signs describe the same process with different reference conventions. The <RL circuit> uses the terminal-drop convention throughout.

A finite terminal <electric potential difference> cannot make the <electric current> of an ideal <inductor> with constant $L>0$ jump. Integrating its <differential equation> gives
$$
I(t_2)-I(t_1)=\frac1L\int_{t_1}^{t_2}v_L(t)\,dt.
$$
If $v_L$ stays bounded, the <integral> tends to zero as the interval shrinks, ensuring <continuity> of <electric current>. A step in terminal <electric potential difference> changes $dI/dt$ immediately, while <electric current> remains continuous. At constant <electric current>, the self-induced <electromotive force> is zero, although the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> can remain nonzero.

= Inductor
{parent=Self-inductance}
{wiki}

An <inductor> is an <electrical circuit> component designed to provide <self-inductance>, often a coil such as a <solenoid>. An ideal linear <inductor> has constant $L>0$ and zero <electrical resistance>. Its stored <magnetic energy>, with zero chosen at zero <electric current>, is
$$
U=\frac12LI^2.
$$
For <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$ and <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$, this gives $U=2.25\,\mathrm J$. Unlike <Joule heating>, ideal storage is recoverable: the <inductor> can return <energy> to an <electrical circuit> when its <electric current> decreases. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/14-3-energy-in-a-magnetic-field[OpenStax University Physics 2, §14.3: energy stored by an inductor]{ref}.

The short derivation uses the terminal convention in <self-inductance>. The <power> absorbed by an ideal <inductor> is
$$
P_L=v_L I=LI\frac{dI}{dt}=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac12LI^2\right).
$$
Starting from zero <electric current>, the <integral> of this <power> gives
$$
U=\int_0^t P_L(t')\,dt'=\int_0^I Li\,di=\frac12LI^2.
$$
Positive $P_L$ increases stored <magnetic energy>; negative $P_L$ returns <energy>. At constant <electric current> $3\,\mathrm A$ in the example, $P_L=0$ and induced <electromotive force> is zero, but $U=2.25\,\mathrm J$ still resides in the <magnetic field>. The <battery> in the steady <RL circuit> supplies <Joule heating> in the <electrical resistance>, rather than further increasing the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy>.

\Image[inductor-energy-storage-and-release.png]
{title=Stored <magnetic energy> and its release as <Joule heating> in an <RL circuit>}
{description=Left: the <inductor>'s <magnetic energy> during buildup and a separate decay experiment starting at $3\,\mathrm A$. Right: the <power> of <Joule heating> during decay; the shaded <integral> over all time is $2.25\,\mathrm J$. The decay path stays closed through <electrical resistance> $2\,\Omega$ and <self-inductance> $0.5\,\mathrm H$.}

During the closed-loop release in <RL circuit>, stored <magnetic energy> plus accumulated <Joule heating> stays $2.25\,\mathrm J$. A real coil also has <electrical resistance>, so its <Joule heating> must be included separately from ideal <inductor> storage. The constant-$L$ formulas assume a fixed linear magnetic response.

= Bound charge and bound current
{parent=Electromagnetism}

Polarization and magnetization produce the effective sources
$$
\rho_{\rm b}=-\nabla\cdot P,
\qquad
J_{\rm b}=\partial_tP+\nabla\times M.
$$
Defining $D=\epsilon_0E+P$ and $H=B/\mu_0-M$ moves these sources into the macroscopic fields.

= Bound current
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{title2=$\mathbf J_b=\partial_t\mathbf P+\nabla\times\mathbf M$}

The current associated with <electric polarization> and <magnetization> in matter. If the magnetic response is neglected, it is $\mathbf J_b=\partial_t\mathbf P$. It is distinguished from a free conduction current in the <Maxwell equations in matter>.

= Polarization density
{title2=$\mathbf P$}
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{wiki}

= Electric polarization
{synonym}

Electric polarization is electric dipole moment per unit volume. It produces bound volume and surface charge
$$
\rho_{\rm b}=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf P,
\qquad
\sigma_{\rm b}=\mathbf P\cdot\mathbf n.
$$
Across an interface, the net bound sheet charge is $(\mathbf P_1-\mathbf P_2)\cdot\mathbf n$, where $\mathbf n$ points from medium one to medium two.

= Electric displacement field
{title2=$\mathbf D$}
{parent=Polarization density}
{wiki}

The electric displacement is $\mathbf D=\epsilon_0\mathbf E+\mathbf P$ and obeys $\nabla\cdot\mathbf D=\rho_{\rm free}$. In a linear isotropic dielectric, $\mathbf D=\epsilon\mathbf E$.

= Magnetization
{title2=$\mathbf M$}
{parent=Bound charge and bound current}
{wiki}

Magnetization is magnetic dipole moment per unit volume. It produces bound volume and surface currents
$$
\mathbf J_{\rm b}=\nabla\times\mathbf M,
\qquad
\mathbf K_{\rm b}=\mathbf M\times\mathbf n.
$$
Across an interface, the net bound sheet current is $(\mathbf M_1-\mathbf M_2)\times\mathbf n$.

= Paramagnetism
{parent=Magnetization}
{wiki}
{title2=$\chi>0$}

In a linear isotropic regime, a paramagnetic material has positive <magnetic susceptibility>: its induced <magnetization> is aligned with the applied <magnetic field intensity>. <Molecular oxygen> supplies an example discussed through <molecular orbital theory> in the <covalent bond> article.

= Curie temperature
{c}
{parent=Magnetization}
{title2=$T_C$}
{wiki}

The Curie temperature is the transition temperature above which a ferromagnetic material loses spontaneous <magnetization>. A hot conducting planetary core can generate a <magnetic field> through <dynamo action> without behaving as a permanent magnet. The blocking temperature at which a mineral records crustal remanence also depends on its relaxation timescale and need not equal its Curie temperature.

= Remanent magnetization
{parent=Magnetization}

= Remanence
{synonym}

Remanent magnetization is <magnetization> retained after the applied <magnetic field> has been removed. Magnetic minerals cooling in an ambient field can preserve information about that field in a planetary crust. Such a record may outlive the <planetary dynamo> which originally produced it. Crustal remanence therefore differs from a present field maintained by conducting-fluid motion.

= Magnetization current
{title2=$J_{\mathrm{mag}}^\mu$}
{parent=Magnetization}

A relativistic magnetization current has the form $J_{\mathrm{mag}}^\mu=\partial_\nu M^{\nu\mu}$ for an antisymmetric polarization-magnetization tensor. It is identically conserved because commuting derivatives contract an antisymmetric tensor.

= Magnetic field intensity
{title2=$\mathbf H$}
{parent=Magnetization}

The magnetic field intensity is $\mathbf H=\mathbf B/\mu_0-\mathbf M$ and obeys $\nabla\times\mathbf H=\mathbf J_{\rm free}$ in magnetostatics. In a linear isotropic medium, $\mathbf B=\mu\mathbf H$.

= Magnetic spherical-shell matching
{parent=Electromagnetism}

Across an interface with no free surface current, the normal component of $B$ and tangential component of $H$ are continuous. For an axisymmetric spherical shell, harmonic scalar potentials have dipole form $(ar+b/r^2)\cos\theta$ in each radial region.

= Maxwell stress tensor
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

With electromagnetic momentum density $\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B$, the momentum-flux convention
$$
\sigma_{ij}=-\epsilon_0E_iE_j+\frac{\epsilon_0}{2}E^2\delta_{ij}
-\frac1{\mu_0}B_iB_j+\frac1{2\mu_0}B^2\delta_{ij}
$$
satisfies
$$
\partial_tg_j+\partial_i\sigma_{ij}=-(\rho\mathbf E+\mathbf J\times\mathbf B)_j.
$$

= Electromagnetic momentum density
{title2=$\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B=\mathbf S/c^2$}
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

The electromagnetic momentum density in vacuum is $\mathbf g=\epsilon_0\mathbf E\times\mathbf B$. It equals the <Poynting vector> divided by $c^2$, expressing the relativistic relation between energy flux and momentum density.

= Local conservation of electromagnetic momentum
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

With the momentum-flux sign convention used for the <Maxwell stress tensor>, Maxwell's equations imply
$$
\nabla\mathbin{\cdot}\boldsymbol\sigma
+\partial_t\mathbf g=-\mathbf f_{\rm L},
$$
where $\mathbf g$ is the <electromagnetic momentum density> and $\mathbf f_{\rm L}$ is the <Lorentz force density> exerted by the field on matter. Thus total field-plus-matter momentum is locally conserved.

= Radiation pressure on a perfectly absorbing surface
{parent=Maxwell stress tensor}

A normally incident vacuum wave of time-averaged intensity $I$ transfers momentum $I/c$ per unit area and time to a perfectly absorbing surface, so its radiation pressure is
$$
p=\frac Ic.
$$
A perfect reflector reverses the normal momentum and gives $2I/c$.

= Proca action
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

The Proca action adds $m^2A_\mu A^\mu/2$ to the Maxwell Lagrangian. It describes a massive spin-one field and explicitly breaks the Abelian gauge symmetry of the massless theory.

= Proca equation
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}

A massive vector field coupled to a conserved current obeys
$$
\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}-m^2A^\nu=-\mu_0J^\nu.
$$
The mass term breaks electromagnetic gauge invariance.

= Proca field
{c}
{parent=Proca equation}

A Proca field is a massive spin-one vector field. Its field equation enforces transversality and leaves three physical polarizations.

= Proca propagator
{c}
{parent=Proca field}

For metric signature $(+---)$, the momentum-space Feynman propagator of a Proca field is
$$
\widetilde\Delta_{\mu\nu}(p)=\frac{-i}{p^2-m^2+i\epsilon}\left(\eta_{\mu\nu}-\frac{p_\mu p_\nu}{m^2}\right).
$$
The longitudinal numerator term inverts the massive vector kinetic operator without gauge fixing.

= High-energy obstruction for a hard vector mass
{parent=Proca propagator}
{title2=$D_{\mu\nu}(k)=-i(g_{\mu\nu}-k_\mu k_\nu/m^2)/(k^2-m^2+i0)$}

A free <Proca field> is consistent and has three physical <particle polarizations>. The difficulty for generic interactions is the ultraviolet behavior of its <Proca propagator>: the longitudinal numerator makes part of it approach order $1/m^2$, rather than decay as $1/k^2$. Longitudinal external <polarization vectors> also behave as $\varepsilon_L^\mu=k^\mu/m+O(m/E)$. Without suitable identities and cancellations, amplitudes grow with energy and perturbative counterterms of increasing dimension are needed. Conserved-current Abelian models and the <Stueckelberg mechanism> are important exceptions; a massive interacting vector is not intrinsically inconsistent as an <effective field theory>.

= Proca time-ordering contact term
{c}
{parent=Proca propagator}
{title2=$\Delta^{\rm cov}_{ab}-\Delta^{T}_{ab}=i\delta_a^0\delta_b^0\delta^{(4)}/m^2$}

For a free massive <Proca field> in the mostly-plus <Minkowski metric>, the covariant inverse of the quadratic <action> and the canonical <time-ordered product> of vector potentials differ locally. With <Fourier transform> $e^{iq\cdot(x-y)}$, the covariant <Proca propagator> is $-i(\eta_{ab}+q_aq_b/m^2)/(q^2+m^2-i0)$, while the literal canonical <two-point correlation function> subtracts $i\delta_a^0\delta_b^0/m^2$ in momentum space. The time component is nondynamical; its physical-mode numerator is $|\boldsymbol q|^2/m^2$, rather than $-1+(q^0)^2/m^2$. The difference is an instantaneous <Dirac delta function>, not another propagating state. Both expressions agree away from coincidence. This distinction matters when identifying a covariant <Green function> with a canonical correlator in a constrained field theory.

= Transverse vector field
{parent=Proca field}

A vector field is transverse when $\partial_\mu V^\mu=0$ in spacetime, or $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf V=0$ for a spatial Fourier mode.

= Lorenz constraint in Proca theory
{parent=Proca equation}
{c}

Taking a divergence and using antisymmetry of $F^{\mu\nu}$ and current conservation gives $m^2\partial_\mu A^\mu=0$. For nonzero mass, the Lorenz condition follows from the field equation rather than from a gauge choice.

= Yukawa potential
{parent=Proca equation}
{c}
{wiki}

The static Green function of $\nabla^2-m^2$ in three dimensions is proportional to $e^{-mr}/r$. It describes an interaction screened beyond range $m^{-1}$ and tends to the Coulomb potential as $m\to0$.

= Relativistic Lorentz force
{parent=Electromagnetism}

$m\,du^\mu/d\tau=qF^\mu{}_\nu u^\nu$ gives $d\mathbf p/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ and $d(\gamma mc^2)/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$.

= Constant-field relativistic Lorentz-force solution
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

For a constant <electromagnetic field tensor>, the <relativistic Lorentz force> is a constant-coefficient linear system on <four-velocity>, with solution
$$
u(\tau)=\exp\!\left(\frac qmF\tau\right)u(0).
$$
The electric-type blocks of $F$ generate hyperbolic rotations, while magnetic-type blocks generate ordinary rotations.

= Relativistic motion in parallel electric and magnetic fields
{parent=Constant-field relativistic Lorentz-force solution}

When uniform $\mathbf E$ and $\mathbf B$ are parallel, the <relativistic Lorentz force> splits into independent hyperbolic motion along the fields and circular motion in the perpendicular plane. The circle is periodic in <proper time>, while electric acceleration makes the helix's longitudinal pitch grow exponentially from one proper-time cyclotron period to the next.

= Relativistic hyperbolic motion in a uniform electric field
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

A particle released from rest in a constant electric field parallel to its motion follows a spacetime hyperbola and has rapidity proportional to proper time.

This force law realizes <hyperbolic motion> with constant <proper acceleration>.

= Null crossed electromagnetic field
{parent=Relativistic Lorentz force}

Perpendicular fields with $\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B=0$ and $E^2-c^2B^2=0$ form a null electromagnetic field. The constant-field Lorentz-force generator is nilpotent, so its exponential terminates as a polynomial.

Both invariants of the <electromagnetic field tensor> vanish for this field class.

= Relativistic trajectory in a constant null crossed field
{parent=Null crossed electromagnetic field}

Choose
$$
\mathbf B=B\,\mathbf e_z,\qquad
\mathbf E=cB\,\mathbf e_x,
\qquad
\Omega=\frac{qB}{m}.
$$
A particle released from rest at the origin has the proper-time trajectory
$$
t(\tau)=\tau+\frac{\Omega^2\tau^3}{6},
\qquad
x(\tau)=\frac{c\Omega\tau^2}{2},
\qquad
y(\tau)=-\frac{c\Omega^2\tau^3}{6},
\qquad z(\tau)=0.
$$
Eliminating <proper time> gives the semicubical parabola
$$
\boxed{y^2=\frac{2qB}{9mc}x^3.}
$$
This signed coefficient assumes that the positive $x$ direction is chosen along $\mathbf E$; reversing that axis changes the signs of both $x^3$ and its coefficient.

= Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A velocity cross magnetic-field force bends perpendicular motion into a circle while doing no work.

= Magnetic angular-momentum invariant
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{title2=$\mathbf L\cdot\mathbf B+\frac q2|\mathbf r\times\mathbf B|^2$}

For a particle obeying $m\dot{\mathbf v}=q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$ in a constant uniform <magnetic field>, the displayed combination is conserved. The derivative of mechanical <angular momentum> projected onto the field is canceled by the quadratic position term. This is the rotational analogue of the distinction between mechanical and <canonical momentum> in an electromagnetic field.

= Helical motion in a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}

In a uniform <magnetic field>, the <Lorentz force> leaves the component of <velocity> parallel to the field constant and rotates the perpendicular component at the nonrelativistic <cyclotron frequency> $|q|B/m$. The resulting path is a <helix> around a field line.

= Guiding center
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{wiki}

= Guiding centre
{synonym}

The guiding center is the center of a charged particle's circular cyclotron orbit. In a nonuniform field it drifts slowly compared with the rapid <cyclotron frequency>.

= Damped cyclotron motion
{parent=Charged particle in a uniform magnetic field}
{wiki}

Adding linear drag to cyclotron motion produces an exponentially contracting spiral.

\Include[electrostatics]{parent=Electromagnetism}

= Magnetostatics
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Magnetostatics studies time-independent magnetic fields and steady currents. In vacuum its field equations are $\nabla\mathbin\cdot\mathbf B=0$ and $\nabla\mathbin\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J$.

= Magnetic scalar potential
{parent=Magnetostatics}
{title2=$\mathbf B=-\nabla\psi$}
{wiki}

In a simply connected current-free region of constant <magnetic permeability>, <Ampère's circuital law> makes the <magnetic field> curl-free, so it admits a scalar potential. The absence of magnetic monopoles then gives <Laplace's equation> for that potential. Some conventions define the potential for $\mathbf H$ instead of $\mathbf B$, differing by the constant permeability. Boundary conditions, including imposed fields at infinity, determine the potential up to an additive constant.

= Gravity-equivalent magnetic surface density
{parent=Magnetic scalar potential}
{title2=$\Sigma_M=-B_{z0}/\sqrt{\pi G\mu_0}$}

For an isolated razor-thin disk, rescale its exterior <magnetic scalar potential> to satisfy $\sqrt{4\pi G/\mu_0}\mathbf B=-\nabla\Phi_M$. Comparing the normal magnetic boundary derivative with $\partial_z\Phi=2\pi G\Sigma$ identifies the displayed equivalent signed <surface density>. Both exterior potentials satisfy <Laplace's equation>. The equivalent <surface density> is a representation of the magnetic boundary data, rather than physical negative mass.

\Include[maxwell-equations]{parent=Electromagnetism}

= Magnetic vector potential
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A magnetic vector potential satisfies B equal to its curl and is defined up to a gradient.

= Magnetic helicity
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{title2=$H=\int_V\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B\,dV,\quad\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$}
{wiki}

The <magnetic helicity> integrates the <dot product> of a <magnetic vector potential> and its <magnetic field>. It measures linkage and twisting of magnetic flux. For a <solenoidal vector field> $\mathbf B$ tangent to the boundary, it is unchanged by the gauge transformation $\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi$, since the change is $\int_V\nabla\cdot(\chi\mathbf B)\,dV=\int_{\partial V}\chi\mathbf B\cdot d\mathbf S=0$. If its density satisfies $\partial_t h=\nabla\cdot(f\mathbf B-h\mathbf u)$ with both vector fields tangent to the boundary of a fixed volume, the <divergence theorem> gives conservation of its integral. This is an instance of <helicity conservation by tangent boundary conditions>.

= Magnetic-energy lower bound from helicity
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$E_B\geq |H_M|/(2\mu_0 C_D)$}

In a bounded regular domain, fix the global extension or circulation convention for the <magnetic vector potential>. A bounded inverse of <curl> on tangent <solenoidal vector fields> gives $\|\mathbf A\|_2\leq C_D\|\mathbf B\|_2$. The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> then gives $|H_M|\leq C_D\|\mathbf B\|_2^2=2\mu_0 C_DE_B$. One construction extends the tangent field by zero outside the domain and uses the whole-space <Biot-Savart law>; its kernel of size $|\mathbf x-\mathbf y|^{-2}$ is integrable over a bounded region, giving the required $L^2$ bound. Nonzero conserved <magnetic helicity> therefore prevents complete decay of <magnetic energy>.

= Current helicity
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$H_c=\langle\mathbf b\cdot\nabla\times\mathbf b\rangle$}

Current helicity correlates a <magnetic field> with its <curl>, which is proportional to <electric current density>. It differs from <magnetic helicity>, which uses a <magnetic vector potential>. For a solenoidal monochromatic field of wavenumber $k$ in a periodic domain, choose $\mathbf a_b=\nabla\times\mathbf b/k^2$. Then $\nabla\times\mathbf a_b=\mathbf b$ and $H_c=k^2\langle\mathbf a_b\cdot\mathbf b\rangle$.

= Gauge dependence of helicity in an open magnetic flux tube
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$H_m/L=\pi bCR_0^2$}

An open <flux tube> carries <magnetic flux> through its end caps. Its ordinary <magnetic helicity> can therefore change under a single-valued <gauge transformation>. For $\mathbf B=aR\mathbf e_\phi+b\mathbf e_z$ inside $R<R_0$ and zero outside, a regular family of <magnetic vector potentials> is $A_\phi=bR/2$, $A_z=C-aR^2/2$ inside and $A_\phi=bR_0^2/(2R)$, $A_z=C-aR_0^2/2$ outside. Their tangential components agree at $R_0$. Inside, $\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B=bC$, so the helicity per unit length is $\pi bCR_0^2$. Changing $C$ is the gauge transformation $\Lambda=\Delta C\,z$, and changes this result whenever $b\ne0$. The convention $A_z=0$ outside gives $C=aR_0^2/2$ and helicity per unit length $\pi abR_0^4/2$, while $C=0$ gives zero. Without an end-cap or gauge convention the ordinary helicity is not unique.

= Material transport of magnetic helicity density
{parent=Magnetic helicity}
{title2=$D_t(\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B/\rho)$}

For smooth <ideal magnetohydrodynamics>, write $\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$ and $\partial_t\mathbf A=\mathbf u\times\mathbf B-\nabla\varphi$. The <continuity equation> and induction equation give $D_t(\mathbf B/\rho)=(\mathbf B/\rho)\cdot\nabla\mathbf u$. The vector-potential equation becomes $D_t\mathbf A=\nabla(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi)-(\nabla\mathbf u)^T\mathbf A$. Contracting these two equations cancels their velocity-gradient terms and proves
$$
D_t\left(\frac{\mathbf A\cdot\mathbf B}{\rho}\right)=\frac{\mathbf B}{\rho}\cdot\nabla(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi).
$$
Consequently the <magnetic helicity> in a material volume satisfies $dH_m/dt=\int_{\partial V}(\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf A-\varphi)\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf n\,dS$. It is conserved when the <magnetic field> is tangent to the boundary. A single-valued <gauge transformation> changes $H_m$ by $\int_{\partial V}\Lambda\mathbf B\cdot\mathbf n\,dS$, which also vanishes under that condition.

= Radial-gauge potential for a divergence-free magnetic field
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{title2=$\mathbf A(\mathbf x)=\int_0^1s\,\mathbf B(s\mathbf x)\times\mathbf x\,ds$}

For a smooth <divergence>-free field on $\mathbb R^3$, the <differential form> $F=\iota_{\mathbf B}(dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3)$ is closed. The radial homotopy formula gives a global <magnetic vector potential> $\mathbf A(\mathbf x)=\int_0^1s\,\mathbf B(s\mathbf x)\times\mathbf x\,ds$ with <curl> $\mathbf B$. For constant $\mathbf B$, this reduces to $\mathbf A=\tfrac12\mathbf B\times\mathbf x$.

= Neumann's mutual-inductance formula
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{c}

For thin closed wire loops $C_1,C_2$, the mutual inductance is
$$
L_{12}=L_{21}
=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}
\oint_{C_1}\oint_{C_2}
\frac{dx_1\cdot dx_2}{|x_1-x_2|}.
$$

= Coulomb gauge
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{c}
{wiki}

The Coulomb gauge imposes
$$
\nabla\cdot A=0
$$
on the <magnetic vector potential>. A <gauge transformation> $A\mapsto A+\nabla\chi$ reaches this gauge when $\chi$ solves
$$
\nabla^2\chi=-\nabla\cdot A.
$$

= Radiation gauge
{parent=Coulomb gauge}
{title2=$A_0=0,\quad\partial_iA_i=0$}

For a free <electromagnetic field> with suitable boundary conditions, combine <Coulomb gauge> $\partial_iA_i=0$ with $A_0=0$. <Gauss's law> makes the second condition consistent in the source-free problem. The remaining field operator is spatially transverse and describes the two radiative <photon> polarizations. A constrained scalar potential must be restored when charges are present.

= Gauge transformation
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{wiki}

The transformation $A\mapsto A+\nabla\chi$ leaves $B=\nabla\times A$ unchanged because the <curl> of a <gradient> vanishes.

= Gauge-covariant time translation
{parent=Gauge transformation}
{title2=$\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}$}

For the <Maxwell field>, the variation $\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}=\alpha(\partial_0A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_0)$ is time translation combined with a <gauge transformation>. With constant $\alpha$, $\delta F_{\mu\nu}=\alpha\partial_0F_{\mu\nu}$, and the Lagrangian changes by $\alpha\partial_0\mathcal L$. Its <Noether current> is directly the time component of the <gauge-invariant Maxwell stress-energy tensor>, providing the physical energy density without a potential-dependent improvement step.

= Magnetic dipole moment
{parent=Magnetic vector potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathbf m$}

= Magnetic moment
{synonym}

A <magnetic dipole moment> is a <vector> that summarizes the strength and orientation of a localized magnetic source. For a planar <current loop>, it is the <electric current> times the <vector area>: $\mathbf m=IA\widehat{\mathbf n}$. The <right-hand rule> fixes the unit <normal vector> $\widehat{\mathbf n}$: curl the fingers of the right hand along the <conventional current>, and the thumb points along $\mathbf m$. This single <vector> predicts both the distant <magnetic dipole field> and the turning effect of an imposed <magnetic field>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/11.5 Force and Torque on a Current Loop>[§11.5, equations 11.20–11.22: loop moment, winding count and torque]{ref}

The <SI> unit is <ampere> times square metre, $\mathrm{A\,m^2}$, equivalently <joule> per <tesla (unit)>. A <magnetic dipole moment> is neither a <magnetic field> nor a <magnetic monopole>: a closed <current loop> can have a nonzero <magnetic dipole moment> while its net outward <magnetic flux> through any closed surface remains zero, as required by <Gauss's law for magnetism>.

For a localized, steady <electric current density> $\mathbf J(\mathbf r)$, the more general definition is
$$
\mathbf m=\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}\mathbf r\times\mathbf J(\mathbf r)\,d^3r.
$$
Here the <cross product> is integrated over the complete source, including return paths. Steadiness requires $\nabla\cdot\mathbf J=0$, and localization requires sufficiently rapid decay for the boundary terms to vanish. Under these assumptions the <localized steady-current moment identities> give $\int\mathbf J\,d^3r=0$: shifting the origin by $\mathbf a$ changes the proposed <magnetic dipole moment> by $-\tfrac12\mathbf a\times\int\mathbf J\,d^3r=0$. An isolated open wire segment does not satisfy these assumptions. <Classical Electromagnetism by Richard Fitzpatrick/Localized Current Distribution>{ref}.

= Current loop
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\mathbf m=I\mathbf A$}

A <current loop> is a closed path carrying <electric current>. For a thin wire following a closed <curve> $C$, the <volume integral> defining its <magnetic dipole moment> becomes a <line integral>:
$$
\mathbf m=\frac I2\oint_C\mathbf r\times d\mathbf r=I\mathbf A,
\qquad
\mathbf A=\frac12\oint_C\mathbf r\times d\mathbf r.
$$
The <vector area> $\mathbf A$ is $A\widehat{\mathbf n}$ for a planar <current loop>. For a nonplanar <current loop>, use the <vector area>, rather than the scalar <area> of an arbitrarily chosen spanning surface. Reversing the <electric current> reverses $\mathbf m$ without changing the geometry. <Classical Electromagnetism by Richard Fitzpatrick/Localized Current Distribution>{ref}.

\Image[current-loop-moment-right-hand-rule.png]
{title=Current direction and the right-hand rule for a planar magnetic dipole moment}
{description=Viewed face-on, counterclockwise <conventional current> gives a <magnetic dipole moment> out of the page, shown by a dot; reversing the <electric current> gives a <magnetic dipole moment> into the page, shown by a cross. The curled fingers follow the <electric current> and the thumb follows the unit <normal vector>.}

For $N$ closely spaced identical turns carrying the same <electric current>, the <magnetic dipole moments> add: $\mathbf m=NIA\widehat{\mathbf n}$. Each turn contributes its own <vector area>; unequal areas or differently oriented turns require a <vector> sum. As a calculated example, twelve circular turns of <radius> $R=3.0\,\mathrm{cm}$ carrying $I=0.40\,\mathrm A$ give $m=NI\pi R^2=1.36\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{A\,m^2}$. This is an idealized example, not an experimental measurement.

= Helmholtz coil
{c}
{parent=Current loop}
{title2=$B_0=(4/5)^{3/2}\mu_0N_HI_H/R_H$}
{wiki}

A <Helmholtz coil> arrangement uses two identical coaxial circular windings separated by their <radius>, carrying equal <electric currents> in the same circulation sense. Their combined <magnetic field> is especially uniform near the midpoint, making it useful for a <current-loop torque experiment>. The cancellation of the axial second <derivative> there follows from the <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop>.

= Magnetic dipole torque
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\tau=\mathbf m\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}$}

An externally imposed <magnetic field> that is nearly uniform over a rigid <current loop> produces the <torque>
$$
\boldsymbol\tau=\mathbf m\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}},
\qquad
U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}=-mB\cos\theta.
$$
The <angle> $\theta$ is measured between the <magnetic dipole moment> and the external <magnetic field>, not between the plane of the <current loop> and the <magnetic field>. The <magnetic dipole potential energy> uses a <dot product>, whereas the <torque> uses a <cross product>. The <torque> is largest when the <magnetic dipole moment> is perpendicular to the <magnetic field>; parallel alignment minimizes the <potential energy>.<University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/11.5 Force and Torque on a Current Loop>[§11.5, equations 11.22–11.23: vector torque and orientation energy]{ref}

For the sign convention $\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}=B\widehat{\mathbf x}$ and $\mathbf m=m(\cos\theta\widehat{\mathbf x}+\sin\theta\widehat{\mathbf y})$, positive rotation increases $\theta$ and
$$
\tau_z=-mB\sin\theta=-\frac{dU}{d\theta}.
$$
Thus at $0<\theta<\pi$ the <torque> turns the <magnetic dipole moment> toward the external <magnetic field>. Both parallel and antiparallel alignment have zero <torque>, but they are respectively a <stable equilibrium> and an <unstable equilibrium>. With no <damping>, a freely released <current loop> can undergo <oscillation> around the <stable equilibrium>; settling requires <damping>.

\Image[magnetic-dipole-angular-energy-torque.png]
{title=Angular potential energy and signed torque of a magnetic dipole in an imposed field}
{description=The ideal model has $U/(mB)=-\cos\theta$ and $\tau_z/(mB)=-\sin\theta$. The diagram fixes the external <magnetic field> along positive $x$, so the <torque> is clockwise for the illustrated positive <angle>. These curves are calculated, not measured data.}

In the twelve-turn example, take $B=0.020\,\mathrm T$ and $\theta=60\degree$. The predicted <torque> is $\tau_z=-2.35\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{N\,m}$ and the <magnetic dipole potential energy> is $U=-1.36\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm J$. The unit check is $(\mathrm{A\,m^2})\mathrm T=\mathrm{N\,m}=\mathrm J$; <torque> and <energy> have the same <physical dimensions> but different physical meanings.

A short derivation starts with the <magnetic force on a current-carrying wire>, $d\mathbf F=I\,d\mathbf r\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}$. In an exactly uniform <magnetic field>, the net <force> is $I(\oint_Cd\mathbf r)\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}=0$, but its <torque> need not vanish: opposite sides produce a turning <couple>. Integrating $\mathbf r\times d\mathbf F$ gives $\mathbf m\times\mathbf B_{\mathrm{ext}}$. Integrating $dU=-\tau_z\,d\theta$ at fixed $m$ and $B$ gives the orientation <potential energy>. This is the mechanical orientation <potential energy> for a maintained <electric current>, with the external source held fixed; the <energy> accounting for field and power supply is a separate problem. A varying external <magnetic field> requires corrections across a finite <current loop>, and can also give a net <force>.

= Current-loop torque experiment
{parent=Magnetic dipole torque}

The PHYWE guide <Magnetic moment in the magnetic field> makes the <magnetic dipole moment> measurable. A circular test <current loop> sits between a pair of <Helmholtz coils>, with separate supplies and meters for the test <electric current> $I$ and field-producing <electric current> $I_H$. The test <current loop> is mounted on a <torsion dynamometer>. The guide varies $I_H$, the number of turns, the test <electric current>, diameter, and the <angle> between the <magnetic dipole moment> and <magnetic field>. Its angular settings are spaced by $15\degree$. The leads hang loosely and are twisted together to suppress extra <torque>; the instrument zero is checked frequently because moving leads can shift it. Its reported results include approximately linear dependence on each <electric current> and on winding count, and approximately quadratic dependence on diameter.<Magnetic moment in the magnetic field>[pp. 4–9: theory, equipment, setup, angular settings, Table 1 and Figures 2–6]{ref}

The readout is a balance of <torques>. According to the manufacturer's <Torsion dynamometer 0.01 N operating instructions>, a metallic band supplies the restoring <torque>. First set the indication to zero with the applied <force> absent and center the lever using the zero knob. Apply the load, then turn the indicating knob until the lever returns to its original zero position. The scale then reads a force equivalent, not a <torque> directly. The quoted scale division is $0.1\,\mathrm{mN}$. For a <force> applied perpendicular to the calibration <lever arm> at distance $\ell$, the conversion is $\tau=F\ell$; a <current loop> applies a <couple>, so its <torque> must be compared with the calibrated restoring <torque>.<Torsion dynamometer 0.01 N operating instructions>[§§1–4: metallic-band compensation, zero procedure, force indication and scale specification]{ref}

For a quantitative repeat, independently check that conversion with known <forces> at a measured perpendicular <lever arm>, and check the imposed <magnetic field> at the test <current loop>. These are proposed calibration checks, not additional measurements reported here. The ideal <Helmholtz coil> model predicts
$$
B_0=\left(\frac45\right)^{3/2}\frac{\mu_0N_HI_H}{R_H},
\qquad
|\tau|=NI\pi R^2 B_0|\sin\theta|,
$$
where $N_H$ and $R_H$ are the winding count and radius of each field-producing <Helmholtz coil>, distinct from $N$ and $R$ of the test <current loop>. Derive $B_0$ by adding the <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop> at distances $R_H/2$ from both coils. Independently calibrating $B_0$ and the restoring <torque> lets a fitted <torque> amplitude determine $m$ as amplitude divided by $B_0$, to compare with $NI\pi R^2$.

Useful additional controls are to reverse only $I$, reverse only $I_H$, and reverse both: the signed <torque> should reverse in either single reversal and be unchanged in the double reversal. Record baselines with each <electric current> separately zero, and subtract a nearby zero reading to reduce <torsion balance zero drift>. Repeat angular settings in both directions to reveal lead-dependent offsets. These are suggested controls. Limits include finite <magnetic field> uniformity, uncertainty in effective winding area and angular zero, meter calibration, <Joule heating>, and residual lead <torque>. An apparently sinusoidal trace alone does not establish the absolute <magnetic dipole moment>. No raw laboratory data or new measured values are claimed here.

= Torsion dynamometer
{parent=Current-loop torque experiment}
{tag=Torsion balance}

A <torsion dynamometer> compares an applied <torque> with the restoring <torque> of a twisted elastic element. Returning the lever to its zero position allows a <current-loop torque experiment> to hold the test orientation fixed while reading the compensating <torque>.

= Far-field approximation for a current loop
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\mathbf B\simeq\mu_0[3(\mathbf m\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf m]/(4\pi r^3)$}

Far outside a localized steady source of size $a$, at distance $r\gg a$, its <multipole expansion> starts with the <magnetic dipole moment> if that moment is nonzero. In <vacuum>, using <SI> units,
$$
\mathbf A_{\mathrm{dip}}(\mathbf r)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{\mathbf m\times\mathbf r}{r^3},
\qquad
\mathbf B_{\mathrm{dip}}(\mathbf r)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi r^3}\left[3(\mathbf m\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})\widehat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf m\right],
$$
where $\mu_0$ is the <vacuum permeability>, $\widehat{\mathbf r}=\mathbf r/r$, and the <magnetic field> is the <curl> of the <magnetic vector potential>. The <magnetic dipole field> falls as $r^{-3}$, and the <magnetic vector potential> as $r^{-2}$. The <localized steady-current moment identities> remove the putative $r^{-1}$ term in the <magnetic vector potential>. No <magnetic monopole> is needed. If different <current loops> cancel the total <magnetic dipole moment>, a higher term of the <multipole expansion> supplies the leading distant <magnetic field>. <Classical Electromagnetism by Richard Fitzpatrick/Localized Current Distribution>{ref}.

The approximation is not valid at the center of a finite <current loop>. For a circular <current loop> of <radius> $R$ and <electric current> $I$, the exact <on-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop> and the distant approximation are
$$
B_z(z)=\frac{\mu_0IR^2}{2(R^2+z^2)^{3/2}},
\qquad
B_{z,\mathrm{dip}}(z)=\frac{\mu_0IR^2}{2|z|^3}.
$$
Their ratio is $B_z/B_{z,\mathrm{dip}}=(1+R^2/z^2)^{-3/2}$: at $|z|=10R$, the approximate <magnetic field> exceeds the exact <magnetic field> by about $1.50\%$ relative to the exact value. Near the source, retain its geometry rather than replacing it with a point source specified only by its <magnetic dipole moment>.

= Localized steady-current moment identities
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{title2=$\int j_i=0,\quad\int x_i j_k=-\int x_k j_i$}

A localized steady <electric current density> satisfies $\nabla\cdot\mathbf j=0$. Integrate the divergences of $x_i\mathbf j$ and $x_ix_k\mathbf j$ over a region enclosing its support; all boundary fluxes vanish. The first identity removes the monopole term from the magnetic vector potential expansion. The antisymmetric first moment is equivalent to the <magnetic dipole moment> $\frac12\int\mathbf x\times\mathbf j\,dV$.

= Magnetic dipole potential energy
{title2=$U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B$}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

In an external field that varies slowly across a localized magnetic dipole,
$$
U=-\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B,
\qquad
\mathbf F=\nabla(\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf B).
$$

The <magnetic dipole moment> determines the coupling to the external magnetic field.

= Bohr magneton
{title2=$\mu_B$}
{c}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}
{wiki}

The Bohr magneton,
$$
\mu_B=\frac{e\hbar}{2m_e},
$$
is the natural unit of an electron's orbital and spin magnetic moment.

= Coulomb-gauge vector potential of a localized steady current
{c}
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

If $\nabla\cdot J=0$ and the current decays enough for boundary terms to vanish, then
$$
A(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int\frac{J(x')}{|x-x'|}\,d^3x'
$$
satisfies $\nabla\cdot A=0$. Its far field is
$$
A(x)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\frac{m\times x}{|x|^3}+\cdots.
$$

= Magnetic dipole moment of a rigidly rotating charge distribution
{parent=Magnetic dipole moment}

A circular charge distribution rotating with angular speed $\omega$ can be decomposed into current loops. A hoop of line density $\eta$ and radius $R$ has $m=\pi\eta\omega R^3\hat n$; a disc of surface density $\sigma$ has $m=\pi\sigma\omega R^4\hat n/4$.

= Biot-Savart law
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

For a steady <current density> $J$, the <magnetic field> is
$$
B(r)=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi}\int
\frac{J(r')\times(r-r')}{|r-r'|^3}\,d^3r'.
$$

= On-axis magnetic field of a circular current loop
{parent=Biot-Savart law}

A circular loop of radius $R$ carrying <electric current> $I$ has, on its axis,
$$
B(z)=\frac{\mu_0IR^2}{2(R^2+z^2)^{3/2}}\,\hat z,
$$
where the sign of $\hat z$ is fixed by the <right-hand rule>. At its centre, $B(0)=\mu_0I/(2R)$.

= Magnetic-field cancellation between oppositely driven coaxial loops
{parent=Biot-Savart law}

If coaxial loops of radii $R$ and $2R$, separated by $D$, carry currents $I$ and $2I$ in opposite senses, their on-axis fields cancel between the loops at distance $D/3$ from the smaller loop.

= Beltrami field
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

A Beltrami field is a vector field parallel to its curl:
$$
\nabla\times J=\lambda J.
$$
If it is divergence-free and used as a magnetostatic current with constant nonzero $\lambda$, then $B=\mu_0J/\lambda$ solves $\nabla\times B=\mu_0J$ and $\nabla\cdot B=0$.

= Relativistic electromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

Relativistic electromagnetism combines electric and magnetic fields into Lorentz tensors.

= Relativistic charged-particle action
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}

For metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$, a particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ coupled to a four-potential has the reparametrization-invariant action
$$
S=-mc\int\sqrt{-\dot x^2}\,d\lambda
+q\int A_\mu(x)\dot x^\mu\,d\lambda.
$$

= Electromagnetic minimal coupling
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}

Electromagnetic minimal coupling replaces the free kinetic momentum in an action or Hamiltonian by a combination involving the electromagnetic potential. For $L=-m\sqrt{\dot x^a\dot x_a}-qA_a\dot x^a$, the canonical covector is $P_a=-m\dot x_a/\sqrt{\dot x^2}-qA_a$, so $P_a+qA_a$ recovers the potential-independent covector. Under $A_a\mapsto A_a+\partial_a\chi$, the action changes by an endpoint term. The fixed-endpoint equations are the <relativistic Lorentz force> equation and are gauge invariant.

= Gauge invariance of the charged-particle worldline action
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}

Under $A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\chi$, the interaction changes by
$$
q\int\frac{d\chi(x(\lambda))}{d\lambda}\,d\lambda
=q\,[\chi]_{\rm endpoints}.
$$
Fixed-endpoint equations of motion are therefore gauge invariant.

= Lorentz-force equation from the worldline action
{parent=Relativistic charged-particle action}
{c}

Varying the <relativistic charged-particle action> with fixed endpoints gives
$$
m\frac{du_\mu}{d\tau}=qF_{\mu\nu}u^\nu,
$$
or the equivalent raised-index equation.

= Electromagnetic four-potential
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}

= Four-potential
{synonym}

The four-potential combines the <electric potential> and <magnetic vector potential> as $A^\mu=(\phi/c,\mathbf A)$. The electromagnetic four-potential $A_\mu$ transforms under
$$
A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\chi,
$$
while the antisymmetric field tensor $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu$ is unchanged.

= Electromagnetic multipole expansion
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}

An electromagnetic multipole expansion expresses the potentials or fields of a localized source as successively smaller monopole, dipole, quadrupole, and higher moments in the ratio of source size to observation distance.

= Retarded potential
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

= Retarded electromagnetic potential
{synonym}

A retarded potential evaluates each source at the event on the source's past light cone. This accounts for propagation at the speed of light and enforces <causality>.

= Retarded time
{title2=$t_{\rm ret}$}
{parent=Retarded potential}
{wiki}

For a source point $y(s)$ and field event $(x,t)$, the retarded time solves
$$
t_{\rm ret}+\frac{|x-y(t_{\rm ret})|}{c}=t.
$$

= Uniqueness of retarded time for a subluminal source
{parent=Retarded time}

For $R(s)=|x-y(s)|$ and $|v|<c$, the function $F(s)=s+R(s)/c$ has
$$
F'(s)=1-\widehat R\cdot v/c>0.
$$
It is strictly increasing, so the retarded-time equation has at most one solution.

= Liénard–Wiechert potential
{c}
{parent=Retarded potential}
{wiki}

The potentials of a point charge $q$ are
$$
\phi=\frac{q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
\frac1{R-\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf R/c},
\qquad
\mathbf A=\frac{\mathbf v}{c^2}\phi,
$$
with every source quantity evaluated at the <retarded time>.

= Lorenz gauge condition
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

= Lorenz gauge
{c}
{synonym}

The Lorenz gauge condition is
$$
\frac1{c^2}\partial_t\phi+\nabla\cdot\mathbf A=0,
$$
or $\partial_\mu A^\mu=0$ in covariant notation.

= Residual Lorenz gauge symmetry
{parent=Lorenz gauge condition}
{title2=$\Box\lambda=0$}

An Abelian <gauge transformation> $A_a\mapsto A_a+\partial_a\lambda$ changes $\partial\cdot A$ by $\Box\lambda$. Consequently <gauge fixing> by a quadratic function of $\partial\cdot A$ retains transformations satisfying $\Box\lambda=0$, subject to the same <boundary conditions>. Eliminating those residual transformations is part of specifying an inverse with a particular <Green function> prescription.

= Electromagnetic field tensor
{title2=$F^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Electromagnetic four-potential}
{wiki}

The electromagnetic field tensor is
$$
F^{\mu\nu}=\partial^\mu A^\nu-\partial^\nu A^\mu.
$$
It packages the electric and magnetic fields into an antisymmetric rank-two Lorentz tensor and transforms as
$$
F'^{\mu\nu}(x')
=\Lambda^\mu{}_\rho\Lambda^\nu{}_\sigma F^{\rho\sigma}(x).
$$

= Dual electromagnetic field tensor
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{title2=$\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}$}

The dual electromagnetic field tensor is obtained by applying the <Hodge star operator> to the <electromagnetic field tensor>. For metric signature $(+---)$ and $\epsilon^{0123}=+1$, define $\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=\tfrac12\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}$. With $F^{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=-\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, it has components $\widetilde F^{0i}=-B_i$ and $\widetilde F^{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}E_k/c$. The homogeneous <Maxwell equations> become $\partial_\mu\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=0$.

= Maxwell Lagrangian
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{title2=$\mathcal L=-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}$}

The source-free electromagnetic <Lagrangian density> is quadratic in the <electromagnetic field tensor>. Its <Euler-Lagrange field equation> is $\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=0$. Antisymmetry of $F$ makes the density invariant under <gauge transformations>, and contraction of its indices makes it a <Lorentz scalar>.

= Maxwell action
{c}
{parent=Maxwell Lagrangian}
{title2=$S_E=\tfrac14\int F_{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}\,d^4x$}

The source-free <Maxwell Lagrangian> gives $S=-\tfrac14\int F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}\,d^4x$ in mostly-minus Lorentzian signature. Its <Euclidean action> is the displayed positive quadratic functional of the <electromagnetic field tensor>. Variation, with boundary terms vanishing, yields $\partial_\mu F_{\mu\nu}=0$.

= First-order Maxwell action
{parent=Maxwell action}
{title2=$S=\frac1{16\pi}\int\sqrt{-g}\,[F^{ab}F_{ab}-2F^{ab}(\nabla_aA_b-\nabla_bA_a)]$}

Vary the antisymmetric auxiliary <electromagnetic field tensor> and the <electromagnetic four-potential> independently. The first variation sets $F_{ab}=\nabla_aA_b-\nabla_bA_a$ algebraically, and variation of the potential gives $\nabla_bF^{ab}=0$. Substitution of the algebraic equation reduces the first-order action to the usual negative quadratic <Maxwell action>. Metric variation gives the same stress tensor after eliminating the auxiliary field, because the chain-rule term multiplying its variational equation then vanishes.

= Electromagnetic Bianchi identity
{c}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}

Because the <electromagnetic field tensor> is the <exterior derivative> $F=dA$ of a potential, $dF=d^2A=0$. For a torsion-free connection this is
$$
\nabla_aF_{bc}+\nabla_bF_{ca}+\nabla_cF_{ab}=0,
$$
which is the covariant form of the two homogeneous <Maxwell equations>.

= Lorentz force
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$}
{wiki}

= Lorentz force law
{c}
{synonym}

The <Lorentz force> is the <force> exerted by an <electric field> $\mathbf E$ and a <magnetic field> $\mathbf B$ on a body with <electric charge> $q$ and <velocity> $\mathbf v$. In <SI> units its form in nonrelativistic <classical mechanics>, using <mass> $m$ and <Newton's second law>, is
$$
\mathbf F=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),\qquad
m\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B).
$$
Both fields are evaluated at the body's <position> at the current <time>. The <electric field> contributes $q\mathbf E$ even at rest, whereas the magnetic <force> depends on <velocity> through a <cross product>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/5-4-electric-field[OpenStax, electric force and field]{ref}; https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-2-magnetic-fields-and-lines[OpenStax, magnetic force on moving charge]{ref}.

For a concrete <charged particle in a uniform magnetic field>, use <nondimensionalization> to stipulate $m=q=B=1$, <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(0,0,1)$, zero <electric field>, initial <position> $(0,0,0)$, and initial <velocity> $(1,0,0)$. These are rescaled quantities, not an assertion that the <mass>, <electric charge>, and <magnetic field> have the same physical units. At the start, $(1,0,0)\times(0,0,1)=(0,-1,0)$, so the magnetic <acceleration> points toward $-y$. The resulting <circular motion> is
$$
\mathbf r(t)=(\sin t,\cos t-1,0),\qquad
\mathbf v(t)=(\cos t,-\sin t,0),\qquad
\mathbf a(t)=(-\sin t,-\cos t,0).
$$
At $t=\pi/2$ the <position> is $(1,-1,0)$ and the <velocity> is $(0,-1,0)$; at $t=\pi$ the <position> is $(0,-2,0)$ and the <velocity> is $(-1,0,0)$. Thus the <circle> has center $(0,-1,0)$ and <radius> one. Viewed from $+z$ toward the $xy$ <plane>, the motion is clockwise. Changing only the <electric charge> to $q=-1$ gives <position> $(\sin t,1-\cos t,0)$ and reverses the bending to counterclockwise.

\Image[lorentz-force-orbits.png]
{title=<Lorentz force>: <circular motion> and a <helix> in the same uniform <magnetic field>}
{description=Stipulated rescaled $m=q=B=1$ and zero <electric field>. Left: positive <electric charge> follows the clockwise <circle> from initial <velocity> $(1,0,0)$; the <velocity> is tangent and the magnetic <force> points inward. Dots denote a <magnetic field> out of the <plane>. Right: adding parallel <velocity> $v_z=0.5$ gives <helical motion in a uniform magnetic field>, with <position> $z=0.5t$ and the same transverse <radius> one. Straight arrows show <magnetic field lines>, distinct from the curved path.}

Adding initial <velocity> $v_z=0.5$ leaves the transverse <circular motion> unchanged and gives
$$
\mathbf r(t)=(\sin t,\cos t-1,0.5t),\qquad
\mathbf v(t)=(\cos t,-\sin t,0.5).
$$
This is a <helix> of transverse <radius> one, <cyclotron frequency> $\omega_c=1$, and transverse <period> $T=2\pi$; the advance along $z$ in one turn is $\pi$. The parallel component of <velocity> stays constant because the magnetic <force> has no parallel component: there is no magnetic <acceleration> along the <magnetic field>. If the initial <velocity> were entirely parallel to the <magnetic field>, the magnetic <force> would vanish and the path would be straight. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/11-3-motion-of-a-charged-particle-in-a-magnetic-field[OpenStax, circular and helical motion]{ref}.

More generally, with zero <electric field>, no other <forces>, and a uniform constant <magnetic field> of magnitude $B>0$, decompose <velocity> into perpendicular and parallel components. For nonzero <electric charge> $q$, the perpendicular <speed> $v_\perp$ determines the <gyroradius>, while the <cyclotron frequency> is an <angular frequency>:
$$
r=\frac{mv_\perp}{|q|B},\qquad
\omega_c=\frac{|q|B}{m},\qquad
T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_c}=\frac{2\pi m}{|q|B}.
$$
These are nonrelativistic formulas; see the <relativistic cyclotron orbit> for the correction. The beauty is that increasing perpendicular <speed> increases the <radius> in exactly the proportion needed to leave the <period> unchanged. Conversely, known <magnetic field>, perpendicular <speed>, and measured <radius> determine the magnitude of the <electric charge> divided by <mass>. This makes the same geometry useful for comparing bodies with different <electric charge> and <mass>.

Two short checks explain the result. First, the <cross product> gives $\mathbf v\times\mathbf B=(-\sin t,-\cos t,0)$ for both stipulated positive-<electric charge> paths, exactly their <acceleration>. Second, the transverse magnetic <force> supplies the <centripetal acceleration>, so $mv_\perp^2/r=|q|v_\perp B$; for $v_\perp>0$ this gives the <gyroradius>, and $\omega_c=v_\perp/r$ gives the <cyclotron frequency>.

<Magnetic forces do no work>: the instantaneous <power> of the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> vanishes because a <cross product> is perpendicular to its first argument. The <work-energy theorem> therefore gives
$$
\frac{dK}{dt}=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v
=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v
+q\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)
=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v,
\qquad K=\tfrac12m|\mathbf v|^2.
$$
The <dot product> $\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ is zero at every instant, rather than only after a full turn. With zero <electric field> and no other <forces>, the <kinetic energy> and <speed> remain constant; the <electric field> can increase or decrease <kinetic energy> according to the sign of $q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$. In the two stipulated examples the constant <kinetic energy> is respectively $K=1/2$ and $K=5/8$ in rescaled units. The extra parallel <velocity> raises <kinetic energy> without changing the <gyroradius> or the <cyclotron frequency>.

For an electric example in rescaled units, stipulate $m=q=1$, zero <magnetic field>, constant <electric field> $\mathbf E=(2,0,0)$, no other <forces>, and rest at $t=0$. The <Lorentz force> gives <acceleration> $(2,0,0)$ and <velocity> $(2t,0,0)$, hence <kinetic energy> $K=2t^2$ and <power> $dK/dt=4t=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$. At $t=1$, the <kinetic energy> has risen from zero to two: the <electric field> has done positive <work>.

The existing <electromagnetic field tensor> formulation expresses the same interaction covariantly. For <four-momentum> $p^\mu$ and <four-velocity> $u^\mu$, using <proper time> $\tau$, the <Lorentz force law> is
$$
\frac{dp^\mu}{d\tau}=qF^{\mu\nu}u_\nu.
$$
Its spatial and temporal components are
$$
\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B),
\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$

= Hall effect
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{wiki}

The <Hall effect> is a transverse <electric potential difference> produced when an <electric current> flows through a material in a transverse <magnetic field>. The contact orientation fixes its measured sign. The <Drude conductivity and Hall experiment> gives an explicit convention and the limitations of inferring a <number density> from it.

= Hall coefficient
{c}
{parent=Hall effect}
{title2=$R_H=E_y/(J_xB_z)$}

The <Hall coefficient> relates a transverse <electric field> to longitudinal <current density> and transverse <magnetic field>, with oriented axes as in the displayed formula. Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{m^3/C}$. For one isotropic <Drude model> species it is $1/(nq)$; multiple <charge carriers> need not follow that formula.

= Gyroradius
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$r=mv_\perp/(|q|B)$}

= Larmor radius
{c}
{synonym}

The <gyroradius> is the <radius> of the transverse <circular motion> of a <charged particle in a uniform magnetic field>. In nonrelativistic <classical mechanics>, with zero <electric field> and no other <forces>, it is $mv_\perp/(|q|B)$, where $v_\perp$ is the <speed> perpendicular to the <magnetic field>. The parallel component of <velocity> changes the advance of the <helix>, rather than its transverse <radius>.

= Larmor theorem
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\boldsymbol\omega=-e\mathbf B/(2m)$}

For particles with the same charge-to-mass ratio, rotationally invariant mutual <central forces>, and a weak uniform <magnetic field>, a frame with the displayed <angular velocity> cancels the velocity-dependent magnetic <Lorentz force> against the <Coriolis force>. The remaining rotational and magnetic terms are quadratic in $\mathbf B$. Thus, to first order in the field, the rotating-frame equations coincide with the zero-field equations. This is the classical dynamical basis of <Larmor precession>; equality is an approximation rather than an exact removal of the magnetic field.

= Radial escape in an inverse-radius axial magnetic field
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\dot r^2=v_\perp^2-(\ell/r-c)^2,\quad c=qB_0/m$}

For an axial <magnetic field> $\mathbf B=(B_0/r)\mathbf e_z$, let $L=mr^2\dot\theta$. The <Lorentz force> gives $\dot L=-qB_0\dot r$, so $\ell=(L+qB_0r)/m$ is constant. Since <magnetic forces do no work> and $\dot z$ is constant, the transverse speed $v_\perp$ is constant and $\dot r^2=v_\perp^2-(\ell/r-c)^2$. If $c\ell>0$ and $0<v_\perp<|c|$, two finite radial <turning points> confine the motion. If $v_\perp>|c|$, an outward branch escapes to infinite radius. At equality a marginal escaping branch exists when $c\ell>0$, but $\ell=0$ gives a circular orbit instead. The transverse <escape velocity> threshold is $|qB_0|/m$; motion parallel to the <magnetic field> cannot provide transverse escape energy.

= Velocity rotation in a time-dependent axial magnetic field
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\dot x+i\dot y=V\exp\left(i\phi-\frac{iq}{m}\int_0^t B_z(s)\,ds\right)$}

Under the magnetic-only <Lorentz force>, planar velocity has constant magnitude and an accumulated phase determined by the time integral of the axial field. For $B_z(t)=\beta t$, the planar position involves <Fresnel integrals>. With initial planar velocity $(1,0)$ and zero initial position, its limit is $(\sqrt{\pi m/(4q\beta)},-\sqrt{\pi m/(4q\beta)})$. The model prescribes a magnetic-only force; a physical time-dependent field may also produce an induced electric field.

// Target: number-theory.bigb

= Magnetic forces do no work
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$(q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)\cdot\mathbf v=0$}

The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> is perpendicular to the particle <velocity>. Its power is therefore zero, so with no other forces the <kinetic energy> remains constant. This remains true relativistically because $dE/dt=\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf v$; a magnetic force can change momentum direction without changing total energy.

= Geometrization of the Lorentz force by an affine connection
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=-E^i,\quad\Gamma^i{}_{tj}=\epsilon^i{}_{jk}B^k$}

For smooth stationary fields on $\mathbb R^3$, set $\Gamma^i{}_{tt}=-E^i$ and $\Gamma^i{}_{tj}=\Gamma^i{}_{jt}=\epsilon^i{}_{jk}B^k$, with all other <Christoffel symbols> zero. This <torsion-free connection> makes the equation $\ddot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf E+2\mathbf B\times\dot{\mathbf x}$ a <geodesic equation> with time as <affine parameter>. Arbitrary smooth fields suffice; a metric realization imposes additional potential conditions.

= Electric drift in crossed uniform fields
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$\mathbf v_D=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/B^2$}

= Electric drift
{synonym}

In uniform perpendicular electric and <magnetic fields>, the guiding centre of a charged particle drifts with $\mathbf v_D=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/|\mathbf B|^2$. This <velocity> is independent of the charge and mass; cyclotron motion is superposed on it.

= Lorentz four-force
{parent=Lorentz force}
{title2=$f^\mu=qF^\mu{}_{\nu}u^\nu$}
{c}

The covariant force on a charged particle is its charge times the electromagnetic field-strength tensor contracted with its <four-velocity>. Antisymmetry keeps the four-force orthogonal to that velocity and preserves the rest mass.

= Magnetic force on a current-carrying wire
{parent=Lorentz force}

A wire carrying current $I$ in a magnetic field experiences
$$
d\mathbf F=I\,d\boldsymbol\ell\times\mathbf B.
$$
This follows by summing the magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> over the moving charge carriers.

= Relativistic cyclotron orbit
{title2=$\omega_\tau=qB/m$}
{parent=Lorentz force}

In a constant <magnetic field> $B\widehat z$ with no <electric field>, a charged relativistic particle moves in a circle in the perpendicular plane. Its angular frequency with respect to <proper time> is
$$
\omega_\tau=\frac{qB}{m},
$$
whereas its angular frequency in an inertial coordinate time is $\omega_t=qB/(\gamma m)$. The orbit radius at speed $v$ is
$$
R=\frac{\gamma mv}{|q|B}.
$$

= Charged-particle action over one relativistic cyclotron orbit
{parent=Relativistic cyclotron orbit}

For $qB>0$, choose $A_y=Bx$ and parametrize one clockwise orbit by $x=R\cos(\omega_\tau\tau)$ and $y=-R\sin(\omega_\tau\tau)$. Over $\Delta\tau=2\pi/\omega_\tau$, the <relativistic charged-particle action> is
$$
S=-mc^2\Delta\tau+q\oint A\mathbin\cdot dx
=-\frac{\pi mc^2}{\omega_\tau}(1+\gamma^2).
$$
The same formula with $|\omega_\tau|$ applies for either orbit orientation.

= Lorentz force density
{title2=$\mathbf f_{\rm L}$}
{parent=Lorentz force}

The Lorentz force per unit volume is $\mathbf f_{\rm L}=\rho_e\mathbf E+\mathbf j\times\mathbf B/c$ in Gaussian units.

= E-cross-B drift
{title2=$\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/B^2$}
{c}
{parent=Lorentz force}
{wiki}

In uniform perpendicular electric and magnetic fields, a charged particle's cyclotron orbit has guiding-centre velocity
$$
\mathbf v_D=\frac{\mathbf E\times\mathbf B}{B^2},
$$
independent of its charge and mass.

= Relativistic electromagnetic work-energy theorem
{parent=Lorentz force}

For $\mathcal E=\gamma mc^2$, the temporal component of the covariant Lorentz-force equation gives
$$
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v.
$$
The magnetic field does no work because $\mathbf v\cdot(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)=0$.

= Coordinate acceleration of a relativistic charged particle
{parent=Lorentz force}

Combining $d(\gamma m\mathbf v)/dt=q(\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)$ with $mc^2\,d\gamma/dt=q\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf v$ gives
$$
\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}
=\frac q{m\gamma}
\left[
\mathbf E+\mathbf v\times\mathbf B
-\frac{\mathbf v(\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf E)}{c^2}
\right].
$$

= Electromagnetic field invariants
{title2=$F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu},\,F_{\mu\nu}\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{wiki}

For signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and a consistent orientation,
$$
F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}
=2\left(B^2-\frac{E^2}{c^2}\right),
\qquad
F_{\mu\nu}\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}
=-\frac4c\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B.
$$
Overall signs depend on tensor and orientation conventions, while their vanishing does not.

= Null electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetic field invariants}
{wiki}

A null field has both electromagnetic invariants zero:
$$
\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf B=0,
\qquad
E^2=c^2B^2.
$$
For a nonzero constant null field, the mixed tensor is nilpotent of index three,
$$
F^\mu{}_\rho F^\rho{}_\sigma F^\sigma{}_\nu=0.
$$

= Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor
{parent=Electromagnetic field tensor}
{wiki=Electromagnetic_stress–energy_tensor}

For metric signature $(-,+,+,+)$,
$$
T^{\mu\nu}=\frac1{\mu_0}
\left(F^{\mu\rho}F^\nu{}_{\rho}
-\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F^{\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}\right).
$$
In particular, $T^{00}=\tfrac12(\epsilon_0E^2+B^2/\mu_0)$.

= Electromagnetic stress-energy divergence identity
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$\nabla_bT^{ab}=F^a{}_c\nabla_bF^{cb}/(4\pi)$}

For signature $(+---)$ in Gaussian electromagnetic units, $T^{ab}=(4\pi)^{-1}[-F^a{}_cF^{bc}+g^{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd}/4]$. The <electromagnetic Bianchi identity> gives $F^{bc}\nabla_bF^a{}_c=\nabla^a(F_{cd}F^{cd})/4$, so differentiating the stress tensor proves the displayed identity. Stress conservation implies the source-free <Maxwell equations> if the matrix $F^a{}_c$ is invertible; otherwise it only forces the field divergence into that matrix's kernel. Conversely source-free Maxwell equations always imply stress conservation.

= Gauge-compensated time translation in electromagnetism
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$\delta A_\nu=\alpha F_{0\nu}$}

With signature $(+,-,-,-)$ and $\mathcal L=-F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}/4$, combine a time translation $\alpha\partial_0A_\nu$ with the <gauge transformation> of parameter $-\alpha A_0$. Its <Noether current> is $J^\mu=-F^{\mu\nu}F_{0\nu}-\delta^\mu_0\mathcal L$, the gauge-invariant energy current. The density is $(|\mathbf E|^2+|\mathbf B|^2)/2$. On the source-free field equations it differs from the canonical translation current by $\partial_\nu(A_0F^{\mu\nu})$, an antisymmetric superpotential divergence, so total energies agree when the boundary term vanishes.

= Null energy condition for the electromagnetic field
{parent=Electromagnetic stress-energy tensor}

For every null vector $k$, put $q^\rho=k_\mu F^{\mu\rho}$. Antisymmetry gives $q\cdot k=0$, so $q$ has nonnegative norm and
$$
T^{\mu\nu}k_\mu k_\nu=\frac1{\mu_0}q^\rho q_\rho\geq0.
$$

= Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields
{parent=Relativistic electromagnetism}
{c}
{wiki}

Lorentz boosts mix transverse electric and magnetic field components.

= Relativistic charge density of counterstreaming beams
{parent=Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields}
{title2=$\lambda'=-\gamma vI/c^2$}

Two equal opposite line charge densities moving in opposite directions have zero net line charge but a nonzero total current $I$. Under a longitudinal <Lorentz boost>, the charge-current four-vector gives $\lambda'=\gamma(\lambda-vI/c^2)=-\gamma vI/c^2$. Equivalently, for lab densities $n$ and speeds $\pm u$, their separate boosted number densities are $\gamma n(1\mp vu/c^2)$. Their imbalance creates the boosted radial <electric field>, consistently with <Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields> and <Gauss's law>.

= Electromagnetic jump conditions across a normally moving plane
{parent=Lorentz transformation of electromagnetic fields}

If a plane is stationary in one frame and an observer moves normally to it with velocity $\mathbf v$, then
$$
[B'_n]=0,
\qquad
[\mathbf E'_t]=\mathbf v\mathbin\times[\mathbf B'_t].
$$
The second relation accounts for the magnetic-field discontinuity moving through the observer's frame.

= Electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetism}
{wiki}

An <electromagnetic wave> is a propagating disturbance of the <electric field> and <magnetic field> obeying the <Maxwell equations>. In source-free vacuum its speed is
$$
c=\frac1{\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0}}\simeq2.998\times10^8\,\mathrm{m/s},
$$
the <speed of light>. An <electromagnetic wave> can travel through vacuum: a conducting medium is unnecessary. A single traveling <plane electromagnetic wave> has mutually perpendicular <electric field>, <magnetic field>, and propagation direction. This statement applies to that wave; arbitrary <near and far field> configurations need not share these directions or the relation $|\mathbf B|=|\mathbf E|/c$.

The visualization compares perpendicular components on a common scale: $c\mathbf B$ has the same units as $\mathbf E$. The curves are field values along space, rather than paths traced by particles. Their common phase moves toward $+z$, while the <Poynting vector> records the associated <energy flux>.

\Image[electromagnetic-plane-wave.png]
{title=Vacuum plane wave with electric field along x, magnetic field along y, and energy flux along positive z}

The <electromagnetic wave equation> gives a propagation law, while the full <Maxwell equations> additionally enforce the field directions and relative amplitudes. This is why solving an independent scalar <wave equation> for each component is insufficient to construct an <electromagnetic wave>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_20.html[Feynman lectures, II.20: solutions in free space]{ref}.

= Electromagnetic waveguide
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}

An electromagnetic waveguide confines an <electromagnetic wave> transversely while allowing propagation along its axis. Conducting or dielectric boundaries select transverse modes. In a uniform guide a mode with transverse eigenvalue $k_\perp^2$ has longitudinal wavenumber $k_x^2=k^2-k_\perp^2$; below cutoff it is evanescent. At an open <aperture>, the confined field radiates into an angular spectrum rather than retaining a single guided mode.

= Scalar-potential conducting waveguide modes
{parent=Electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\gamma^2=\omega^2/c^2-k^2$}

For dependence $e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$, a <Helmholtz equation> $(\Delta_\perp+\gamma^2)\psi=0$ generates transverse-magnetic fields $e_\perp=\nabla_\perp\psi$, $e_z=-i\gamma^2\psi/k$, $b_\perp=\omega\,\widehat z\times\nabla_\perp\psi/(kc^2)$, $b_z=0$, for $k\ne0$. The conducting wall requires a <Dirichlet boundary condition> on $\psi$. A transverse-electric representation is $e_\perp=\widehat z\times\nabla_\perp\psi$, $e_z=0$, $b_\perp=-k\nabla_\perp\psi/\omega$, $b_z=i\gamma^2\psi/\omega$, with a <Neumann boundary condition>. These are families of modes, not a completeness assertion for multiply connected guides and their possible TEM fields.

= Electromagnetic aperture angular spectrum
{parent=Electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\mathbf q=(\kappa,\nu,\eta),\quad\kappa=\sqrt{k^2-\nu^2-\eta^2}$}

For an aperture normal to $\mathbf e_x$, let $F_y,F_z$ be its tangential <electric field> traces extended by zero outside the aperture. With the outgoing branch of $\kappa$, the <Weyl plane-wave representation> and the curl aperture integral give a spectrum proportional to $(-(\nu\widehat F_y+\eta\widehat F_z)/\kappa,\widehat F_y,\widehat F_z)$. Each wave is transverse to $\mathbf q$. The longitudinal electric component is regenerated by oblique diffraction even when it vanishes at the input. For $G=e^{ikr}/(4\pi r)$ and a unit prefactor on the stated curl integral, the spectrum has an overall factor $1/2$; a doubled half-space Green kernel gives factor one. The normalization must follow the chosen Green kernel and equivalent-current convention.

= Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}

A rectangular electromagnetic waveguide has a vacuum interior bounded by four <perfect conductor> walls parallel to its axis. The harmonic fields obey zero tangential <electric field> and zero normal <magnetic field> at the walls. <Maxwell equations> determine transverse fields from longitudinal components; a purely longitudinal oscillating <magnetic field> by itself cannot propagate, since its divergence would be nonzero.

= Waveguide cutoff frequency
{parent=Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\omega_{mn}=c\pi\sqrt{m^2/a^2+n^2/b^2}$}

For a <transverse electric mode of a rectangular waveguide>, the dispersion relation is $\omega^2=c^2(k^2+\kappa_{mn}^2)$, with $\kappa_{mn}^2=(m\pi/a)^2+(n\pi/b)^2$. Below $\omega_{mn}=c\kappa_{mn}$, the longitudinal wave number is imaginary and the mode is evanescent. If $a\ge b$, the lowest propagating cutoff is $\omega_{10}=\pi c/a$, or ordinary frequency $c/(2a)$.

= Waveguide phase and group velocities
{parent=Waveguide cutoff frequency}
{title2=$v_{\mathrm{ph}}v_g=c^2$}

Above a mode's <waveguide cutoff frequency> $\omega_c$, its <phase velocity> is $c/[1-(\omega_c/\omega)^2]^{1/2}$ and its <group velocity> is $c[1-(\omega_c/\omega)^2]^{1/2}$. Differentiate $\omega^2=c^2k^2+\omega_c^2$ to obtain the latter. Phase travels faster than $c$ while the wave packet travels slower, so the phase speed does not imply superluminal information transport.

= Transverse electric mode of a rectangular waveguide
{parent=Rectangular electromagnetic waveguide}
{title2=$\mathrm{TE}_{mn}$}

For a transverse electric mode, $E_z=0$ and $B_z=B_0(x,y)e^{i(kz-\omega t)}$. Put $\Delta=(\omega/c)^2-k^2$. <Maxwell equations> give $B_x=ik\partial_xB_z/\Delta$, $B_y=ik\partial_yB_z/\Delta$, $E_x=i\omega\partial_yB_z/\Delta$, and $E_y=-i\omega\partial_xB_z/\Delta$. The wall conditions become <Neumann boundary conditions> for $B_0$, whose separated solutions are $\cos(m\pi x/a)\cos(n\pi y/b)$. The constant $(0,0)$ candidate is excluded by Faraday's equation because its corresponding electric field vanishes.

= Transverse magnetic polarization
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf H=H_y\widehat{\mathbf y}$}

= TM wave
{c}
{synonym}

= TM polarization
{c}
{synonym}

Relative to a propagation plane, transverse magnetic polarization has its magnetic field perpendicular to that plane. At a perfectly conducting corrugation its scalar magnetic component obeys a Neumann condition. It reduces to a scalar two-dimensional problem, but not to the Dirichlet problem for the transverse electric component.

= Transverse electric polarization
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf E=E_y\widehat{\mathbf y}$}

= TE wave
{c}
{synonym}

= TE polarization
{c}
{synonym}

Relative to a propagation plane, transverse electric polarization has its electric field perpendicular to that plane. For a conducting corrugation independent of the perpendicular coordinate, the scalar electric component obeys the Helmholtz equation with Dirichlet data at the metal surface.

= TE wave equation in a stratified magnetodielectric
{c}
{parent=Transverse electric polarization}
{title2=$E_{y,xx}+E_{y,zz}-\frac{\mu'}\mu E_{y,z}+\omega^2\mu\varepsilon E_y=0$}

With permittivity and permeability depending only on $z$ and time dependence $e^{i\omega t}$, a <transverse electric polarization> $\mathbf E=\widehat{\mathbf y}E_y(x,z)$ obeys the displayed equation. <Maxwell's equations> give $H_x=E_{y,z}/(i\omega\mu)$ and $H_z=-E_{y,x}/(i\omega\mu)$. The latter satisfies $H_{z,xx}+H_{z,zz}+(\mu'/\mu)H_{z,z}+[\omega^2\mu\varepsilon+(\log\mu)'']H_z=0$. At normal incidence $H_z=0$; the nonzero magnetic component is $H_x$.

= Refractive index
{title2=$n$}
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

The refractive index is the ratio of the vacuum speed of light to a medium's phase speed. In a homogeneous isotropic medium, $n=c\sqrt{\varepsilon\mu}$ in compatible units.

= Inverse-square refractive-index profile
{parent=Refractive index}
{title2=$k^2(z)=k_0^2[a^2-b^2/(z-z_0)^2]$}

A <transverse electric polarization> with constant permeability and conserved transverse wavenumber $k_0\sin\theta_0$ satisfies $e''+[\beta^2-(\nu^2-1/4)/(z-z_0)^2]e=0$, with $\beta^2=k_0^2(a^2-\sin^2\theta_0)$ and $\nu^2=1/4+k_0^2b^2$. Substitution $e=\sqrt{|z-z_0|}Z_\nu(\beta|z-z_0|)$ reduces it to the <Bessel equation>. The profile is singular at $z_0$, so each side requires its own boundary conditions.

= Electromagnetic wave equation
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\nabla^2\mathbf E-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf E=0$}
{wiki}

In source-free vacuum the <electric field> and <magnetic field> obey
$$
\nabla^2\mathbf E-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf E=0,\qquad
\nabla^2\mathbf B-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\mathbf B=0.
$$
A brief derivation takes the <curl> of <Faraday's law> and substitutes the <Ampère-Maxwell equation>. The <vector calculus> identity $\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf E)=\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf E)-\nabla^2\mathbf E$, together with source-free <Gauss's law>, leaves $\nabla^2\mathbf E=\mu_0\epsilon_0\partial_t^2\mathbf E$. The same argument gives the <magnetic field> equation. The operator $\nabla^2$ is the <Laplacian>, applied componentwise.

= Plane electromagnetic wave
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{title2=$\mathbf B=\widehat{\mathbf k}\times\mathbf E/c$}
{wiki}

A monochromatic vacuum <plane electromagnetic wave> has phase $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t$, where $\mathbf k$ is the <wavevector>, $\omega>0$ the <angular frequency>, and $\omega=c|\mathbf k|$. Its field amplitudes obey $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf E_0=\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf B_0=0$ and $\mathbf B_0=\mathbf k\times\mathbf E_0/\omega$.

For the <linear polarization> and orientation in the visualization, choose a right-handed <Cartesian coordinate system> and put
$$
\mathbf E(z,t)=E_0\cos(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf x},\qquad
\mathbf B(z,t)=\frac{E_0}{c}\cos(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf y},\qquad
k>0,\quad\omega=ck.
$$
Constant phase means $z=ct+\text{constant}$, so propagation is along $+z$. The <cross product> $\widehat{\mathbf x}\times\widehat{\mathbf y}=\widehat{\mathbf z}$ fixes the positive <Poynting vector>. Both fields reverse together every half-period and are in phase. Reversing the propagation direction with the same electric orientation reverses the magnetic orientation.

These fields are spatially uniform across every transverse plane. An infinite <plane electromagnetic wave> is an idealization with infinite total <electromagnetic energy>; it is useful as a local approximation and as a building block for <superposition>. For <frequency> $f=100\,\mathrm{MHz}$, $\omega=2\pi f$ and <wavelength> $\lambda=c/f\simeq3.00\,\mathrm m$, independently of $E_0$.

= Polarization of an electromagnetic wave
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}

= Electromagnetic polarization
{synonym}

The polarization of an electromagnetic wave describes the path traced by its transverse electric-field vector at a fixed point. A monochromatic wave may be linearly, circularly, or elliptically polarized; in vacuum it has two independent transverse polarization states.

This specializes <polarization of waves> to electric-field oscillations.

= Faraday effect
{c}
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

= Faraday rotation
{c}
{synonym}

The Faraday effect rotates the orientation of linear <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> during propagation through a magnetized medium. The two opposite <circular polarizations> accumulate different phases; their recombination rotates the linear polarization. In a sufficiently high-frequency cold <plasma>, this angle varies as the squared <wavelength> times a <rotation measure>.

= Rotation measure
{parent=Faraday effect}
{title2=$\mathrm{RM}=a_0\int n_e B_\parallel\,dz$}

For the high-frequency <Faraday effect> in a cold <plasma>, the rotation measure is the coefficient in $\Delta\phi=\lambda^2\mathrm{RM}$. It is a density-weighted line integral of the line-of-sight <magnetic field>; the coefficient $a_0$ depends on the units. A sign convention for the line of sight fixes its sign. Correlations across an extended source can constrain a foreground <magnetic energy spectrum>, subject to statistical assumptions.

= Long-path projection of a magnetic correlation
{parent=Rotation measure}
{title2=$C_{\mathrm{RM}}(s)\simeq a_0^2n_e^2L\int H(k_\perp)e^{i\mathbf k_\perp\cdot\mathbf s}d^2k_\perp/(2\pi)^2$}

For constant <electron> density, common path length $L$, and a <solenoidal isotropic spectral tensor>, the rotation-measure <correlation> contains the window $|\int_0^Le^{ik_zz}dz|^2$. As $L$ becomes large compared with the <correlation length>, this window acts as $2\pi L\delta(k_z)$ within the <integral>. The transverse projector then has $zz$ component one, yielding the displayed two-dimensional <Fourier transform>. For finite $L$, the windowed integral retains longitudinal modes and endpoint corrections. The method of using such <rotation measure> correlations to infer magnetic spectra is developed by https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0302426[Enßlin and Vogt].

= Hankel inversion of a rotation-measure correlation
{c}
{parent=Long-path projection of a magnetic correlation}
{title2=$H(k)=\frac{2\pi}{a_0^2n_e^2L}\int_0^\infty rJ_0(kr)C_{\mathrm{RM}}(r)\,dr$}

Invert the two-dimensional <Fourier transform> in the <long-path projection of a magnetic correlation>, then perform the angular integration in <polar coordinates>. The angular integral is $2\pi J_0(kr)$, a <Bessel function of the first kind>, giving the displayed <Hankel transform> with the stated <Fourier-Hankel normalization for an isotropic spectrum>. The result recovers the scalar tensor coefficient $H$, whose shell-integrated <magnetic energy spectrum> has an additional factor $k^2$.

= Polarizer
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

= Polariser
{synonym}

A linear <polarizer> transmits one <linear polarization> and suppresses the orthogonal one. Two ideal crossed <polarizers> block light unless an intervening medium changes its <polarization of an electromagnetic wave>. A <twisted nematic field effect> uses this selection to convert field-driven reorientation into brightness modulation.

= Stokes parameters
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{title2=$(I,Q,U,V)$}

The Stokes parameters $I,Q,U,V$ describe intensity, two linear-polarization components and circular polarization. $Q$ and $U$ depend on the chosen transverse basis. On the celestial sphere, $Q\pm iU$ transform as spin-two fields rather than as ordinary scalars.

= Linear polarization
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}

A wave is linearly polarized when its oscillating <electric field> always lies along one fixed line. For a transverse <plane wave>, that line is perpendicular to the <wavevector>. Unlike <circular polarization>, the field oscillates along the line rather than rotating through directions.

= Circular polarization
{parent=Polarization of an electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

An electromagnetic wave has circular polarization when its transverse <electric field> has constant magnitude and rotates once during each period. In complex-amplitude notation, two orthogonal components have equal magnitude and a relative phase of one quarter-cycle.

= Perfect conductor
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

At the surface of a perfect conductor, the tangential <electric field> and normal <magnetic field> vanish. An incident <plane electromagnetic wave> is therefore accompanied by a reflected wave whose tangential electric field cancels the incident field at the surface.

= Scalar polarization reduction at a conducting corrugation
{parent=Perfect conductor}
{title2=$E_y|_\Gamma=0,\quad\partial_nH_y|_\Gamma=0$}

If a metal surface is $z=h(x)$ and the incident field is independent of $y$, Maxwell's equations separate into <TE polarization> and <TM polarization>. The metal condition that the tangential electric field vanish sets $E_y=0$ in the TE sector. In the TM sector $\mathbf E=i\nabla\times(H_y\widehat{\mathbf y})/(\omega\epsilon)$, so the same condition gives $(\partial_z-h'\partial_x)H_y=0$. This explains why the TE problem agrees with scalar Dirichlet acoustics while the TM problem has different rough-surface boundary forcing.

= Normal reflection of an electromagnetic wave from a perfect conductor
{parent=Perfect conductor}

At normal incidence, the reflected electric amplitude is the negative of the incident amplitude at the surface, while the reflected magnetic amplitude has the same sign. Their superposition forms a <standing wave> with an electric node and a magnetic antinode at the conductor.

= Interface conditions for electromagnetic fields
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

Across an interface with unit normal $\mathbf n$ from medium one to medium two, <Maxwell equations> imply $\mathbf n\times(\mathbf E_2-\mathbf E_1)=0$, $\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf B_2-\mathbf B_1)=0$, $\mathbf n\cdot(\mathbf D_2-\mathbf D_1)=\sigma_f$, and $\mathbf n\times(\mathbf H_2-\mathbf H_1)=\mathbf K_f$. Here $\sigma_f$ and $\mathbf K_f$ are the free surface <electric charge> and <electric current> densities.

= Dielectric-interface boundary conditions
{parent=Interface conditions for electromagnetic fields}

Without free surface charge or current, the tangential components of $E$ and $H$ and the normal components of $D$ and $B$ are continuous across an interface.

= Phase matching at a planar wave interface
{parent=Dielectric-interface boundary conditions}

Equality of the boundary fields for every tangential position and time forces equal frequency and equal tangential wavevector for incident, reflected, and transmitted plane waves.

= Snell law for electromagnetic waves
{parent=Phase matching at a planar wave interface}
{c}

For refractive indices $n_-$ and $n_+$,
$$
\theta_R=\theta_I,
\qquad
n_-\sin\theta_I=n_+\sin\theta_T.
$$

= Total internal reflection
{parent=Snell law for electromagnetic waves}
{wiki}

Total internal reflection occurs when a wave travels from higher to lower <refractive index> above the critical incidence angle, leaving no propagating transmitted plane wave normal to the interface.

= Critical angle for total internal reflection
{title2=$\theta_c$}
{parent=Total internal reflection}

For refractive indices $n_->n_+$, the critical angle satisfies $\sin\theta_c=n_+/n_-$. Above it, the transmitted normal wavenumber is imaginary and the transmitted field is an <evanescent wave>.

= Fresnel equations
{c}
{parent=Dielectric-interface boundary conditions}
{wiki}

The <Fresnel equations> give reflection and transmission amplitudes for both transverse-electric and transverse-magnetic <polarization of an electromagnetic wave> at a planar interface. They follow by applying <interface conditions for electromagnetic fields> to incident, reflected and refracted <plane electromagnetic waves>.

= Transverse-electric Fresnel reflection coefficient
{parent=Fresnel equations}

When the permeabilities agree, the signed reflected-to-incident electric-field ratio for polarization normal to the plane of incidence is
$$
r_{\rm TE}
=\frac{n_-\cos\theta_I-n_+\cos\theta_T}
{n_-\cos\theta_I+n_+\cos\theta_T}.
$$
For incidence from lower to higher refractive index it never vanishes.

= Reflected-to-transmitted transverse-electric amplitude ratio
{parent=Transverse-electric Fresnel reflection coefficient}

For equal permeabilities on the two sides of an interface, tangential-field continuity and <Snell law for electromagnetic waves> give
$$
\frac{E_R}{E_T}
=\frac12\left(1-\frac{\tan\theta_R}{\tan\theta_T}\right)
$$
for consistently signed electric amplitudes normal to the plane of incidence.

= Plane wave in an anisotropic dielectric
{parent=Plane electromagnetic wave}

For $D_i=\varepsilon_{ij}E_j$, $B=\mu H$, and a plane wave with electric polarization $e$, the source-free Maxwell equations imply
$$
k\times(k\times e)+\omega^2\mu\varepsilon e=0.
$$
If $e$ is an eigenvector of $\varepsilon$, then $e\perp k$, the dispersion relation is $|k|^2=\omega^2\mu\varepsilon_e$, and the <Poynting vector> is parallel to $k$.

= Poynting vector
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf S=\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/\mu_0$}
{wiki}

In vacuum the <Poynting vector>
$$
\mathbf S=\frac1{\mu_0}\mathbf E\times\mathbf B
$$
is the local <energy flux>, measured in watts per square metre. Its <surface integral> $P=\int_A\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf n\,dA$ gives signed <power> crossing a surface with unit <normal vector> $\mathbf n$. Positive flux follows the chosen normal; for a closed surface the usual convention is outward. <Power> depends on the normal component and area, rather than just the field magnitude.

The <Poynting vector> applies to steady fields as well as <electromagnetic waves>. In a driven resistive <electrical circuit>, surrounding fields can carry <energy> into the conductor, where electrical <work> becomes <Joule heating>. This turns a circuit-level power balance into a spatial description of energy delivery. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/res-6-001-electromagnetic-fields-and-energy-spring-2008/0f814aee1c72517df7d7e7e96b7365e1_11.pdf[Haus and Melcher, Electromagnetic Fields and Energy, chapter 11, §§11.0–11.3]{ref}.

= Poynting theorem
{parent=Poynting vector}
{c}
{title2=$\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot\mathbf S=-\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$}

= Poynting's theorem
{c}
{synonym}

The <Poynting theorem> is the local <energy> balance of classical <electromagnetism>. For vacuum field laws, with <current density> $\mathbf J$, the <electromagnetic energy> per unit volume is
$$
u=\frac{\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2}{2}+\frac{|\mathbf B|^2}{2\mu_0},
$$
and
$$
\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot\mathbf S=-\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E.
$$
Thus a decrease in local <electromagnetic energy> can supply outward <energy flux> or <work> on <electric charge>. The term $\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E$ is <power> transferred to matter per unit volume. Its sign matters: positive values remove field <energy>, while negative values describe matter supplying the field. The magnetic part of the <Lorentz force> supplies no work term.

For a fixed volume $V$, the <divergence theorem> gives the directly measurable balance
$$
\frac{d}{dt}\int_Vu\,dV
=-\int_{\partial V}\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf n\,dA
-\int_V\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf E\,dV.
$$
For example, $2.0\,\mathrm W$ entering a region with $0.50\,\mathrm W$ leaving and $1.20\,\mathrm W$ transferred to matter means stored <electromagnetic energy> increases at $0.30\,\mathrm W$. In source-free vacuum only the boundary flux changes the stored <energy>; a nonzero local <Poynting vector> can still have zero net closed-surface flux.

A brief proof takes the <dot product> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> with $\mathbf E/\mu_0$ and uses <Faraday's law> with $\mathbf B/\mu_0$. The identity $\nabla\cdot(\mathbf E\times\mathbf B)=\mathbf B\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf E)-\mathbf E\cdot(\nabla\times\mathbf B)$ turns the remaining terms into the <divergence> of the <Poynting vector>. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/II_27.html#Ch27-S3[Feynman lectures, II.27.3: local field-energy conservation]{ref}.

= Closed-surface Poynting flux of a plane wave
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For a real linearly polarized monochromatic vacuum <plane wave>, the local <Poynting vector> is proportional to $\cos^2(\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf k}$. Its flux through a closed surface generally oscillates at $2\omega$, with zero cycle average, although particular volume geometries can cancel it identically. For a circularly polarized plane wave of constant amplitude the <Poynting vector> is spatially and temporally constant, so the net flux through every closed surface is exactly zero. A nonzero constant local energy flux must not be confused with the net closed-surface flux.

= Electromagnetic energy
{title2=$U_{\rm EM}$}
{parent=Poynting theorem}

In vacuum the electromagnetic energy density is
$$
u=\frac{\varepsilon_0E^2}{2}+\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0},
$$
and the field energy in a region is $U_{\rm EM}=\int u\,dV$.

= Magnetic energy
{parent=Electromagnetic energy}
{title2=$E_B$}

In vacuum-permeability <magnetohydrodynamics>, the <magnetic energy> in a volume is $E_B=\int B^2/(2\mu_0)\,dV$. Its volume density equals the <magnetic pressure>.

= Magnetic energy spectrum
{parent=Magnetic energy}
{title2=$E_B(k)$}

The shell-integrated magnetic energy spectrum distributes <magnetic energy> over <wavenumbers>. With a <solenoidal isotropic spectral tensor> normalized to $\widehat C_{ii}=2H(k)$ and the inverse <Fourier transform> measure $d^3k/(2\pi)^3$, one has $\mathbb E|\mathbf B|^2=\pi^{-2}\int k^2H(k)dk$. In <Gaussian units>, $E_B(k)=k^2H(k)/(8\pi^3)$ integrates to the energy density $\mathbb E|\mathbf B|^2/(8\pi)$. A spectrum per unit mass or normalized to $B^2/2$ has different constant factors. Scale-local fluctuation estimates give $\delta B_l^2$ proportional to $kE_B(k)$ at $k\sim l^{-1}$ when the spectrum is normalized consistently.

= Energy density and flux of a plane electromagnetic wave
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For a single traveling vacuum <plane electromagnetic wave>, $\mathbf B=\widehat{\mathbf k}\times\mathbf E/c$ gives equal electric and magnetic contributions to <electromagnetic energy>:
$$
u_E=u_B=\frac12\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2,\qquad
u=\epsilon_0|\mathbf E|^2,\qquad
\mathbf S=cu\widehat{\mathbf k}.
$$
For the fields with <linear polarization> along $x$ and magnetic orientation along $y$ specified above,
$$
\mathbf S(z,t)=\epsilon_0cE_0^2\cos^2(kz-\omega t)\widehat{\mathbf z},\qquad
\langle\mathbf S\rangle=\frac{\epsilon_0cE_0^2}{2}\widehat{\mathbf z}.
$$
Angle brackets mean an average over one period at fixed position. The factor $1/2$ comes from averaging squared <cosine>; $E_0$ is the peak amplitude, whereas the <root mean square> field is $E_{\mathrm{rms}}=E_0/\sqrt2$. The equal energy contributions oscillate together, rather than trading energy as in an <RLC circuit>. https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-013-electromagnetics-and-applications-spring-2009/d3be4ea78b036a6362230fb41780cf54_MIT6_013S09_notes.pdf[MIT 6.013 course notes, §2.7.3: power and energy in uniform plane waves]{ref}.

For $E_0=100\,\mathrm{V/m}$, using $c=299792458\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $\epsilon_0\simeq8.854\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm{F/m}$,
$$
B_0=\frac{100}{c}\,\mathrm{V/m}\simeq3.34\times10^{-7}\,\mathrm T,
\qquad
\langle S_z\rangle\simeq13.3\,\mathrm{W/m^2},
\qquad
\langle u\rangle\simeq4.43\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{J/m^3}.
$$
A $0.010\,\mathrm{m^2}$ surface normal to $+z$ therefore has average crossing <power> $0.133\,\mathrm W$. This is incident <power>; the amount absorbed depends on the surface. Doubling the field amplitude quadruples the average flux.

= Poynting theorem in a linear anisotropic medium
{c}
{parent=Poynting theorem}

For symmetric time-independent constitutive tensors,
$$
u=\frac12(\varepsilon_{ij}E_iE_j+\mu_{ij}H_iH_j),
\qquad
S=E\times H,
$$
and the macroscopic Maxwell equations give $\partial_tu+\nabla\cdot S=-E\cdot J$.

= Near and far field
{parent=Electromagnetic wave}
{wiki}

The <near and far field> distinguish source-dependent electromagnetic field regions. Close to a localized source, reactive fields and induction terms contribute; sufficiently far away, the leading transverse fields decrease as the inverse distance and carry outgoing power. The <radiation zone> describes this far-field limit.

= Radiation zone
{parent=Near and far field}

In the radiation zone of a localized source, the leading electromagnetic fields decay as $1/R$, are transverse to the observation direction, and satisfy $B=\widehat R\times E/c$. Terms decaying faster than $1/R$ do not contribute to the limiting radiated power.

= Intensity of an isotropic radiator
{parent=Radiation zone}

An isotropic source of average power $P$ has intensity
$$
\langle S\rangle=\frac{P}{4\pi r^2}
$$
at distance $r$. For monochromatic vacuum radiation, its electric-field amplitude is $E_0=\sqrt{2\langle S\rangle/(\epsilon_0c)}$.

= Multipole radiation
{parent=Radiation zone}
{wiki}

<Multipole radiation> expands radiation from localized time-dependent sources into electric and magnetic multipoles. <Electric dipole radiation> is the leading electric term, while <magnetic dipole radiation> and higher electric multipoles contribute when the leading term vanishes or higher precision is needed.

= Electric dipole radiation
{parent=Multipole radiation}

For a localized source whose size is small compared with its radiation wavelength, the radiation fields of its electric dipole moment $p$ are
$$
E_{\rm rad}=\frac{\mu_0}{4\pi R}\widehat R\times(\widehat R\times\ddot p(t-R/c)),
\qquad
B_{\rm rad}=\frac1c\widehat R\times E_{\rm rad}.
$$
Their total instantaneous power is
$$
\mathcal P=\frac{\mu_0}{6\pi c}|\ddot p(t-R/c)|^2.
$$

= Electric-dipole approximation for radiation
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

= Dipole approximation for radiation
{synonym}

For a source of characteristic size $a$ and <angular frequency> $\omega$, the electric-dipole approximation requires
$$
\frac{\omega a}{c}\ll1,
$$
so the source is much smaller than its radiation wavelength. Observing the pure $1/R$ radiation field additionally requires the <radiation zone>, $R\gg c/\omega$ and $R\gg a$.

= Angular distribution of electric dipole radiation
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

If $\theta$ is the angle between the observation direction and $\ddot p$, then
$$
\frac{dP}{d\Omega}
=\frac{\mu_0}{16\pi^2c}|\ddot p|^2\sin^2\theta.
$$
Integrating over <solid angle> uses $\int\sin^2\theta\,d\Omega=8\pi/3$ and gives the total <electric dipole radiation> power.

= Radiation damping of a harmonically oscillating charge
{parent=Electric dipole radiation}

A charge $q$ undergoing <simple harmonic motion> of <angular frequency> $\omega$ and slowly varying amplitude $A$ radiates the averaged power
$$
\langle P\rangle=\frac{\mu_0q^2A^2\omega^4}{12\pi c}.
$$
Its mechanical energy $E=mA^2\omega^2/2$ therefore has <exponential decay> rate $\gamma=\mu_0q^2\omega^2/(6\pi mc)$ and <half-life>
$$
t_{1/2}=\frac{6\pi mc\log2}{\mu_0q^2\omega^2}.
$$

= Magnetic dipole radiation
{parent=Multipole radiation}

Electromagnetic duality replaces the electric dipole $p$ by $m/c$ for a magnetic dipole moment $m$. Therefore
$$
\mathcal P=\frac{\mu_0}{6\pi c^3}|\ddot m|^2.
$$

This is the magnetic-dipole term of the <multipole radiation> expansion.

= Magnetic-dipole spin-down
{parent=Magnetic dipole radiation}

If a rigid body's inclined magnetic dipole rotates at angular speed $\Omega$, its radiated power is $C\Omega^4$. Conservation of rotational energy $I\Omega^2/2$ then gives
$$
\dot\Omega=-\frac CI\Omega^3,
\qquad
\Omega(t)^{-2}=\Omega_0^{-2}+\frac{2C}{I}t.
$$
